In \cite{b19}, the authors demonstrate that Crowd Sensing Intelligence (CSI) 
leverages diverse sensors for data collection but faces a trade-off in mobile sensing: higher detection accuracy demands more sensors on vehicles, increasing the required number of sensing units. In \cite{zhang2020mobile}, they design an intelligent mobile charging control mechanism for electric vehicles (EVs), which can be integrated with mobile sensing.

% is a promising paradigm that utilizes the collective intelligence of heterogeneous sensing resources to gather data and information from Cyber-Physical-Social Systems. However, the mobile sensing technique employed in CSI may not ensure high detection accuracy. To achieve better accuracy, a higher detection frequency is required, which in turn necessitates equipping more vehicles with detection sensors. Thus, there is a trade-off between detection accuracy and the number of sensing units needed in mobile sensing systems.
\end{itemize}

