%\end{definition}

Another problem related to cohomologous symplectic forms on a fixed closed manifold, raised in \cite{Salamonsurvey}, concerns their first Chern classes. In dimension $4$, it is a consequence of Seiberg-Witten theory that cohomologous symplectic forms must have the same first Chern class (\cite[Corollary A]{Salamonsurvey}). In higher dimensions, one can ask the following question.

\begin{problem}[\cite{Salamonsurvey} Discussion 4.6]\label{prob:Salamon}
Do cohomologous symplectic forms on a closed manifold of dimension $\geq 6$ always have the same first Chern class?
\end{problem}


The aim of this note is to provide examples to both of the above problems in dimension $\geq 6$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
    Let $X$ be any symplectic $4$-manifold homeomorphic but not diffeomorphic to the rational manifold $\CC\PP^2\#k\overline{\CC\PP^2}$ with $k\geq 1$. Then there exists some symplectic form $\omega_X$ on $X$ and $\omega_{S^2}$ on $S^2$ such that the $6$-manifold $M:=X\times S^2$ admits symplectic form cohomologous to the product symplectic form $\omega_X\oplus \omega_{S^2}$ but with smaller Gromov width. Moreover, the first Chern classes of these cohomologous symplectic forms are different.
