\begin{abstract}
Model reduction with error bounds in nonlinear systems with non-affine control inputs remains an active field of research.
In this work we present a construction for Controllability and Observability Gramians in a class of non-affine control input systems satisfying certain induced norm properties. 
We do so using a combination of representational forms, including a novel function decomposition that resembles linear Singular Value Decomposition (SVD), in tandem with an additional unconventional decomposition of the dynamics, and Koopman operator theory.
The resulting representation allows one to place error bounds on the $H_{\infty}$ norm on a reduced-order representation of the system computed using finite-dimensional nonlinear Controllability and Observability Gramians.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Gramian, model reduction, SVD, Koopman,
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Observability and Controllability Gramians describe the input-output energy relationships in control systems and have been an essential tool for model reduction and modal analysis in linear systems since their introduction by Kalman in 1963 \cite{kalman1963gramains}.
They are used to define the Hankel singular values of a system, which in turn bound the approximation error when a balanced truncation is performed on the state space of a system \cite{dullerud2000chapter4}.
As control systems have become more complicated, increasing interest has been placed on identifying Gramian analogues for nonlinear systems, usually in bilinear systems or in systems with affine control inputs.
Beginning in 1993, Scherpen \cite{scherpen1993balancing} formulated observability and controllability functions which can be used to balance a nonlinear system when a certain Hamilton-Jacobi equation (partial differential equation, or PDE) is satisfied.
Later results identified infinite-dimensional nonlinear Hankel Operators and Gramians \cite{gray1998gramians}, and subsequent work identified algebraically defined finite-dimensional nonlinear Gramians for bilinear systems \cite{gray2006algebraic}.
These pioneering efforts demonstrated that a meaningful definition of Gramians exist for nonlinear systems, though practical computations for strong nonlinearities remained a challenge.

In the early 2000's, data-driven approaches began leveraging computational simulations of systems to estimate Gramians in place of explicitly solving PDEs. 
Contributions included seminal work from Lall and Marsden \cite{lall2002subspace}, who developed a method for Gramian estimation that reduces to the familiar Observability and Controllability Gramians in the linear case.
Subsequent variations improved efficiency and further validated the efficacy of the data-driven approach \cite{hahn2002improved, condon2004empirical}.

The introduction of error bounds for control-affine nonlinear model reduction using incremental balanced truncation marked a significant advancement \cite{besselink2014incremental}.
Meanwhile, a parallel track of work included Differential Gramians, defined locally around a trajectory in state space \cite{fujimoto2005differential}, with more recent applications combining data-driven advances with differential approaches \cite{kawano2021empirical}.
In addition, data-driven nonlinear extensions of Cross Gramians, which simultaneously capture observability and controllability behaviors, have been especially useful due to readily available code implementations \cite{himpe2014cross, himpe2018emgr}.

The advent of Koopmanism, launched by early work of Schmidt \cite{schmid2010dynamic} and Mezić \cite{mezic2013analysis} in the early 2010's, precipitated numerous results due to its ability to frame many nonlinear systems within the mathematically convenient context of linear systems theory by describing the dynamics of (potentially infinitely many) observables or dictionary functions of the original state space \cite{brunton2022review}. 
Computing observable functions is sometimes referred to as ``lifting" the state to a higher-dimensional space, since there are generally more dictionary functions than state dimensions.
The linear function that describes these potentially infinite-dimensional dynamics of the observables is called a ``Koopman generator," while its discrete-time analogue is the Koopman operator.
As a square, linear function acting on members of a function space, both the generator and operator have \emph{eigenfunctions}.
For the continuous-time case, this means any function, $\varphi_i$, that satisfies \mbox{$\varphi_i(x)L = \lambda_i \varphi_i(x)$}, for some Koopman generator, $L$.
The function space from which to select $\varphi_i$ is arbitrary.
While exact finite-dimensional representations are not generally possible, dictionary functions can be chosen from eigenfunctions that are associated with the most significant eigenvalues of the Koopman generator such that a finite-dimensional representation can be found that captures the most significant modes of the lifted dynamics.
This process is usually referred to as identifying a ``Koopman-invariant subspace" \cite{brunton2022review}.
Popular approaches for learning eigenfunctions of the Koopman operator include dynamic mode decomposition (DMD) \cite{schmid2010dynamic, proctor2016dynamic, mezic2013analysis}, extended dynamic mode decomposition (eDMD) \cite{williams2015data}, and deep learning approaches \cite{lusch2018deep, yeung2019learning}.

The finite-dimensional linearity afforded by a Koopman-invariant subspace is frequently exploited to derive control laws using canonical results from control theory \cite{brunton2017chaos, korda2018mpc, kaiser2021data}, but other work, foundational to the results presented here, extends the idea of Gramians to Koopman representations \cite{lieu2018koopman} and uses these to perform balanced truncations on finite-dimensional models \cite{yeung2018koopman}. 

It is worth briefly noting several other nonlinear model reduction techniques that are commonly employed.
These include singular perturbation theory \cite{khalil2002singular}, which is useful for separating dynamics that occur at vastly different timescales, and the manifold boundary approximation method (MBAM), which leverages information geometry to collapse parameters that are least identifiable given some data \cite{transtrum2014model}.
MBAM has been applied to a variety of systems \cite{transtrum2016bridging, transtrum2017measurement}, and has been shown to be a generalization of both singular perturbations and balanced truncations \cite{pare2019unifying}.
In contrast to techniques that remove states after a model has been identified, some data-driven approaches aim to identify parsimonious models during the learning process, for example SINDy \cite{brunton2016discovering}, which leverages an $L_1$ penalty to promote coefficient sparsity, and which has been used to identify important eigenfunctions of the Koopman operator \cite{kaiser2021data}, and variational autoencoders \cite{lusch2018deep}, which can be used to identify low-dimensional representations of the dynamics of a system.

Critical for the results presented here, most of results described above (particularly those leveraging linearized representations) apply to systems of the form:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\dot{x} &= f(x) + g(x)u \\
y &= h(x),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
and, except in the case of incremental balanced truncation \cite{besselink2014incremental}, error bounds are rarely available.
A major goal of the work presented here will be the development of Controllability and Observability Gramians, and associated bounds on model reduction, for systems of the form:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:system_type}
\begin{aligned}
\dot{x} &= f(x, u) \\
y &= h(x)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
provided $f$ belongs to a particular class of nonlinear functions with certain induced norm properties.
We will show this by leveraging a recently developed technique that decomposes a function, $f$, satisfying these induced norm properties, into a form resembling the Singular Value Decomposition (SVD), in that it comprises the composition of a unitary matrix, $U$, a positive, rectangular diagonal matrix, $\Sigma$, and a norm-preserving and injective (relaxations of the unitary property) ``lifting" $v(x)$ \cite{brown2024svd}:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:svd_like}
f(x) = U \Sigma v(x).
\end{align}
\emph{The primary contribution of this paper will be to leverage 1) this novel SVD-like decomposition in tandem with 2) an additional novel decomposition of the dynamics and 3) a finite-dimensional approximation of the Koopman invariant subspace of the system transient dynamics, to derive a pseudo-affine, lifted representation of systems of the form \eqref{eq:system_type}, thereby admitting Controllability and Observability Gramians that relate directly to the error introduced by model reduction via balanced truncation.
}

\section{Background and Notation}

We will consider vector norms, norms of infinite-dimensions, function norms, induced norms of finite-dimensional functions, and induced operator norms.
These are distinguished in the following table:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabularx}{\columnwidth}{|c|c|X|}
\hline
\label{tabs:norms}
\textbf{Notation} & \textbf{Type of Object} & \textbf{Definition} \\ \hline
$\|f(x)\|_2$ & $f(x) \in \mathbb{R}^m$ & $\left( \sum_{i=1}^m |f_i(x)|^2 \right)^{1/2}$ \\ \hline
$\|u\|_{L_2}$ & $u(t) \in L_2$ & $\left( \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \|u(t)\|_2^2 \,dt \right)^{1/2}$ \\ \hline
$\|f\|_{2\to2}$ & Mapping $f: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^m$ & $\sup_x \frac{\|f(x)\|_2}{\|x\|_2}$ \\ \hline
$\|G\|_{H_{\infty}}$ & Operator $G: L_2 \to L_2$ & $\sup_u \frac{\|G(u)\|_{L_2}}{\|u\|_{L_2}}$ \\ \hline
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

We will frequently reference functions with ``finite 2-induced norm", i.e. functions, $f$, satisfying $\|f\|_{2 \to 2} < \infty$, or more concretely:
\begin{align}
\sup_{x\neq0} \frac{\|f(x)\|_2}{\|x\|_2} < \infty.
\end{align}
We denote the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse of a matrix, $A$ as $A^{\dagger}$.
We denote an identity matrix of dimension $n \times n$ as $I_n$, and we denote a zeros matrix of dimension $m \times n$ as $0_{m \times n}$.

\section{Results}
In this section we present both preliminary results that develop a generalized singular value decomposition, building on the work from \cite{brown2024svd}, and our main result, Controllability and Observability Gramians for a particular class of nonlinear control systems.
\subsection{Preliminary Results: Generalized SVD}
This section will comprise numerous ancillary results centered on the theme of novel function decompositions that preserve many of the properties of linear SVD, but in the context of nonlinear functions with certain boundedness properties on their induced norms.
Our first preliminary result is to provide a simpler (and thus more easily analyzed) construction for the result presented \cite{brown2024svd}.
\emph{The primary purpose of these results will be to decompose nonlinearities into a left-invertible, norm-preserving component that is distinct from a non-invertible stretching component}.

\begin{lemma}[Simple construction of a norm--preserving lifting]\label{lemma:simpler_svd}
Let $f:\mathbb R^{n}\to\mathbb R^{p}$ satisfy
\begin{align}
  \|f\|_{2\to2} &\triangleq \sup_{x\in\mathbb R^{n}\setminus\{0\}}
    \frac{\|f(x)\|_{2}}{\|x\|_{2}} < \infty.
\end{align}
Set $m\triangleq n+p$.  Then there exist
\begin{enumerate}
\item a real unitary matrix $U\in\mathbb R^{p\times p}$,
\item a non--negative diagonal matrix $\Sigma\in\mathbb R^{p\times m}$, and
\item an injective map $v:\mathbb R^{n}\to\mathbb R^{m}$ with \mbox{$\|v(x)\|_{2}=\|x\|_{2}, \quad \forall x \in \mathbb{R}^n$}
\end{enumerate}
such that
\begin{align}
  f(x)=U\Sigma v(x)\qquad \forall\,x\in\mathbb R^{n}.
\end{align}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Define $\Sigma \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times m}$ to be a rectangular diagonal matrix whose diagonal elements are positive and will be specified later, with the $n$ right-most columns being zero.
Similarly, let $U \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times p}$ be a unitary matrix that will be specified later.
Let $v_{\mathrm{support}}: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^{p}$ be defined as follows:
\begin{align}
  v_{\mathrm{support}}(x) \triangleq \Sigma^{\dagger} U^T f(x).
\end{align}
Define $v_{\mathrm{kernel}}: \mathbb{R}^{n} \to \mathbb{R}^n$ as:
\begin{align}
  v_{\mathrm{kernel}}(x) \triangleq
  \begin{bmatrix}
    0_{p\times n} \\
    I_{n \times n}
  \end{bmatrix}
  x \sqrt{\frac{\|x\|_2^2 - \|v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)\|_2^2}{\|x\|_2^2}}.
\end{align}
Further define $v: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^{n+p}$ as:
\begin{align}
  v(x) \triangleq v_{\mathrm{support}}(x) +  v_{\mathrm{kernel}}(x).
\end{align}
$v$ is injective since the lower $n$ terms of $v(x)$ are simply a scaled version of $x$ itself, and, by construction, point-wise norm-preserving for every $x \neq 0$ since:
\begin{align*}
  \|v(x)\|_2^2 &= \|v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)\|_2^2 + \|x\|_2^2 \frac{\|x\|_2^2 - \|v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)\|_2^2}{\|x\|_2^2} \\
               &= \|v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)\|_2^2 + \|x\|_2^2 - \|v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)\|_2^2 \\
               &= \|x\|_2^2.
\end{align*}
For $v_{\mathrm{kernel}}(x)$ to be real--valued we require
\begin{align}
  \|x\|_2^2 - \|v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)\|_2^2 > 0 \quad \forall x \in \mathbb{R}^n.
\end{align}
This holds if each $\sigma_i$ satisfies
\begin{align}
  \sum_{i=1}^p \frac{f_i(x)^2}{\|x\|_2^2 \sigma_i^2} < 1, \quad \forall x \in \mathbb{R}^n,
\end{align}
which is guaranteed whenever
\begin{align}
  \sum_{i=1}^p \frac{\| f_i \|_{2\to2}^2}{\sigma_i^2} < 1.
\end{align}
Finally, let $U$ be the permutation matrix that orders the diagonal elements of $\Sigma$ in decreasing order.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Lemma~\ref{lemma:simpler_svd} is identical to the result presented in \cite{brown2024svd}, though it comprises a considerably simpler construction.
See that result for illustrative examples and figures.
\end{remark}

% --- Notation: barred quantities that vary with $x$ -------------------
% Note: $\bar U$ is constant, whereas $\bar\Sigma(x)$ and $\bar V(x)$ depend on the current state~$x$.

\begin{definition}
\label{def:linear_plus}
Define $\mathrm{Linear}^+$ to be the set of functions $f: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^p$ for which there exist
\begin{enumerate}
  \item a constant unitary matrix $U \in \mathbb{R}^{p\times p}$,
  \item an $x$--dependent, diagonal, non--negative matrix $\bar{\Sigma}(x) \in \mathbb{R}^{p\times n}$ whose diagonal elements satisfy $\bar{\sigma}_i(x) \ge \bar{\sigma}_j(x)$ for all $x$ and $i<j$, and
  \item an $x$--dependent unitary matrix $\bar {V}(x) \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$,
\end{enumerate}
such that
\begin{align}
  f(x) = U\,\bar{\Sigma}(x)\,\bar{ V}(x)\,x.
\end{align}
Moreover, for each diagonal element $\bar{\sigma}_i$ we require
\begin{align}
  \sup_{x\in\mathbb R^{n}} \bar{\sigma}_i(x) < \infty.
\end{align}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
One family of functions that satisfies Definition~\ref{def:linear_plus} comprises those with finite 2--induced norm whose Jacobian admits an SVD with a constant left singular matrix~$U$ for all inputs.
\end{remark}



\begin{corollary}[Generalized SVD]\label{corollary:generalized_svd}
Let $(f: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^p) \in \mathrm{Linear}^{+}$ and set $m\triangleq n+p$. Then there exist
\begin{enumerate}
  \item a unitary matrix $U \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times p}$,
  \item a non--negative diagonal matrix $\Sigma \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times m}$, and
  \item an injective mapping $v: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^m$ with \mbox{$\|v(x)\|_2 = \|x\|_2\;\forall x\in\mathbb R^{n}$},
\end{enumerate}
such that
\begin{align}
  f(x)=U\Sigma v(x),\qquad \forall x\in\mathbb R^{n}.
\end{align}
Furthermore, $\sigma_1$ arbitrarily tightly bounds the 2--induced norm of $f$.  If we set $F\triangleq U^{T}f$, each $\sigma_i$ arbitrarily tightly bounds the 2--induced norm of the $i$--th coordinate functional of $F$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Because $f$ is a composition of bounded factors, $\|f\|_{2\to2}<\infty$.  Define the rotated map
\begin{align}
  F(x)\;\triangleq\; U^{\!T}f(x)=\bar{\Sigma}(x)\,\bar{V}(x)\,x.
\end{align}
Lemma~\ref{lemma:simpler_svd} applied to $F$ yields a diagonal matrix $\Sigma\in\mathbb R^{p\times m}$ and a map $v:\mathbb R^{n}\to\mathbb R^{m}$ with $\|v(x)\|_2=\|x\|_2$ such that
\begin{align}
  F(x)=\Sigma v(x),
\end{align}
(the permutation matrix in Lemma~\ref{lemma:simpler_svd} is the identity in this case because $\bar{\Sigma}(x)$ is already sorted).
Write $v=v_{\mathrm{support}}+v_{\mathrm{kernel}}$ with
\begin{align}
  v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)&\triangleq\Sigma^{\dagger}F(x), \\
  v_{\mathrm{kernel}}(x)&\triangleq
      \begin{bmatrix}0_{p\times n}\\[-0.3em]I_{n}\end{bmatrix}
      x\sqrt{\frac{\|x\|_2^{2}-\|v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)\|_2^{2}}{\|x\|_2^{2}}} . \label{eq:kernel_def_new}
\end{align}

Set
\begin{align}
  \Sigma_{ii}\;\triangleq\;\sup_{x\in\mathbb R^{n}} \bar{\sigma}_i(x),\qquad i=1,\dots,p .
\end{align}
The diagonal is non--negative and ordered, so $(\Sigma^{\dagger}\bar{\Sigma}(x))_{ii}\le 1$ for all $x$.
Using this bound and that $\bar{V}(x)$ is unitary,
\begin{align}
  \|v_{\mathrm{support}}(x)\|_2
  =\|\Sigma^{\dagger}\bar{\Sigma}(x)\bar{V}(x)x\|_2
  \le \|x\|_2, \quad \forall x .
\end{align}
Hence the radicand in \eqref{eq:kernel_def_new} is non--negative and $v_{\mathrm{kernel}}(x)$ is real--valued.

Combining \mbox{$f= U F$} with $F=\Sigma v$ gives
\begin{align}
  f(x) = U\Sigma v(x).
\end{align}

For any non‑zero $x$, 
\begin{align}
\frac{\|f(x)\|_{2}}{\|x\|_{2}} = \frac{\|\Sigma v(x)\|_{2}}{\|v(x)\|_{2}}\le\sigma_1 
\end{align}
Given $\varepsilon>0$, pick $x$ such that $\bar{\sigma}_1(x)\ge\Sigma_{11}-\varepsilon$ (possible by definition of supremum).  Repeating this yields the desired lower bound, showing the estimate is arbitrarily tight.
Applying the same argument to $F=U^{T}f$ yields the coordinate-wise bounds.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Regardless of whether $f$ is in $\mathrm{Linear}^+$ or simply has a finite 2-induced norm, the value in the SVD-like representation presented here is its ability to isolate any gain and non-injectivity into a single, static matrix ($\Sigma$), which remains separate from any nonlinearity ($v(x)$).
Furthermore, this nonlinearity is expressed as an injective, point-wise norm-preserving function.
%This combination of properties will be essential for bounding the control input's contribution to nonlinear dynamics.
\end{remark}
