\subsection{Main Results Part 1: Nonlinear Gramians under Strong Assumptions}

In this subsection we will leverage the preceding results, as well as Koopman operator theory, to develop Gramians for a novel class of control systems which will allow us to place bounds on the $H_{\infty}$ norm of error introduced via model reduction.
We will first consider results under unrealistically idealized assumptions about Koopman representations, namely, perfect representation in finite dimensions, while the following subsection will relax this assumption.
We begin with a definition of the idealized class of systems that we will consider in this subsection.
\begin{definition}[Nonlinear Control Systems with Particular Induced Norm Properties and Finite-Dimensional Koopman Representation]
\label{def:def_of_j}
Let $\mathcal{J}$ be the set of all systems, $G$, with state space representation
\begin{align}
\dot{x} &= f(x, u)\\
y &= h(x),
\end{align}
where the autonomous dynamics,
\begin{align}
\dot{x} = f(x,0) \\
y = h(x),
\end{align}
and associated Koopman generator for the autonomous dynamics, $A$, with eigenfunctions $\varphi_0$,
\begin{align}
\dot{\varphi}_0(x) = A \varphi_0(x),
\end{align}
have the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $f(x,0)$ must be hyperbolic around a single invariant compact set,
    \item $f$ is Lipschitz continuous,
    \item $f(0, 0) = 0$,
    \item the eigenfunctions of the autonomous transient dynamics, $\varphi_0: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^q$ are finite dimensional ($q < \infty$),
    \item $\varphi_0$ is state-inclusive,
    \item $\varphi_0$ is Lipschitz continuous,
    \item $\varphi_0(0)=0$,
    \item the Jacobian of $\varphi_0(x)$, denoted by $D_{\varphi_0}(x) \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times q}$, is Lipschitz continuous,
    \item $D_{\varphi_0}(0)$ = 0,
    \item $h$ lies in the span of $\varphi_0$.
\end{itemize}
As a convention, we refer to the dimensions of the various components of such systems as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x \in \mathbb{R}^n$,
\item $u \in \mathbb{R}^l$,
\item $\varphi_0(x) \in \mathbb{R}^q$,
\item $y \in \mathbb{R}^p$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}
%We now present a result which is critical for the development of Gramians for the class $\mathcal{J}$ and a later expansion of that class.
%This theorem will derive a representational form of systems of the form $\mathcal{J}$ which will allow us to identify error bounds under balanced truncation.
\begin{theorem}[Point-wise Norm-preserving, Affine-like Control Inputs in Non-affine Systems]
\label{theorem:norm_preserving}
For every system, $G \in \mathcal{J}$, there exists a function, $v:\mathbb{R}^n\times\mathbb{R}^l \to \mathbb{R}^{l+q}$, satisfying
\begin{align}
\|v(x, u)\|_2 = \|u\|_2, \quad \forall x \in \mathbb{R}^n, u \in \mathbb{R}^l
\end{align}
such that $G$ has a finite-dimensional state-space representation,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:linear_system_rep}
\dot{\varphi}_0(x, u) &= A \varphi_0(x) + B v(x, u) \\
y &= C \varphi_0(x),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
with $A \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times q}$ Hurwitz, $B \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times (l+q)}$, and $C \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times q}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We begin with the following decomposition of $f$ into autonomous dynamics and dynamics induced by the control:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f_0(x) &\triangleq f(x, 0) \\
\label{eq:def_for_f_u}
f_u(x, u) &\triangleq f(x, u) - f_0(x),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
such that
\begin{align}
f(x, u) = f_0(x) + f_u(x, u).
\end{align}

Recall that the transient of the autonomous dynamics, $f_0$, admitted a Koopman generator with finitely many Lipschitz--continuous basis functions.
We may thus consider \mbox{$\varphi_0: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^q$}, for some finite $q$, and $A \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times q}$, as well as a matrix $C \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times q}$ such that:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\dot{\varphi}_0(x) &= A \varphi_0(x) \\
y &= C \varphi_0(x).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Now let $\varphi(x,u) \triangleq \varphi_0(x)$, i.e. $\varphi(x,u)$ is a lifting function of both $x$ and $u$ that is invariant under $u$.

Recall we denote the Jacobian of $\varphi_0$ as $D_{\varphi_0}$.
From the chain rule, we have that the dynamics are given by the Lie derivative of $\varphi_0$ along $f_0$:
\begin{align}
\dot{\varphi}_0(x) = D_{\varphi_0}(x)f_0(x).
\end{align}
When we consider the Lie derivative of $\varphi$ along the controlled dynamics, $f$, we have:
\begin{align}
\dot{\varphi}(x, u) = D_{\varphi_0}(x) f(x, u).
\end{align}
By the multivariate chain rule, we would need to include the partial of $\varphi$ with respect to $u$, but since $\varphi$ is invariant with respect to $u$, this term is always zero.

Furthermore, from \cite{mauroy2016stability}, we know that since $f$ is continuous and hyperbolic around an invariant compact set, the restricted Koopman generator, $A$, must be Hurwitz.
From here, we can consider the contribution of the control inputs in the lifted representation of the system, defined similar to \eqref{eq:def_for_f_u}, such that:
\begin{align}
\dot{\varphi}_u(x, u) &\triangleq \dot{\varphi}(x, u) - \dot{\varphi}_0(x) \\
&= D_{\varphi_0}(x) f(x, u) - D_{\varphi_0}(x) f(x, 0).
\end{align}
Define $\bar{f}_0: \mathbb{R}^q \to \mathbb{R}^q$, $\bar{f}_u: \mathbb{R}^q \times \mathbb{R}^l \to \mathbb{R}^q$, and $\bar{f}: \mathbb{R}^q \times \mathbb{R}^l \to \mathbb{R}^q$, such that:
\begin{align}
\bar{f}_0(x) &\triangleq \varphi_0(x) \\
\bar{f}_u(x,u) &\triangleq \varphi_u(x, u) \\
\bar{f}(x,u) &\triangleq \bar{f}_0(x) + \bar{f}_u(x, u)
\end{align}
We currently assume that $\bar{f}_0$ is perfectly represented by a $q$-dimensional Koopman representation that is linear in $\varphi_0(x)$, i.e.:
\begin{align}
\bar{f}_0(x) = A \varphi_0(x).
\end{align}
We note parenthetically that \emph{this is precisely the assumption that will be relaxed in the sequel}.
From Lemma \ref{lemma:one_argument}, we can identify an SVD-like decomposition of $\bar{f}_u(x, u)$ provided $\bar{f}_u$ satisfies:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:constraint_fu}
\sup_{(x,u)\neq0} \frac{\|\bar{f}_u(x,u)\|_2}{\|u\|_2} < \infty, \quad \forall x \in \mathbb{R}^n, u \in \mathbb{R}^m.
\end{align}
To prove that $\bar{f}_u$ satisfies these properties, we will show that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\lim_{u \to 0} \bar{f}_u(x, u) = 0, \quad \forall x \in \mathbb{R}$
\item $\bar{f}_u$ is Lipschitz in $u$.
\end{itemize}
More specifically, we will show that $\bar{f}_0$ and $\bar{f}$  satisfy both of these properties individually by the definition of $\mathcal{J}$, and therefore $\bar{f}_u$ must satisfy these conditions as well.

First, it is easy to show that $\bar{f}_0(x)$ satisfies both conditions since $\varphi_0$ satisfies both conditions by axiom and $A$ is a finite-dimensional real-valued matrix and therefore must satisfy these properties as well.
Second, recall that $\bar{f}(x,u) = \dot{\varphi}(x, u) = D_{\varphi_0}(x) f(x, u)$, i.e. $\dot{\varphi}(x,u)$ is the product of two functions that are Lipschitz continuous and that satisfy $D_{\varphi_0}(0) = 0$ and $f(0, 0) = 0$ by axiom.
Given these conditions, we have that $\bar{f}_u$ satisfies \eqref{eq:constraint_fu}.

From Lemma \ref{lemma:one_argument}, we then have that $\bar{f}_u$ has a representation of the form:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:def_of_U_and_Sigma}
\bar{f}_u = U \Sigma v(x, u),
\end{align}
where $v$ satisfies $\|v(x,u)\|_2 = \|u\|_2$.
For consistency with traditional representations of LTI systems, let \mbox{$B \triangleq U \Sigma$}, and we arrive at the desired statement:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\dot{\varphi}_0(x) &= A \varphi_0(x) + B v(x, u) \\
y &= C \varphi_0(x),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
This theorem simultaneously establishes two facts.
First, it introduces an ``affine-like" decomposition of systems with potentially non-affine control inputs, in the sense that \emph{any} control-induced gain that enters the dynamics does so through a function of the control input, $v$, in composition with the $B$ matrix, and conversely $B v(x,u)$ \emph{only} contains the gain introduced by the control input being non-zero.
Separating $f$ this way expands the set of systems we can consider in the main result, including systems that \emph{cannot} be factored into the traditional form for nonlinear control:
\begin{align}
\dot{x} = f(x) + g(x)u.
\end{align}
Second, this result establishes a Koopman representation for a large class of control systems wherein the lifting for the control input is point-wise norm-preserving.
The existence of such a set of lifting functions is essential to the formation of Gramians that describe input-output energy relations.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}
This representation gives an LTI system that is control-affine in $v$.
However, $v$ is a function of both the state and the control.
Care will be taken in later results to bound any error in model reduction introduced by truncating the state and its consequent impact on $v$.
\end{remark}
