In this section, we prove that if the fields $(g,H,\Phi,\eta)$ satisfy \eqref{eq:fermionicvariations}, then
\beq \label{eq:orderzero}
H^{(0)} \= 0~, \quad \Phi^{(0)} \= {\rm const}, \quad N^{(0)} \= 0~,
\eeq
and $(X,J^{(0)}, g^{(0)})$ is a complex K\"ahler manifold. We will often use in the following sections a consequence of this result, which is that
\beq\notag
H \= \cO(\ap)~, \quad \nabla \Phi \= \cO(\ap)~.
\eeq
To see \eqref{eq:orderzero}, send $\ap \rightarrow 0$ in \eqref{eq:H-locked}, \eqref{eq:dilatino2}, \eqref{eq:dilatino1}, \eqref{eq:Bianchi2}. We obtain
\begin{align}
  H^{(0)} -i (\partial-\bar{\partial})_{J^{(0)}} \omega^{(0)} -N_{J^{(0)}} &\= 0~, \\
 i \Lambda_{\omega^{(0)}} H^{(0)} + 2 (\partial - \bar{\partial})_{J^{(0)}} \Phi^{(0)} &\= 0~, \\
  (H^{(0)})^{(3,0)} &\= 0~, \\
  d H^{(0)} &\= 0~.
\end{align}
It follows that
\beq\notag
(N^{(0)})^{(3,0)} \= (H^{(0)})^{(3,0)} \= 0~,
\eeq
and so $N_{J^{(0)}}=0$ since the Nijenhuis tensor is purely $(3,0)$-type. We conclude that $J^{(0)}$ defines an analytic complex structure by the Newlander-Nirenberg theorem. Therefore
\beq \label{eq:orderzero0}
H^{(0)} \= \ii (\partial - \bar{\partial})\omega^{(0)}~, \quad  (H^{(0)})_\mu{}^\mu{}_\lambda \=\! - 2 \partial_\lambda \Phi^{(0)} ~, \quad i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega^{(0)}\=0~.
\eeq
It is well-known \cite{Ivanov:2000fg, Papadopoulos:2002gy} that \eqref{eq:orderzero0} implies that $\omega^{(0)}$ is K\"ahler. To see this explicitly, we can take the trace of the exchange relation on Chern curvatures \eqref{exchange-relation} to derive
\beq \notag
(\ii \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)_\mu{}^{\mu \alpha}{}_{\alpha} \= g^{\alpha \bar{\beta}} \partial_\alpha T_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\beta}} - g^{\alpha \bar{\beta}} \partial_{\bar{\beta}} T_\mu{}^\mu{}_\alpha + T_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} T^{\mu \bar{\nu} \alpha}~, \quad T \= \ii (\partial - \bar{\partial}) \omega~,
\eeq
which is a general identity that holds on any hermitian manifold $(X,\omega)$. Substituting \eqref{eq:orderzero0} into the above identity, we obtain
\beq\notag
4 g^{\alpha \bar{\beta}} \partial_\alpha \partial_{\bar{\beta}} \Phi^{(0)} \= \frac{1}{4}  (H^{(0)})_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} (H^{(0)})^{\mu \bar{\nu} \alpha} ~\geq~ 0~.
\eeq
The maximum principle for elliptic equations on a compact manifold implies that $\Phi^{(0)}$ is a constant and $H^{(0)}=0$. Therefore, at zeroth order the geometry is K\"ahler Calabi-Yau, and we will see that once $\ap$-corrections are included the geometry is perturbed to a non-K\"ahler Calabi-Yau structure.



\subsection{The manifold is complex at  \texorpdfstring{$\ap^2$}{ap2}}
The first order analysis in \cite{Strominger:1986uh} proves that $J^{(0)} + \ap J^{(1)}$ is integrable. We now show the Nijenhuis tensor vanishes to $\ap^2$. The only component   to check is $N^{3,0}$ and its complex conjugate.  From \eqref{eq:N30}, 
\beq\label{eq:N30dH}
 N_{\m\n\l} \=  \frac{\ap}{4}  \Big( (\nabla^-)^\r (\dd H)_{\r\m\nu \lambda} + (\nabla^-)^\rb (\dd H)_{\rb\m\nu \lambda}\Big)~.
\eeq
where $\{ e_\m \}_{\m=1}^3$ is a local frame for $\ccT_X^{(1,0)}$ over an almost-complex manifold $X$.

From \citeS, it is shown that  $F^{0,2} = \cO(\ap^2)$. The curvature of the Hull connection satisfies $R^{\H\,0,2} = \cO(\ap)$ using the  complex geometry valid at first order in $\ap$. We do this explicitly \eqref{eq:ChernHullRelation1}-\eqref{eq:RH02} below.  Consequently, 
\beq\notag
\frac{\ap}{4} \tr F\w F \= \frac{\ap}{4}( \tr F \w F )^{2,2} + \cO(\ap^3)~, \quad \frac{\ap}{4} \tr R^\H \w R^\H \= \frac{\ap}{4}( \tr R^\H \w R^\H )^{2,2} + \cO(\ap^2)~.
\eeq
The Bianchi identity \eqref{eq:Bianchi2} then implies $(\dd H)^{3,1} = \cO(\ap^2)$ and $\dd H^{4,0} = \cO(\ap^3)$. Hence, \eqref{eq:N30dH} gives
\beq\notag
N_{\m\n\l} \= \cO(\ap^3)~.
\eeq
The manifold is complex at $\ap^2$ with $J = J^{(0)} + \ap J^{(1)} + \ap^2 J^{(2)}$ integrable.

Hence, \eqref{eq:H-locked} becomes 
\beq\notag
T \= \ii (\del-\delb)\o \= H - 2\ap P~.
\eeq



\subsection{The constraint for  \texorpdfstring{$i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega$}{deldelbom} at \texorpdfstring{$\ap^2$}{ap2} } \label{sec:ddbar-ap2}

The Bianchi identity \eqref{eq:Bianchi2} repeated here is determined by anomaly cancellation and so unexpectedly not modified by $\ap$ corrections:
\beq\notag
\dd H\= \frac{\ap}{4}\Big(\tr F \w F - \tr R^\H \w R^\H \Big) ~.
\eeq
What is corrected is the relation between $\del\delb\o$ and the gauge bundle. We calculate this here. For this reason it is important that we refer to \eqref{eq:Bianchi2} as the Bianchi identity and not its expression in terms of $\o$. We will derive
 \beq \label{eq:delbdel-o}
\begin{split} 
 2\ii \delb\del \o &- \frac{\ap}{4} \left[ \tr F \w F -  \tr R^\Ch \w R^\Ch \right]    +\cO(\ap^3)\=0~.
 \end{split}
\eeq
To start, we first improve on the previous subsection by showing $[\dd H]^{3,1} =0 + \cO(\ap^3)$.  
Write  $\Th^\H = \Th^\Ch + S$ where $S$ is some tensor
 \beq\label{eq:ChernHullRelation1}
\begin{split}
R^\H &\= R^\CH + \dd_\Th S + S^2~, \\
 \tr R^\H \w R^\H &\=  \tr R^\CH \w R^\CH + \dd\left[ \tr \left(2S\w R^\CH +S\w \dd_{\Th^\Ch} S+ \frac{2}{3} S^3 \right)\right]~.
\end{split}
 \eeq
In  complex coordinates  
\beq\label{eq:RH02}
R^{\H\,0,2}{\,}^\sb{}_\r \= \dd x^{\mb\nb} \nabla^\Ch_\mb H_\nb{}^\sb{}_\r~.
\eeq
 $R^\H $ fails to be a holomorphic instanton at first order in $\ap$, consistent with   \eqref{eq:RhdH}. 




Split $S$ into holomorphic and antiholomorphic components $S = \S + \Sb$, where from \sref{app:ConnectionSymbols} the only non-vanishing components are
\beq\label{eq:cScSb}
\S^\n{}_\rb \=  H_\m{}^\n{}_\rb~ \dd x^\m~, \qquad \Sb^\nb{}_\r \=  H_\mb{}^\nb{}_\r~ \dd x^\mb ~.
\eeq
Using $R^{\Ch\,0,2} = 0$ we find (to save space, we write $\Th$  in this subsection to mean $\Th^\Ch$):
\beq\notag
\begin{split}
 \tr S\w R^\Ch &\= 0~,\qquad  \tr S^3 \=  0~,\\
 \tr S\w \dd_\Th S      &\= 2  \S^\n{}_\rb\w \big( \del_\Th  \Sb^\rb{}_\n + \delb_\Th  \Sb^\rb{}_\n\big) +  \dd\left(\, \tr    \S \w \Sb \right) ~,
\end{split}
\eeq 
we find
\beq\label{eq:trRHtrRCh}
\begin{split}
 \tr R^\H \w R^\H - \tr R^\Ch \w R^\Ch 
 &\=  2( (\del_\Th + \delb_\Th) \S^\n{}_\rb)\w \big( \del_\Th  \Sb^\rb{}_\n + \delb_\Th  \Sb^\rb{}_\n\big) \\[3pt]
 &\qquad +  2 \S^\n{}_\rb\w \big( R^\Ch{}^\rb{}_\sb \Sb{}^\sb{}_\n - R^\Ch{}^\s{}_\n \Sb{}^\rb{}_\s \Big)~.
\end{split}
\eeq
 We can  isolate the components that are not $(2,2)$ in the Bianchi:
\beq\label{eq:trRR31}\notag
[\tr R^\H \w R^\H ]^{(1,3)} \= 2\delb \left(\S{}^\n{}_\rb \w \delb_\Th \Sb{}^\rb{}_\n  \right) ~, \qquad\qquad [\tr R^\H \w R^\H ]^{(0,4)}  \= 0~.\\[3pt]
\eeq
and so 
\beq\notag
[\dd H]^{1,3}  \=  \frac{\ap}{2} \delb \left(\S{}^\n{}_\rb \w \delb_\Th \Sb{}^\rb{}_\n  \right) \= \cO(\ap^3) ~,\qquad \qquad [\dd H]^{0,4} \= 0 ~.\label{eq:dH13}\\[3pt]
 \eeq


Now we find the $\ap^2$-corrected relation between $\o$ and $F$ using the Binachi identity and the gravitino equation. Substitute \eqref{eq:H-locked} into \eqref{eq:Bianchi2}, with terms manifestly $(2,2)$  on the left hand side,
\beq\label{eq:Balp3}
\begin{split}
  \dd \dd^c\o - \frac{\ap}{4} \tr (F \w F)  & \=\! -\frac{\ap}{4}\Big(  \tr R^\H \w R^\H \Big)  \\[4pt]
&\qquad\qquad +  \frac{\ap^2}{8}  \dd \Big(\nabla^{\Bi\,m} ( \tr F \w F - \tr R^\H \w R^\H\Big)_m \Big)~.
\end{split}
\eeq



Using $H=\cO(\ap)$:
$$
\ap \tr R^\H \w \R^\H - \ap \tr R^\Ch \w R^\Ch \= \cO(\ap^3)~,
$$
from \eqref{eq:trRHtrRCh}. 

While in \eqref{eq:dP}, we show that $\dd P = \cO(\ap^3)$ and so \eqref{eq:Balp3} becomes \eqref{eq:delbdel-o}, which was to be established.

{\bf Remark:} The nonlinear constraint on $i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega$, taken as the following stand-alone mathematical equation
\beq \label{eq:delbdel-o1}
2\ii \delb\del \o - \frac{\ap}{4} \left[ \tr F \w F -  \tr R^\Ch \w R^\Ch \right] \=0~,
\eeq
is given a differential geometric interpretation in \cite{McOrist:2024zdz}, building on \cite{delaOssa:2014cia, McOrist:2021dnd}. Let $E \rightarrow X$ be a holomorphic bundle over a complex manifold $X$ with $F$ the Chern curvature of a hermitian metric on the bundle $E$. There is a differential operator
\beq\notag
\bar{D} : \Omega^{(0,p)}(Q) \rightarrow \Omega^{(0,p+1)}(Q) , \quad Q= \ccT_X^{*(1,0)} \oplus {\rm End} \, E \oplus \ccT_X^{(1,0)}
\eeq
of the form
\beq\notag
\bar{D}= \begin{bmatrix}
\delb & - \frac{\ap}{4} \cF^* & \cT - \frac{\ap}{4} \cR \nabla \\
0 & \delb_A & \cF \\
0 & 0 & \delb
\end{bmatrix}~,
\eeq
which satisfies $\bar{D}^2=0$ if and only if \eqref{eq:delbdel-o1} holds. The definitions are:
\begin{align}
  \cF \colon \Omega^{(0,p)}(\ccT_X^{(1,0)}) 
  &\longrightarrow \Omega^{(0,p+1)}({\rm End} \, E)~, \nonumber \\
  \D 
  &\longmapsto F_{\mu \bar{\nu}} \, \dd x^{\bar{\nu}} \wedge \D^\mu~, \label{eq:F_def} \\[1ex]
%
  \cF^* \colon \Omega^{(0,p)}({\rm End} \, E) 
  &\longrightarrow \Omega^{(0,p)}( \ccT_X^{*(1,0)}), \nonumber \\
  \aa 
  &\longmapsto {\rm Tr}(F_{\mu \bar{\nu}}) \, \dd x^\mu \otimes (\dd x^{\bar{\nu}} \wedge \aa), \label{eq:Fstar_def} \\[1ex]
%
  \cT \colon \Omega^{(0,p)}(\ccT_X^{(1,0)}) 
  &\longrightarrow \Omega^{(0,p)}(\ccT_X^{*(1,0)})~, \nonumber \\
  \D 
  &\longmapsto T_{\rho \bar{\nu} \mu} \, \dd x^\rho \otimes (\dd x^{\bar{\nu}} \wedge \D^\mu), \label{eq:H_def} \\[1ex]
%
  (\cR \nabla) \colon \Omega^{(0,p)}(\ccT_X^{(1,0)}) 
  &\longrightarrow \Omega^{(0,p+1)}(\ccT_X^{*(1,0)})~, \nonumber \\
  \D 
  &\longmapsto - \frac{1}{p!} R^\Ch{}_{\rho \bar{\mu}}{}^\sigma{}_\lambda \, 
    \nabla^\Bi_\sigma \D^\lambda{}_{\bar{\kappa}_1 \dots \bar{\kappa}_p} \, 
    \dd x^\rho \otimes \dd x^{\bar{\mu} \bar{\kappa}_1 \cdots \bar{\kappa}_p}~.\label{eq:R_def}
\end{align}
The operator $\bar{D}$ does not quite define a holomorphic structure on the vector bundle $Q$ due to the $\ap \cR \nabla$ term. Given \eqref{eq:delbdel-o1}, then the differential operator $\bar{D}$ defines an elliptic complex
\beq\notag
0 \longrightarrow \Gamma(Q) \overset{\bar{D}}{\longrightarrow} \Omega^{(0,1)}(Q) \overset{\bar{D}}{\longrightarrow} \Omega^{(0,2)}(Q) \overset{\bar{D}}{\longrightarrow} \cdots~.
\eeq
We refer to \cite{Chisamanga:2024xbm, 2409.04382} for cohomological calculations and physical interpretations of this complex. There is also a mathematical interpretation given in the language of generalized geometry \cite{Garcia-Fernandez:2015hja, Garcia-Fernandez:2018ypt} of related equations of the form $i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega = c \langle F \wedge F \rangle$ where $c$ is a bi-invariant symmetric pairing on the Lie algebra of the structure group of the bundle. For more on interactions between generalized geometry and heterotic string theory, see \cite{Ashmore:2019rkx, Garcia-Fernandez:2020awc}.


\subsection{Holomorphic volume form}
We have shown that the supersymmetry equations \eqref{eq:fermionicvariations} lead to an integrable complex structure $J$ at order $\ap^2$. Next, we investigate holomorphicity of a nowhere vanishing 3-form at order $\ap^2$. Consider the 3-form $\Psi \in \Omega^3(X)$ determined by the positive chirality spinor $\eta$ given by
\beq \label{eq:3form}
\Psi_{ijk} \=\bar{\eta}^T \g_{ijk} \bar{\eta}~.
\eeq
One can check using $\gamma_7 \eta = \eta$ and $\gamma_\alpha \eta = 0$ in holomorphic directions that $\Psi \in \Omega^{3,0}(X,\mathbb{C})$. The gravitino equation \eqref{parallel-spinor} implies
\beq \label{parallel-3form}
 \quad \nabla^{\Bi} \Psi \= 0~,
\eeq
where the connection $\nabla^{\Bi}$ is defined in \eqref{corrected-connection}. Since the connection $\nabla^{\Bi}$ also satisfies $\nabla^{\Bi} g = 0$ and $\nabla^{\Bi} J =0$, its holonomy is contained in the group $SU(3)$.
\beq\notag
{\rm Hol}(\nabla^{\Bi}) \subseteq SU(3)~.
\eeq
The pair $(\omega,\Psi)$ forms an $SU(3)$-structure, namely
\beq\notag
\omega \wedge \Psi \= 0~, \quad i \Psi \wedge \bar{\Psi} \= \frac{\omega^3}{3!}~, \quad |\Psi|_g \= 1~.
\eeq
We note that neither $\omega$ nor $\Psi$ are closed. The constant norm 3-form $\Psi$ will not be a holomorphic volume form at order $\ap^2$, but we will arrange that $\Omega = e^f \Psi$ solves $\bar{\partial} \Omega = 0$ for some conformal factor $e^f$.



Let us rewrite the gravitino equation for $\nabla^{\LC} \eta$ in a different way for later use. Using $\g_\alpha \eta = 0$ in holomorphic directions and $H^{3,0}=\ap P^{3,0} = \cO(\ap^3)$, from the gravitino equation \eqref{parallel-spinor} we derive
\beq\notag
\nabla^{\LC}{}_\alpha \eta = \frac{1}{4} H_\alpha{}^{\mu \bar{\nu}} g_{\mu \bar{\nu}} \eta - \frac{\ap}{2} P_\alpha{}^{\mu \bar{\nu}} g_{\mu \bar{\nu}} \eta~,
\eeq
in holomorphic indices. We next substitute the dilatino equation \eqref{eq:dilatino2}.
\beq \label{eq:dilatino+grav0}
\nabla^{\LC}{}_\alpha \eta \= \frac{1}{2} (\partial_\alpha \Phi) \eta + \frac{\ap}{4} P_\alpha{}^{\mu \bar{\nu}} g_{\mu \bar{\nu}} \eta~.
\eeq
We can also combine the dilatino equation \eqref{eq:dilatino2} with the locking relation for $H$ given in \eqref{eq:H-locked}, knowing now that the Nijenhuis tensor is zero, to see
\beq \label{eq:dilatino+grav}
(i \partial \omega)_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\lambda} - \ap P_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\lambda} \=\! -2\partial_\lambda \Phi~.
\eeq
Combining \eqref{eq:dilatino+grav0} and \eqref{eq:dilatino+grav} yields the identity
\beq \label{eq:dilatino+grav1}
\nabla^{\LC}{}_\alpha \eta \=\!-\frac{1}{4}  (i \partial \omega)_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\alpha} \eta~.
\eeq

With \eqref{eq:dilatino+grav1}, we can now compute the covariant derivative of the 3-form $\Psi$ defined in \eqref{eq:3form}.
\beq\notag
\nabla^{\LC}{}_{\bar{\alpha}} \Psi_{ijk} \= 2 \bar{\eta}^T \g_{ijk} \nabla^{\rm LC}{}_{\bar{\alpha}} \bar{\eta} \=\! -\half   (i \bar{\partial} \omega)_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\alpha}} \Psi_{ijk}~.
\eeq
The Levi-Civita connection induced on top forms $\Psi \in \Omega^{3,0}(X)$ is
\beq\notag
\nabla^{\LC}{}_{\bar{\alpha}} \Psi_{ijk} \= \partial_{\bar{\alpha}} \Psi_{ijk} - \Gamma^{\LC}{}_{\bar{\alpha}}{}^\mu{}_\mu\Psi_{ijk} \= \partial_{\bar{\alpha}} \Psi_{ijk}- \frac{1}{2} (i \bar{\partial} \omega)_{\bar{\alpha}}{}^\mu{}_\mu \Psi_{ijk}.
\eeq
Therefore
\beq\notag
 \partial_{\bar{\alpha}} \Psi_{ijk} \=\! - (\ii \bar{\partial} \omega)_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\alpha}} \Psi_{ijk}~.
 \eeq
 Substituting \eqref{eq:dilatino+grav} gives
\beq\notag
 \partial_{\bar{\alpha}} \Psi_{ijk} \= (2 \partial_{\bar{\alpha}} \Phi+ \ap P_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\alpha}}) \Psi_{ijk}~.
 \eeq
 As expected, $\Psi$ is holomorphic to zeroth order in $\ap$, but to higher order it must be corrected by setting $\Omega = e^f \Psi$ and solving $\bar{\partial} \Omega = 0$ for a suitable conformal factor. For this, we use the identity \eqref{eq:trP}, which states
\beq \label{eq:traceP}
\ap P_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\alpha}} \= \frac{\ap^2}{16} \partial_{\bar{\alpha}} (\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)~,
\eeq
at order $\ap^2$ and which will be derived later on in \eqref{eq:trP}. We can then define
\beq\notag
\Omega \= \exp \bigg( -2 \Phi - \frac{\ap^2}{16}  ( \tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2) \bigg) \Psi, \quad \Psi_{ijk} \= \bar{\eta}^T \gamma_{ijk} \bar{\eta}~,
\eeq
so that at order $\ap^2$ we produced $\Omega \in \Omega^{3,0}(X,\mathbb{C})$ which is nowhere vanishing with $\bar{\partial} \Omega = 0$. Taking the norm of $\Omega$ gives
\beq \label{eq:norm-Omega}
\log \norm{\Omega}_g \=\! -2 \Phi - \frac{\ap^2}{16}  ( \tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)~.
\eeq
As $(X,\omega)$ is a complex hermitian manifold with holomorphic volume form $\Omega$ but with $d \omega \neq 0$, it is a non-K\"ahler Calabi-Yau threefold.


\subsection{Conformally balanced equation}
The supersymmetry equations on spinors have produced a pair $(\omega, \Omega)$, where $\omega$ is a hermitian metric on a complex manifold and $\Omega$ is a holomorphic volume form. We now derive the conformally balanced relation $d( \norm{\Omega}_g \omega^2)=0$.


We combine \eqref{eq:traceP} and \eqref{eq:dilatino+grav} to obtain
\beq \label{todo:recheck}
\frac{1}{2} (\ii \partial \omega)_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\lambda} \=\! -\partial_\lambda \Phi - \frac{\ap^2}{32} \partial_{\lambda} ( \tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)~.
\eeq
Let $e^{\hat{\Phi}}$ be a conformal factor which we introduce to solve $d (e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} \omega^2)=0$. We substitute the supersymmetry constraint \eqref{todo:recheck} into the general identity \eqref{eq:dconfbal} to obtain
\beq\notag
\partial (e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} \omega^2) \= 2 e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} \bigg[ \partial \Phi + \frac{\ap^2}{32} \partial ( \tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2) -  \partial \hat{\Phi} \bigg] \wedge \omega^2~.
\eeq
If we let
\beq\notag
\hat{\Phi} \= \Phi + \frac{\ap^2}{32} (\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)~,
\eeq
then $d (e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} \omega^2)=0$, or written in full
\beq
\dd \bigg( \exp \bigg[ -2 \Phi - \frac{\ap^2}{16} (\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2 \bigg] \omega^2 \bigg) \= 0~.
\eeq
We can write this conformal factor $e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}}$ in terms of the holomorphic volume form $\Omega$ by substituting \eqref{eq:norm-Omega}, and the result is $d ( \norm{\Omega}_g \omega^2 )=0$.

The mathematical significance of the conformally balanced equation becomes apparent when the gaugino equation \eqref{eq:fermionicvariations} is included, which states $g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} F_{\mu \bar{\nu}}=0$. The pair $(\omega,F)$ then solves
\beq\notag
d ( \norm{\Omega}_g  \omega^2 )\=0, \quad F \wedge \omega^2\= 0~.
\eeq
The (conformal) closedness of $\omega^2$ is what allows the numerical pairing $[\norm{\Omega}_g \omega^2] \cdot c_1(S)$ to be defined on subsheaves $S \subseteq E$, and this is what allows a well-defined notion of slope stability as a criterion for the non-K\"ahler Donaldson-Uhlenbeck-Yau theorem \cite{Uhlenbeck1986,0529.53018,0664.53011}: if $[\norm{\Omega}_g \omega^2] \cdot c_1(S) < 0$ for all strict torsion-free coherent subsheaves $S \subseteq E$, then there exists a solution to $F \wedge \omega^2= 0$.




 \section{Integrability of supersymmetry algebra} \label{sec:integrability}

 \subsection{Preliminaries}
Let $\ve$ be the ten-dimensional Majorana Weyl spinor. Recall, there is the torsion of the Bismut connection $T$, its relation to $H$ and a new three-form $\hat H$ that appears in the dilatino variation:
\beq\label{eq:wtHhatH}
T_{mab} \= H_{mab} - 2\ap P_{mab} \= \hat H_{mab} + \ap P_{mab}~, \qquad \hat H_{mab} \= H_{mab} - 3 \ap P_{mab}~.
\eeq
The gravitino variation implies a spinor covariantly constant with respect to the $T$ connection. Take a second covariant derivative of the gravitino variation with respect to Levi-Civita:
\beq
\nabla_n\nabla_m \ve - \frac{1}{8} \nabla_n T_{mab} \gamma^{ab} \ve - \frac{1}{64}T_{mab} T_{ncd} \gamma^{ab} \gamma^{cd} \ve \= 0~,
\eeq
where in this section unless otherwise noted $\nabla = \nabla^\LC$. 
Antisymmetrise using
$
[\nabla_m,\nabla_n] \= \frac{1}{4} R_{mnab} \gamma^{ab}~,
$
where $\g^a$ are the gamma matrices and use the first line of \eqref{eq:gamma_rel2} to give
\beq\label{eq:integ0a}
\dd x^m \w \dd x^n \left( R_{mnab} - \nabla_{[m} T_{n]ab} + \half T_{[m|ac} T_{n]}{}^c{}_b \right) \gamma^{ab} \ve \= 0~.
\eeq
This result can be derived in a second way using Cartan's formulation. Write the gravitino variation as
\beq
 \nabla^\Bi_m \ve \= \del_m \ve + \frac{1}{4} \Th^\Bi_{m\,ab} \gamma^{ab}~, \qquad  \Th^\Bi_m{}^a{}_b \= \Th^\LC_m{}^a{}_b - \half T_m{}^a{}_b~.
\eeq
Apply the integrability condition, that the commutator must vanish:
\beq\label{eq:integ0}
[ \nabla^\Bi_m, \nabla^\Bi_n] \ve \= \frac{1}{4}  R^\Bi_{mnab} \gamma^{ab}\ve\=0~, \qquad  R^\Bi \= \dd \Th^\Bi +  \Th^\Bi \w  \Th^\Bi~.
\eeq
Now, as $\Th^\Bi = \Th^\LC - \half T$ we have
$$
 R^\Bi \= R^\LC - \half \dd_{\nabla^\LC} T +\qrt  T \w T~, 
$$
where $\nabla^\LC$ acts only on the spinor indices labelled $a,b$. Evaluating this gives 
\beq
 R^\Bi_{ab}  \= \half \dd x^m \w \dd x^n \left( R_{mnab}^\LC - \nabla^\LC_{[m} T_{n]ab} + \half T_{[m|a}{}^c T_{n]cb} \right)~,
\eeq
which when put in \eqref{eq:integ0} gives \eqref{eq:integ0a} as promised.

As tempting as it is, we cannot  identify the terms in parenthesis with zero in \eqref{eq:integ0a}. Instead, multiply \eqref{eq:integ0a} by $\g^n$ and use $\g^n \g^{ab} =   g^{na} \g^b - g^{nb} \g^a + \g^{nab}$ via the middle line of \eqref{eq:gamma_rel1} to give
\beq\label{eq:integrab2}
\begin{split}
 \half R^\Bi_{mnab} \g^n\g^{ab} \ve \=&\left(\Ric_{mn}  - \qrt T_{mab} T_{n}{}^{ab}  +\half  \nabla^{\LC\,p} T_{pmn}\right) \g^n\ve \\
&\qquad \quad +\frac{1}{4} \left(\nabla^\LC_m T_{nab} - \nabla^\LC_n T_{mab}-  T_{ma}{}^c T_{bnc}   \right) \gamma^{nab} \ve\= 0~.
\end{split}
\eeq
Note we have used $H^p{}_{pq} = 0$, $\Ric_{mn} = R_{mpn}{}^p$ and the Bianchi identity for the Riemann curvature $R_{m[nab]}=0$ has been used. Our goal is to massage the term proportional to $\g^n \ve$ to be close to the equations of motion as possible.

We need the covariant derivative of the dilatino, the second line of \eqref{eq:fermionicvariations}, taking a derivative using $\nabla^\Bi \ve = 0$ we find
$$
 \left( 2  \nabla^\Bi_m \nabla_n \Phi \g^n\ve-  \frac{1}{6}\nabla^\Bi_m \hat H_{nab} \g^{nab}\right) \ve \= 0~.
$$
which in components becomes
\beq\label{eq:covderivdil}
\begin{split}
  \left( 2 \nabla_m \nabla_n \Phi + H_m{}^p{}_n \nabla_p \Phi \right) \g^n\ve \= \frac{1}{6}\left( \nabla_m \hat H_{nab} - \frac{3}{2} H_{mn}{}^p H_{abp} \right)\g^{nab}\ve ~,
\end{split}
\eeq
where we use $\hat H$ in \eqref{eq:wtHhatH}.

Finally, multiply the gaugino equation $\Fslash \ve = 0$ by $F_{pn}\g^n$ to obtain
\beq\label{eq:gaugeinstantongamma}
F_{pn} F_{ab} \g^{nab} \ve + 2 F_p{}^a F_{an} \g^n \ve \= 0~.
\eeq
Hence, 
\beq\label{eq:gaugino3}
\begin{split}
 &\frac{1}{12} \tr (F \w F)_{mnab} \g^{nab} \ve \= F_m{}^a F_{na}\g^n \ve~, \qquad {\rm where}~~\\[5pt]
&\qquad\qquad \half \tr (F\w F)_{mnab} = F_{mn}  F_{ab} - F_{ma} F_{nb} - F_{mb} F_{an} ~.
\end{split}
\eeq
Note that
\beq\label{eq:Hconfdiv}
- e^{2\Phi} \nabla^p (e^{-2\Phi} H_{pmn} ) \g^n \ve \= - \nabla^p H_{pmn} \g^n \ve + 2(\nabla^p \Phi) H_{pmn} \g^n \ve~.
\eeq


With this collection of results in hand we return to the integrability condition \eqref{eq:integrab2}.  
Using \eqref{eq:wtHhatH}, \eqref{eq:gaugino3} and
\begin{align}
 & (\dd  H)_{mnab} \= \nabla_m H_{nab} - \nabla_n H_{abm} + \nabla_a H_{bmn} - \nabla_b H_{mna}~, \notag\\
& (\dd H)_{mnab} \g^{nab}  \= (\nabla_m H_{nab} - 3 \nabla_n H_{abm} )\g^{nab}~,\notag
\end{align}
gives
\beq\notag
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{mn} - \qrt  H_{mab}  H_{n}{}^{ab}\right) \g^n\ve    -\qrt \left(  H_{ma}{}^c  H_{bnc}   \right) \gamma^{nab} \ve\\
&\quad\=-  \frac{1}{12} \left(\nabla^\LC_m T_{nab} - 3\nabla^\LC_n T_{abm}  + 2\nabla^\LC_m \hat H_{nab}  +2\ap \nabla^\LC_m  P_{nab} \right) \g^{nab}\\
&\qquad\qquad +\ap \nabla^p P_{pmn} \g^n \ve -  H_{pmn} ( \nabla^p \Phi)  \g^n \ve - \half e^{2\Phi} \nabla^p (e^{-2\Phi}  H_{pmn} ) \g^n \ve\\[5pt]
\end{split}
\raisetag{3.5cm}
\eeq
Using \eqref{eq:covderivdil}, together with \eqref{eq:Pdef2} that $\ap P \propto \ap \dd^\dag (\dd H) + \cO(\ap^3)$, so that $\dd^\dag P= \cO(\ap^3)$, this becomes 
\beq\notag
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{mn} + 2 \nabla_m \nabla_n \Phi - \qrt  H_{mab}  H_{n}{}^{ab}\right) \g^n\ve  +  \half e^{2\Phi} \nabla^p (e^{-2\Phi}  H_{pmn} ) \g^n   \\
&\quad\=\!- \frac{1}{12} (\dd T)_{mnab} \g^{nab} - \frac{\ap}{6} \nabla_m P_{nab} \g^{nab} ~. 
\end{split}
\raisetag{3.5cm}
\eeq
Using $T = H - 2\ap P$ and \eqref{eq:gaugeinstantongamma}-\eqref{eq:gaugino3} 
\beq\label{eq:integrab4}
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{mn} +   2\nabla_m \nabla_n \Phi - \qrt  H_{mab}  H_{n}{}^{ab} + \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  F_m{}^a F_{na} \right) \g^n\ve +   \half e^{2\Phi} \nabla^p (e^{-2\Phi}  H_{pmn} ) \g^n\\
&\quad\=\!- \frac{1}{12}\Big(   (\dd  H)_{mnab} - \frac{\ap}{4} (\tr F^2)_{mnab} \Big) \g^{nab} \ve   + \frac{\ap}{6} \Big(  
 (\dd P)_{mnab} -\nabla^\LC_m  P_{nab}   \Big) \g^{nab} \ve~.   
\end{split}
\raisetag{2cm}
\eeq

The first line is converging on the graviton equation of motion. The $H$ equation of motion is there. The second line is converging on the Bianchi identity plus terms manifestly $\ap^2$. 

 We now add and subtract terms to get the graviton equation of motion and evaluate the heterotic Bianchi identity \eqref{eq:Bianchi2}:
\beq\label{eq:integrab5}
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{mn} +   2\nabla_m \nabla_n \Phi - \qrt  H_{mab}  H_{n}{}^{ab} + \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  F_m{}^a F_{na} -  \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  R^\H{}_m{}^a R^\H{}_{na} \right) \g^n\ve \\[5pt]
&+   \half e^{2\Phi} \nabla^p (e^{-2\Phi}  H_{pmn} ) \g^n \ve  \=\! -   \frac{\ap}{4}\tr \left(   R^\H{}_m{}^a R^\H{}_{na} \g^n\ve -  \frac{1}{12} (R^\H \w R^\H)_{mnab}  \g^{nab} \ve \right) \\[5pt]
&~~~ + \frac{\ap}{6} \Big(  
 (\dd P)_{mnab} -\nabla^\LC_m  P_{nab}   \Big) \g^{nab} \ve ~.\\[5pt]  
\end{split}\raisetag{2.5cm}
\eeq
The left hand side are the graviton and $H$ equations of motion. The second line includes a term that is measuring difference of $R^\H$ being traceless. To see this, note that if we treat $R^\H$ in the same way as $F$, then we could apply \eqref{eq:gaugeinstantongamma}-\eqref{eq:gaugino3} and this line would vanish. $R^\H$ is not an instanton \citeBdR, see  \eqref{eq:RhdH} and \eqref{eq:RH02}. The final line are all $\ap^2$ corrections.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$\SU(3)$}{SU3}  manifold}
Thus far we have not specified the dimension of the compactification. To make progress we  specialise \eqref{eq:integrab5} to $SU(3)$. This means we can introduce a complex structure, as we proved earlier that the manifold is complex. The ten-dimensional spinor is written as $\ve = \zeta\otimes \eta + \cc$, where $\eta$ is a Weyl spinor of $SO(6)\cong SU(4)$. As usual we focus just on the internal manifold and discard the $d=4$ spacetime component. 

\subsubsection{\texorpdfstring{$H$}{H} and \texorpdfstring{$F$}{F}  equations of motion} \label{sec:HFeom}

First we comment on the $H$ and $F$ equations of motion. First, the  gaugino variation $\Fslash \ve = 0$. For an $SU(3)$ manifold, 
\beq
F_{\m\nb} \g^{\m\nb} \eta \= 0~,\qquad \o^{\m\nb} F_{\m\nb} \= 0~,
\eeq
where we use \eqref{eq:spinor_rel}. The gaugino is understood to come multiplied by an $\ap$ and so we keep terms up to order $\ap$. This means we can differentiate with respect to the Bismut connection and it will kill $\o$ leaving
\beq\notag
\begin{split}
\o^{\m\nb} \nabla^\Bi_\r F_{\m\nb} &\= \o^{\m\nb}\left( \del^A_\r F_{\m\nb} -\G^\LC_\r{}^\s{}_\m F_{\s\nb} + \half H_\r{}^\s{}_\m F_{\s\nb} + H_\r{}^\sb{}_\nb F_{\m\sb}  \right)
\= \ii \nabla^{\Bi\, \nb} (e^{-2\Phi} F_{\nb\r})~,
\end{split}
\eeq
where $\nabla^A_\m F_{\m\nb}$ is the gauge covariant derivative and we use the Bianchi identity $\del_A F = 0$. We have replaced the torsion $T$ by $H$ as the difference is $\ap^2$, keeping in mind the gaugino comes with an implicit $\ap$, this difference is really $\ap^3$.  We also use $2\nabla^\sb \Phi = H^\r{}_\r{}^\sb + \cO(\ap^2)$. This means supersymmetry implies the gaugino equation of motion and we can use it in the following.

Second, we show that the $H$ equations of motion follow directly from the supersymmetry conditions. Using that $\star \eta^{2,1} = \ii  \eta^{2,1} - \eta_\m{}^\m\w\o$, we find
\beq\label{eq:Tcalib}
\star T \= - e^{2\hat\Phi} \dd (e^{-2\hat \Phi} \o)~, \qquad \hat \Phi= \Phi + \frac{\ap^2}{32} (\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)~,
\eeq
where we use $T = \dd^c\o = H-2\ap P$, the dilatino equation \eqref{eq:dilatino2} applied to $T$ combined with the trace of $P$ \eqref{eq:trP}, calculated below. Hence,   
\beq
\begin{split}
 e^{-2\hat \Phi} \star H &\= 2\ap e^{-2\hat \Phi} \star  P -  \dd (e^{-2\hat \Phi} \o) \\
\end{split}
\eeq
From \eqref{eq:Pdef2} below we see that 
$$
\ap  \dd (e^{-2\hat \Phi} \star P) \=  \ap^2 (\dd^\dag)^2 (\tr F\w F - \tr R^\H \w R^\H)  -2 \dd \hat\Phi \w \star \ap P = \cO(\ap^3)
$$
 and so finally noting that $e^{-2\hat\Phi} H = e^{-2\Phi} H + \cO(\ap^3)$ we end up with
\beq\label{eq:Hcalib}
\dd \left( e^{-2 \Phi} \star H \right) \=0~,
\eeq
which is the H equation of motion up to and including $\ap^2$. It follows directly from supersymmetry.


\subsubsection{Preliminary results on \texorpdfstring{$P$}{P} and \texorpdfstring{$H$}{H} }

We derive some results to be used in the following sections. In the following we assume supersymmetry holds. We have already shown to zeroth order \sref{sec:orderzero} that the $SU(3)$ manifold is complex, \K with Ricci-flat metric. In fact, the Bergshoeff-de Roo algebra has \eqref{eq:RhdH} which to zeroth order in $\ap$ shows $R^\H$ satisfies a Yang-Mills equation and that $R^{\H\,0,2} = \cO(\ap)$ (shown directly using \eqref{eq:susy3} for an $SU(3)$ manifold in the same way as $F$; or using \eqref{eq:RH02} with $H=\cO(\ap)$). Together with Yau's theorem imply the metric is Ricci flat to zeroth order $\Ric_{mn} = \cO(\ap)$. 

For any $p$-form $Q$, we write $Q_m = \frac{1}{(p-1)!} Q_{m n_2\cdots n_p} \dd x^{n_2\cdots n_p}$ and its divergence satisfies:
 \beq\label{eq:divBism}
\begin{split}
  \nabla^{\Bi\, m} Q_m &\= g^{mn} \left( \nabla^\LC_n Q_m + \half H_n{}^p{}_m Q_p  \right)  \= \nabla^{\LC\,m} Q_m + \cO(\ap^2) \\[3pt]
 &\= \left(\nabla^\Ch_\m Q_\nb + \nabla^\Ch_\nb Q_\m \right)g^{\m\nb} + \cO(\ap^2)~,
\end{split}
 \eeq
 where we use the dilatino equation \eqref{eq:dilatino2} with \eqref{eq:Pdef} and that $\dd H = \cO(\ap)$. 

The tensor  $P$ in \eqref{eq:Pdef} simplifies using \eqref{eq:divBism}
\beq\label{eq:Pdef2}
\begin{split}
 \ap P &\= \frac{\ap}{4} e^{2\Phi} \nabla^{\Bi \,m}\left( e^{-2\Phi} (\dd H)_m \right) \\
 &\=  \frac{\ap^2}{8} \nabla^m \left( \tr F_m \w F - \tr R^\H{}_m \w R^\H \right) + \cO(\ap^3) \=\!- \frac{\ap}{4} \dd^\dag \dd H ~.
\end{split}
\eeq
Due to the $\ap^2$, $\nabla^m$ can be any connection and the dilatino equation $\nabla_\r \Phi = \half H_\r{}^\t{}_\t + \cdots  =  \cO(\ap)$ allowed us to eliminate $e^{\Phi}$ from $P$.  Furthermore, $\dd^\dag P = \cO(\ap^3)$. 

We can further simplify the divergence of the Chern class
\beq\label{eq:divTrF2}
\begin{split}
 \frac{\ap^2}{2}  &  \nabla^{\Bi\,m}\Big(\tr (F\w F)_m\Big) \=  \ap^2 \tr \left( \nabla_A^{m} F\w F_m + F\w \nabla_A^{m} F_m\right)\\[5pt]
 & \=\ap^2 \tr ( \dd_A F_m\w F^m)  +\cO(\ap^3)  \=\frac{\ap^2}{2} ( \delb - \del) \tr (F^\r \w F_\r) ~,
\end{split}
\eeq
 where we use $\dd_A F =  0$, the $F$ equation of motion (imposed via the gaugino variation), the dilatino equation implies any $\nabla \Phi$ can be dropped as they are $\ap^3$. Repeat for the corresponding term in $R^\H$, which is straightforward due to $\nabla^m R^\H_m = \cO(\ap)$.

 Consequently, 
 \beq\label{eq:apP}
 \ap P \= \frac{\ap^2}{8} (\delb -\del)\Big( \tr (F^\r \w F_\r) - \tr (R^{\H\,\r} \w R^\H_\r) \Big) ~.
 \eeq
Some immediate results follow. The term  $\ap P^{3,0}$ is higher order
\beq\label{eq:P30}
\ap P^{3,0}  \=0~.
\eeq
The component   $P^{2,1}$: 
\beq\label{eq:P21}
\ap P^{2,1} \=- \frac{\ap^2}{8} \del\left( \tr  F^\r \w F_\r  - \tr R^{\H\,\r} \w R^\H_\r \right)~.
\eeq
 In components
\beq\label{eq:P21components}
 \ap P_{\m\n\sb}  \=- \frac{\ap^2}{4} \nabla_{[\m} \left( \tr  F^\r{}_{\n]} \w F_{\r \sb}   - \tr  R^\H{}^\r{}_{\n]} \w R^\H{}_{\r \sb}  \right) ~,
\eeq
while $P^{1,2}$ and $P^{0,3}$ follow by complex conjugation.

The trace of of $P$ is:
\beq\label{eq:trP}
\ap P^\b{}_{\b m} \dd x^m \=\frac{ \ap^2}{16}   (\del - \delb) \left(\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2  \right) ~,
\eeq
where we again use the $F$ equation of motion and that $F$ is traceless. 





The divergence  $\del^\dag P^{2,1}$ starts with  \eqref{eq:P21components}:
\beq\label{eq:divP21b}
\begin{split}
 \ap \del^\dag  P^{2,1} 
 &\= \frac{\ap^2}{8} \left(\nabla^\m \nabla_\m\left( \tr F^\r \w F_\r - \tr R^\H{}^\r \w R_\r^\H \right) - \frac{1}{2} \del\delb \left(\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2 \right)\right) ~,\\
\end{split}
\eeq
we use that to lowest order the metric is \K Ricci flat.  The $F$ equation of motion is used as well as  the $H$ equation of motion.

The divergence   $\delb^\dag P^{2,1}$ vanishes:
\beq\label{eq:divbP21}
\begin{split}
\ap \delb^\dag P^{2,1} &
\= \frac{\ap^2}{8} \nabla^\sb \left(  \del \left(\tr F^\r \w F_\r\right) \right)_\sb - (F\leftrightarrow R^\H)  \\[5pt]
&\= \frac{\ap^2}{8}  \del\,  \tr ( \del_A F^{\r\sb} )\w F_{\r\sb} 
  - (F\leftrightarrow R^\H) \= 0~,\\
\end{split}\raisetag{0.5cm}
\eeq
where we have used $[\nabla_\mb,\nabla_\nb]=\cO(\ap)$, that  $\del^2 = 0$ and as usual have suppressed $\ap^3$ terms.





Putting together \eqref{eq:trP} and \eqref{eq:divbP21} gives
\beq\label{eq:dPdivPgradtrP}
\begin{split}
\ap \left(  \nabla_\m P^\a{}_{\a\n}  - \nabla^\bb P_{\bb\m\n} \right) &\= \frac{\ap^2}{16} \nabla_\m \nabla_\n( \tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)~.
\end{split}
\eeq

We need some similar results for $\dd H$. The trace of $(\dd H)^{3,1}$: 
\beq\label{eq:dH31}
\begin{split}
( \dd H)^{3,1}&\= \frac{1}{3!} (\dd H)_{\m\n\r\sb} \dd x^{\m\n\r\sb} \= \delb H^{3,0} + \del H^{2,1} \\
&\=  \frac{1}{3!} \left(- \nabla_\sb H_{\m\n\r} +  \nabla_\m H_{\n\r\sb} +  \nabla_\n H_{\r\m\sb} +  \nabla_\r H_{\m\n\sb}\right) \dd x^{\m\n\r\sb} ~.
\end{split}
\eeq 
Hence, 
\beq\label{eq:dHtr}
\begin{split}
(\dd H)_{\m\n\r}{}^\r  
&\=  \left(- \nabla^\r H_{\m\n\r} +  \nabla^\sb H_{\m\n\sb} -  \nabla_\m H{}_\n{}^\r{}_{\r}{} +  \nabla_\n H{}_\m{}^\r{}_{\r} \right)~.
\end{split}
\eeq
The dilatino equation \eqref{eq:dilatino2} with  \eqref{eq:wtHhatH} implies $\nabla_\n H_\m{}^\r{}_\r   = 2\nabla_\n \nabla_\m \Phi + 3\ap \nabla_\n P_\m{}^\r{}_\r$. 
Hence, with \eqref{eq:trP}, 
\beq\notag
\nabla_\n H_\m{}^\r{}_\r  - \nabla_\m H_\n{}^\r{}_\r   \= 0~.
\eeq


While the dilatino equation \eqref{eq:dilatino2}  implies $\hat H_{\m\n\r} = H_{\m\n\r} - 3\ap P_{\m\n\r} \= 0$, and so with \eqref{eq:P30}, we see that
$H_{\m\n\r} = \cO(\ap^3)$, that does not mean $\nabla_\sb H_{\m\n\r}$ vanishes. Levi-Civita in complex coordinates requires care. Using \eqref{eq:LCsymbols} we see that 
$$
-\nabla_\sb H_{\m\n\r} \dd x^{\m\n\r} \= \frac{3}{2} H_\sb{}^\lb{}_\m H_{\lb \n\r} \dd x^{\m\n\r}~, \qquad -\nabla^\r H_{\m\n\r} \= H^{\r\lb}{}_{[\m} H_{\n]\r\lb} - \half H^\r{}_{\r\l} H_{\m\n}{}^\l~.
$$
Together with the $H$ equation of motion and an application of dilatino equation, we find  
\beq\label{eq:divHdH}
\begin{split} 
(\dd H)_{\m\n\r}{}^\r  &\=  -2\nabla^\r H_{\m\n\r} +  \nabla^p H_{\m\n p} \=   -2\nabla^\r H_{\m\n\r} +  2(\nabla^p \Phi) H_{\m\n p}\\
&\= 2H^{\r\lb}{}_{[\m} H_{\n] \r\lb} ~.
\end{split}
\eeq


Now, we compute the trace of $(\dd H)^{2,2} = \delb H^{2,1} + \del H^{1,2}$. Its useful to record the form in components:
$$
(\dd H)_{\m\n\sb\rb}\= \left( \nabla_\sb H_{\m\n\rb} -  \nabla_\rb H_{\m\n\sb} \right)  + \left( \nabla_\m H_{\n\sb\rb} - \nabla_\n H_{\m\sb\rb} \right) ~.
$$
The trace is
\beq\label{eq:dHtrmixed}
\begin{split}
 (\dd H)_{\n}{}^\n{}_{\m\sb} &\=  \nabla_\sb H_\m{}^\n{}_{\n}    -\nabla_\m H_\sb{}^\n{}_{\n} -  \nabla^\n H_{\n\m\sb} + \nabla^\nb H_{\nb\m\sb}  ~.
\end{split}
\eeq 
The dilatino equation \eqref{eq:dilatino2} and \eqref{eq:trP} implies
\beq\notag
\begin{split}
 \nabla_\sb H_\m{}^\r{}_\r   &\= 2\nabla_\sb \nabla_\m \Phi + 3\ap \nabla_\sb P_\m{}^\r{}_\r \= \nabla_\sb \nabla_\m\left(2\hat  \Phi + \frac{\ap^2}{8}  \left( \tr|F|^2 - \tr |R|^2 \right)\right)~,\\
\end{split}
\eeq
with $\nabla_\m H_\sb{}^\r{}_\r$ following by complex conjugation. 

Putting this into \eqref{eq:dHtrmixed}:
\beq\label{eq:dHtrmixed2}
\begin{split}
 (\dd H)_{\n}{}^\n{}_{\m\sb} &\=4\nabla_\m \nabla_\sb \hat\Phi +  \cO(\ap^2)~.
\end{split}
\eeq
Its also useful to trace the Bianchi identity \eqref{eq:Bianchi2}. Using $F^{0,2}=F_\m{}^\m= \cO(\ap^2)$ with $R^\H{}^{0,2} = R^\H{}_\m{}^\m=\cO(\ap)$ and \eqref{eq:gaugino3}, 
\beq\label{eq:trdHtrF}
\begin{split}
  (\dd H)_{\n}{}^\n{}\=  (\dd H)_{\n}{}^\n{}_{\m\sb} \dd x^{\m\sb}  &\= \frac{\ap}{2} (\tr F^\n\w F_{\n} - \tr R^\H{}^\n \w R^\H_\n  )  + \cO(\ap^2)~,
\end{split}
\eeq
and so 
\beq\notag
\nabla^\sb (\dd H)_\n{}^\n{}_{\m\sb} \= \frac{\ap}{4} \nabla_\m (\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2  ) + \cO(\ap^2) ~.
\eeq


Finally, we compute $\dd P$. Using \eqref{eq:apP}, we wind up with 
\beq\label{eq:dP}
\begin{split}
\ap \dd P
 &\=\frac{\ap^2}{4}   \del\delb\Big\{ \tr (F^\m \w F_\m) -\tr (R^\H{}^\m \w R^{\H}{}_\m)\Big\} \\
 &\=  \frac{\ap}{2} \nabla_\m \nabla_\sb  (\dd H)_\n{}^\n \dd x^{\m\sb} + \cO(\ap^3)  \= - 2\ap \nabla_\sb \nabla_\m ( \nabla_\n \nabla_\tb  \hat \Phi) \dd x^{\m\n\sb\tb}  \\
 &  \=0~.
\end{split}
\eeq
where we use   \eqref{eq:dHtrmixed2}-\eqref{eq:trdHtrF} to introduce the trace of $\dd H$ and its relation to the  dilaton and as $\nabla \Phi = \cO(\ap)$, we find $\delb^2 = 0$.


As a check of previous results, we compute the trace of $\dd P$ in two ways either from 
$$
(\dd P)_{\m\n\rb\sb}\= \left( \nabla_\rb P_{\m\n\sb}- \nabla_\sb P_{\m\n\rb}  \right)  + \left( \nabla_\m P_{\n\rb\sb} - \nabla_\n P_{\m\rb\sb} \right) ~,
$$
and using \eqref{eq:trP}, \eqref{eq:divP21b}; or using first line of \eqref{eq:dP}, the result is the same
\beq\label{eq:trdP}
\begin{split}
\ap( \dd P)_\n{}^\n &\=  \frac{\ap^2}{4}  \nabla^\n \nabla_{\n} \, \tr (F^\r\w F_\r ) - (F\leftrightarrow R^\H)~,
\end{split}
\eeq
and is  $\ap^3$. 



\subsubsection{Double holomorphic direction}
We put $m=\m$ in \eqref{eq:integrab5}, which will correspond to the graviton equation of motion in the direction of two holomorphic coordinates:
\beq\label{eq:integrab5b}
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{\m\n} +   2\nabla_\m \nabla_\n \Phi - \qrt  H_{\m ab}  H_{\n}{}^{ab} + \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  F_\m{}^a F_{\n a} -  \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  R^\H{}_\m{}^a R^\H{}_{\n a} \right) \g^\n\eta  \\[6pt]
& +   \half e^{2\Phi} \nabla^p (e^{-2\Phi}  H_{p\m\n} ) \g^\n \eta \=   \frac{\ap}{4} \tr\left(       \frac{1}{4} (R^\H \w R^\H)_{\m\n \a\bb}  \g^{\n \a\bb} \eta - \left(R^\H{}_\m{}^a R^\H{}_{\n a} \right) \g^\n\eta  \right)   \\[7pt]
  &\qquad\qquad\qquad  \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad
-\ap  \nabla_\m P^\a{}_{\a\n}  \g^{ \n} \eta~.
\end{split}\raisetag{2.5cm}
\eeq
 We have used that $P^{3,0}=\cO(\ap^3)$ and $\dd P = \cO(\ap^3)$ in \eqref{eq:P30} and \eqref{eq:dP} respectively. 

The first line is the graviton equation of motion and the $H$ equation of motion. 
The second line we deal with via
\beq\notag
\begin{split}
  \tr  \left(R^\H{}_\m{}^a R^\H{}_{\n a} \right) \g^\n\eta  -  \frac{1}{4}\Big(\tr (R^\H \w R^\H)_{\m\n \a\bb} \Big) \g^{\n \a\bb} \eta  \=  \tr (R^\H_{\m\n} R^\H{}_\a{}^\a )\g^\n \= \cO(\ap^2) ~.
\end{split}
\eeq
We have used that $\tr R^\H \w R^\H$ has no $(4,0)$ component, that 
$$
\half \tr (R^\H\w R^\H)_{\m\n\a}{}^\a = \tr( R^\H{}_{\m\n}  R^\H{}_{\a}{}^\a -  R^\H{}_{\m}{}^{\ab} R^\H{}_{\n}{}_\ab -  R^\H{}_{\m}{}^\a R^\H{}_{\a \n}) ~,
$$
as well as $R^\H{}^{0,2} = \cO(\ap)$ from \eqref{eq:RH02} and $R^\H_\a{}^\a = \cO(\ap)$ from \eqref{eq:RhdH}.

 The last line of \eqref{eq:integrab5b} gives a modification to $\Phi$  after using  \eqref{eq:dPdivPgradtrP}. Finally, we can use \eqref{eq:Hcalib}, that H equations of motion follows from supersymmetry. 


Hence, \eqref{eq:integrab5b} simplifies to
\beq\label{eq:integrab6}
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{\m\n} +   2\nabla_\m \nabla_\n \hat \Phi - \qrt  H_{\m ab}  H_{\n}{}^{ab} + \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  F_\m{}^a F_{\n a} -  \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  R^\H{}_\m{}^a R^\H{}_{\n a} \right) \g^\n\eta\= 0~,  \\[5pt]
 \end{split}
\eeq 
where recall $\hat\Phi = \Phi + \frac{\ap^2}{32} (\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)$.
We find the double holomorphic component of integrability of supersymmetry vanishes if and only if the corresponding component of the graviton equations of motion hold with a modified dilaton. We return to the dilaton issue later.

\subsubsection{Mixed component}

We now study $m=\mb$ in \eqref{eq:integrab5}:
\beq\label{eq:integrab5c}
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{\mb\n} +   2\nabla_\mb \nabla_\n \Phi - \qrt  H_{\mb ab}  H_{\n}{}^{ab} + \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  F_\mb{}^a F_{\n a} -  \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  R^\H{}_\mb{}^a R^\H{}_{\n a} \right) \g^\n\eta   \\[7pt]
&\quad+   \half e^{2\Phi} \nabla^p (e^{-2\Phi}  H_{p\mb \n} ) \g^\n \eta\=    \frac{\ap}{4} \tr \left( R^\H{}_{\mb\n}  R^\H{}_{\a}{}^\a  \right)\g^\n   - \frac{\ap}{6}   
  \nabla^\LC_\mb  P_{nab}    \g^{nab} \eta~.
\raisetag{1cm}
\end{split}
\eeq
We have used that $P^{3,0}=\cO(\ap^3)$ and $\dd P = \cO(\ap^3)$ in \eqref{eq:P30} and \eqref{eq:dP} respectively; $ \tr (R^\H \w R^\H)_{\mb\n \a\b} = \cO(\ap^2)$ and
$$
\tr ( R^\H{}_\mb{}^\ab R^\H{}_{\n \ab} )\g^\n\eta -  \frac{1}{4}\Big(\tr (R^\H \w R^\H)_{\mb\n \a\bb} \Big) \g^{\n \a\bb} \eta \=\! - \tr \left( R^\H{}_{\mb\n}  R^\H{}_{\a}{}^\a  \right) + \cO(\ap^2)~.
$$
As before the first line becomes the graviton equation of motion after using that the $H$ equation of motion is satisfied. The second line is related to the the trace of $R^\H$, followed by additional terms which we now study. 

Combining \eqref{eq:RhdH} and \eqref{eq:dHtrmixed2}
\beq\label{eq:traceRHhessian}
g^{\m\nb} R^\H{}_{\m\nb\a\bb}  \=\! \half (\dd H)_\m{}^\m{}_{\a\bb} \= - 2 \nabla_\a  \nabla_\bb \Phi + \cO(\ap^2)~.
\eeq

 The final term is
\beq\notag
\begin{split}
- \frac{\ap}{6}  \nabla^\LC_\mb  P_{nab}    \g^{nab} \eta &\= \ap        \nabla^\LC_\mb  P^\a{}_{\a\n}  \g^\n  \eta \=\!- \frac{\ap^2}{16}  \nabla_\mb\nabla_\n \left( \tr |F|^2- \tr |R|^2 \right) ~,\\
\end{split}
\eeq
where  \eqref{eq:trP} for trace of $P$. 


Putting it together, using the $H$ equation of motion, we find 
\beq\label{eq:integrab7}
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{\mb\n} +   2\nabla_\mb \nabla_\n \hat \Phi - \qrt  H_{\mb ab}  H_{\n}{}^{ab} + \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  F_\mb{}^a F_{\n a} -  \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  R^\H{}_\mb{}^a R^\H{}_{\n a} \right) \g^\n\eta\\ 
&\quad\= -   \ap\left(  R^\Ch{}_{\n\mb}{}^{\a\bb}  \left(  \nabla_\a \nabla_\bb  \hat \Phi    \right)  \right) \g^\n\eta~.
\end{split}
\eeq 

\subsection{Summary}
If we assume there is a smooth $\ap\to 0$ limit, we have shown in earlier sections that supersymmetry implies $H=\cO(\ap)$, $\nabla \Phi = \cO(\ap)$; that the $H$ and $F$ equation of motion holds to $\ap^2$ if the background is supersymmetric and furthermore that  supersymmetry is integrable if and only if
\beq\notag
\begin{split}
& \left(\Ric_{\m\n} +   2\nabla_\m \nabla_\n \hat \Phi - \qrt  H_{\m ab}  H_{\n}{}^{ab} + \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  F_\m{}^a F_{\n a} -  \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  R^\H{}_\m{}^a R^\H{}_{\n a} \right) \g^\n\eta\= 0~,  \\[5pt]
& \left(\Ric_{\mb\n} +   2\nabla_\mb \nabla_\n \hat \Phi - \qrt  H_{\mb ab}  H_{\n}{}^{ab} + \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  F_\mb{}^a F_{\n a} -  \frac{\ap}{4}\tr  R^\H{}_\mb{}^a R^\H{}_{\n a} \right) \g^\n\eta\\ 
&\qquad \qquad \qquad\=\! -   \ap\left(  R^\Ch{}_{\n\mb}{}^{\a\bb}  \left(  \nabla_\a \nabla_\bb  \hat \Phi    \right)  \right) \g^\n\eta~,
\end{split}
\eeq 
where recall $\hat\Phi = \Phi + \frac{\ap^2}{32} (\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)$. These are  the graviton equations of motion hold with the modified dilaton $\hat \Phi$, with a source term proportional to the hessian of the dilaton.

Suppose the graviton equation of motion holds. Then supersymmetry is integrable if \\ $\nabla_m\nabla_n (\tr|F|^2 - \tr|R^\H|^2) = \cO(\ap)$ and $\nabla_m \nabla_n \Phi = \cO(\ap^2)$. We show in the next section that if the latter holds, then it implies the former. Furthermore, the hessian of the dilaton on $SU(3)$ is pure gauge with respect to diffeomorphisms, up to and including $\ap^2$ and there is a gauge choice in which it vanishes \cite{Witten:1986kg,Anguelova:2010ed}. We analyse this from a mathematical perspective in  \sref{s:gaugefixing}. In that case, $\nabla \Phi = \nabla \hat \Phi$ and integrability takes a particularly simple form: integrability of supersymmetry if and only if the graviton equations of motion hold. 

{\bf Remarks:} First, the dilaton equations of motion do not appear in this analysis.  Second,  no instanton condition required. 


A corollary of \eqref{eq:traceRHhessian} is that the trace of $R^\H$ is pure gauge to $\cO(\ap)$  and vanishes in the natural gauge choice described above. However, $R^{\H\,0,2}$ does not vanish, see \eqref{eq:RH02}, even in this gauge. 


 The results in this section are important, and in the next section we rederive them a second way using just tensor equations and hermitian geometry. 






 \section{Equations of motion from hermitian geometry}
\label{s:eomgeometry}
\subsection{Statement of results}

In Section \ref{sec:susyalg}, we started from constraints on a spinor $\eta$ on a compact 6-manifold $X$ obtained by setting fermionic supersymmetry variations to zero, and from these equations on spinors we found that the compact manifold $X$ is a complex manifold at $\ap^2$ and we derived equations constraining its geometry.

This section tells a self-contained mathematical story based on our findings in earlier sections. The setup is as follows:


 \begin{enumerate}
 \item Let $X$ be a compact manifold of dimension 6. Let $(g_\ap, J_\ap, \Omega_\ap, F_\ap)$ be a family of tensors on $X$ smoothly varying with a parameter $\ap \in [0,\epsilon)$ for $\epsilon>0$ fixed, where $g_\ap$ is a metric tensor, $J_\ap$ is a complex structure, $\Omega_\ap$ is a holomorphic volume form, and $F_\ap$ is the curvature of a connection on a vector bundle $E \rightarrow X$. For example,
  \[
g_\ap \= g^{(0)} + \ap g^{(1)} + \ap^2 g^{(2)} + \dots~,
  \]
  and $g^{(0)}$ is a metric tensor.

\item Suppose $(g_\ap, J_\ap, \Omega_\ap, F_\ap)$ solves the following equations of complex geometry:
\begin{align}
  d  (\norm{\Omega}_g \omega^2) &= 0 \label{eq:holconfbal}\\
  F^{0,2} =F^{2,0} &\= 0~, \\
  F \wedge \omega^2 &\=  0 \label{eq:Fo2}~,\\
2 \ii \partial \bar{\partial} \omega + \frac{\ap}{4} [ \tr F \wedge F - \tr R^{\Ch} \wedge R^{\Ch} ] &\= 0 \label{eq:iddbo}~.
\end{align}
Here $\omega =g (J \cdot, \cdot)$ as usual.  Here and in what follows, we will often drop the subscript $\ap$ on tensors and simply write $g = g_{\ap}$, $\Omega=\Omega_\ap$, etc.

\item Define the 3-form $H_\ap$ and scalar function $\Phi_\ap$ by:
\begin{align}
  H &\= \ii (\partial-\bar{\partial})\omega + 2 \ap P~, \label{eq:truncatedH}\\[5pt]
  2 \ap P &\= \ap  \dd^\dagger (i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)~, \\
  \Phi &\=\! - \half  \log \norm{\Omega}_g -  \frac{\ap^2}{32} ({\rm Tr} \, |F|^2 -{\rm Tr} \, |R^\H|^2)~. \label{eq:truncatedPhi}
\end{align}
In previous sections, we showed that the true physical fields $g$, $H$, and $\Phi$, which receive $\ap$ corrections to all orders, are consistent with the truncated equations given here to 2-loops in the $\ap$-expansion. In particular, equations \eqref{eq:iddbo} and \eqref{eq:truncatedH} are consistent with the Bianchi identity $dH = \frac{\ap}{4} ( \tr FF - \tr R^\H R^\H)$ to 2-loops; see Section \ref{sec:ddbar-ap2}.
\end{enumerate}

We investigate the question of whether these equations of complex geometry imply the equations of motion to 2-loops (i.e. at order $\ap^2$), which we reprint here for ease of reference:
\begin{equation}\begin{split}
& \Ric_{mn}+ 2 \nabla_m \nabla_n\Phi - \frac{1}{4} H_{mab} H_n{}^{ab} +
\frac{\ap}{4} \Big( \tr F_{mp} F_n{}^p - R^\H{}_{mpab} R^\H{}_n{}^{pab} \Big)  \= \cO(\ap^3) ~,\\[6pt]
&\nabla^m(\ee^{-2\Phi} H_{mnp}) \= \cO(\ap^3) ~,\\[8pt]
&\ap \ccD^{-\,m} (\ee^{-2\Phi} F_{mn}) \= \cO(\ap^3) ~,\\[3pt]
&  R - 4(\nabla \Phi)^2 + 4 \nabla^2 \Phi - \half |H|^2 + \frac{\ap}{4} \big(\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2\big) \= \cO(\ap^3)~.\\[3pt]
\end{split}\raisetag{45pt} \label{EOM-ap3} \end{equation}

Here $R^\H$ is defined by $\Gamma_m^\H = \Gamma^\LC_m + \half H_m$ and $\ccD^-$ acts as $\Gamma_m^- = \Gamma^\LC_m - \half H_m$ on tangent bundle indices. In the current section, we simply write $\nabla$ instead of $\nabla^{\LC}$ for the Levi-Civita connection for ease of notation.

Section \ref{sec:integrability} analyzed the relation between the supersymmetry constraints on spinors and the equations of motion. The current section gives a second perspective by working only at the level of complex geometry and making no mention of spinors. The precise statement of the result in this section is:

\begin{prop} \label{prop:herm2eom}
  Under the setup assumptions denoted above by 1, 2, and 3, there exists a 1-parameter family of diffeomorphisms $\varphi_\ap$ with $\varphi_0=id$ such that $(\check{g}_\ap, \check{\Phi}_\ap, \check{H}_\ap, \check{F}_\ap)$ solve the equations of motion \eqref{EOM-ap3} where check denotes the pullback by $\varphi_\ap$. For example, $\check{g}_\ap = \varphi_\ap^* g_\ap$.
\end{prop}

We make three remarks on this Proposition:

\begin{itemize}
\item If we wish to be consistent with the equations of motion at first order in $\ap$, which amounts to replacing $\cO(\ap^3)$ with $\cO(\ap^2)$ on the right-hand side of \eqref{EOM-ap3}, then it is not needed to pullback by diffeomorphisms $\varphi_\ap$. In that case, the equations of motion are a direct consequence of assumptions 1, 2, and 3. It is at order $\ap^2$ that there are extra terms which can be removed by gauge fixing along the flow of $\ap$ via $\varphi_\ap$.

\item Some other studies of heterotic supersymmetry and the equations of motion add the extra hypothesis that $R$ is an instanton e.g. \cite{delaOssa:2014msa, Martelli:2010jx, garcia2023, Ivanov:2009rh, LopesCardoso:2003dvb}, but this condition does not play a role in the proof of Proposition \ref{prop:herm2eom}, and is not compatible with supersymmetry at $\ap^2$ as explained by Bergshoeff-de Roo \cite{Bergshoeff:1989de}.

  \item Sequences solving assumptions 1 and 2 have been constructed by various methods; see \cite{liyau2005, andreas2012, Melnikov:2014ywa, collins2022}.

  \end{itemize}

Lastly, a comment about notation. In this section, we will distinguish equality up to error terms of order $\cO(\ap^3)$ and exact equality. We use the notation
  \beq\notag
A \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} B \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad A \= B + \cO(\ap^3)~.
  \eeq
  This distinction between $=$ and $\overset{\ \ap^2}{=}$ here is only included as a mathematical exercise; in all other sections of the paper this was understood by context and suppressed for ease of notation. 




\subsection{Preliminaries}

We proved in Section \ref{sec:orderzero} that the setup denoted above by 1, 2 and 3 implies that $H = \cO(\ap)$ and $\partial \Phi = \cO(\ap)$. We will use this frequently in the following subsections.
  \beq\notag
H \= \cO(\ap), \quad T \= \cO(\ap), \quad \partial \Phi = \cO(\ap), \quad \ap P \= \cO(\ap^2)~.
  \eeq
We will use the notation
\beq\notag
\hat{\Phi} \= \Phi + \frac{\ap^2}{32} ( \tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2)~.
\eeq
The conformally balanced equation \eqref{eq:holconfbal} and the definition of $\Phi$ \eqref{eq:truncatedPhi} give via \eqref{def:confbal} the identity
\beq \label{eq:trT-improved}
\dd ( e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} \omega^2) \=0~, \quad T_\mu{}^\mu{}_\lambda \=\! - 2 \partial_\lambda \hat{\Phi}~, \quad T_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\lambda}} \=  2 \partial_{\bar{\lambda}} \hat{\Phi}~.
\eeq
In the remainder of this subsection we compare the difference $\hat{\Phi} - \Phi$ and prove various identities for future reference along the way.

\begin{itemize}
\item  The first identity we prove is:
  \begin{align}
    & \ \frac{\ap}{8} (\tr F \wedge F - \tr R^\Ch \wedge R^\Ch)_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\beta} \alpha} \nonumber\\
    &\overset{\ \ap^2}{=} -  \frac{\ap}{4} \tr \bigg[ F_{\alpha \bar{\mu}} F_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{\bar{\mu}} -R^\H{}_{\alpha \bar{\mu}} R^\H{}_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{\bar{\mu}} \bigg] - \frac{\ap}{4} (\Lambda_\omega \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)^m{}_n R^\Ch{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}}{}^n{}_m~.\label{eq:trFFRR}
  \end{align}
 To show \eqref{eq:trFFRR}, we start with
\beq\notag
    (\tr F \wedge F)_{\mu \bar{\nu} \alpha \bar{\beta}} \= 2 \tr F_{\mu \bar{\nu}} F_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} - 2 \tr F_{\mu \bar{\beta}} F_{\alpha \bar{\nu}}~,
 \eeq
  and so since $g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} F_{\mu \bar{\nu}}=0$ then
   \beq
(\tr F \wedge F)_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} \=\! - 2 \tr F_{\mu \bar{\beta}} F_{\alpha}{}^\mu~.
\eeq
Similarly,
  \beq\notag
\ap (\tr R^\Ch \wedge R^\Ch)_{\mu}{}^\mu{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} \= 2 \ap g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} \tr R^\Ch{}_{\mu \bar{\nu}} R^\Ch{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} - 2 \ap g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} \tr R^\Ch{}_{\mu \bar{\beta}} R^\Ch{}_{\alpha \bar{\nu}}~.
\eeq
Up to errors of order $\cO(\ap^3)$, we can convert the second term from Chern to Hull.
\beq\notag
- 2 \ap g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} \tr R^\Ch{}_{\mu \bar{\beta}} R^\Ch{}_{\alpha \bar{\nu}} \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} - 2 \ap \tr R^\H{}_{\mu \bar{\beta}} R^\H{}_{\alpha}{}^\mu
\eeq
Indeed, the explicit formulas in \eqref{defn:RH} show that $R^\H{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}}{}^{\bar{\gamma}}{}_{\lambda} = \cO(\ap)$ since $T = \cO(\ap)$ and 
 \beq\label{eq:RHRCh}
R^{\Tplus}{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}}{}^\lambda{}_\gamma = R^{\rm Ch}{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}}{}^\lambda{}_\gamma + T_\alpha{}^\lambda{}_{\bar{\sigma}} T_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{\bar{\sigma}}{}_\gamma~, \quad R^\H \= R^{\Tplus} + \cO(\ap^2)~.
\eeq
Thus
  \begin{align}
    & \ \frac{\ap}{8} (\tr F \wedge F - \tr R^\Ch \wedge R^\Ch)_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\beta} \alpha} \nonumber\\
    &\overset{\ \ap^2}{=} \!-  \frac{\ap}{4} \tr \bigg[ F_{\alpha \bar{\mu}} F_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{\bar{\mu}} -R^\H{}_{\alpha \bar{\mu}} R^\H{}_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{\bar{\mu}} \bigg]  - \frac{\ap}{4} g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} \tr R^\Ch{}_{\mu \bar{\nu}} R^\Ch{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} ~. \label{eq:trFFRR0}
  \end{align}
The deviation of Chern being an instanton for conformally balanced metrics was calculated in the appendix in equation \eqref{eq:R-inst}.
\beq \label{eq:badguy}
- \ap \tr g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} R^\Ch{}_{\mu \bar{\nu}}{}^\alpha{}_\beta \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} \ap (i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)_\mu{}^{\mu \alpha}{}_\beta~.
\eeq
This proves \eqref{eq:trFFRR}.

\item Equation \eqref{eq:trFFRR} involves $\Lambda_\omega \partial \bar{\partial} \omega$. We remark that
\beq
\Lambda_\omega d d^c \omega \=\! - \Lambda_\omega d^c d \omega = - d^c \Lambda_\omega d \omega + \cO(T)^2 ~,
\eeq
using the K\"aher identity $[\Lambda,d^c] = \cO(d\omega)$. Substituting  $\Lambda_\omega d \omega = 2 d \hat{\Phi}$ \eqref{eq:trT-improved} and $T = \cO(\ap)$ then leads to
\beq \label{eq:trddc-o}
\Lambda_\omega (i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega) \=2 \ii \partial \bar{\partial} \Phi + \cO(\ap^2)~.
\eeq

\item We can now compare $\hat{\Phi}$ and $\Phi$. First, trace \eqref{eq:trFFRR}:
\beq\notag
\ap^2 (\tr |F|^2 - \tr |R^\H|^2) \overset{\ \ap^2}{=}\! -\frac{\ap^2}{2} \Lambda_\omega^2 (\tr F \wedge F - \tr R^\Ch \wedge R^\Ch)~,
\eeq
where we used $\ap^2 \Lambda_\omega \partial \bar{\partial} \omega = \cO(\ap^3)$. The nonlinear constraint \eqref{eq:iddbo} for $i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega$ and definition of $\hat{\Phi}$ imply 
\beq\notag
\hat{\Phi}-\Phi \overset{\ \ap^2}{=}  \frac{\ap}{8} \Lambda^2_\omega (i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)~.
\eeq
We can now apply \eqref{eq:trddc-o} to conclude
\beq \label{eq:madhatter}
\hat{\Phi}-\Phi \overset{\ \ap^2}{=}  \frac{\ap}{4} \Lambda i \partial \bar{\partial} \Phi~.
\eeq

\end{itemize}


  \subsection{Yang-Mills equation}
  The argument at order $\ap^2$ for the divergence Yang-Mills equation derived from the Hermitian Yang-Mills equation over a conformally balanced geometry is well-known in the literature; see \cite{Gillard:2003jh}.
  \beq\notag
  \ccD^{-\,m} (\ee^{-2\Phi} F_{mn}) \= \cO(\ap^2)~.
  \eeq
  We outlined the derivation earlier in the file in Section \ref{sec:HFeom}. Here we simply note that the Hermitian-Yang-Mills equation $g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} F_{\mu \bar{\nu}} = 0$ does not imply the usual Yang-Mills equation $\nabla^m F_{mn} = 0$ when $g$ is non-K\"ahler, but rather the $F$-equation of motion above is the correct adaptation of the Yang-Mills equation.

\subsection{Divergence of the 3-form}
\label{s:HEOM}
In this subsection, we derive
\beq \label{eq:div-H}
\nabla^m(\ee^{-2\Phi} H_{mij}) \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~.
\eeq
We take the approach of \cite{Martelli:2010jx}. The starting point is the following observation due to \cite{Gauntlett:2003cy}: let $\omega$ be a conformally balanced metric on a complex manifold $X$ of dimension 3, so that $d (e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} \omega^2) = 0$. Then
\beq \label{eq:starT}
\star T \=\! - e^{2 \hat{\Phi}} d (e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} \omega)~, \quad d \star (e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} T) \= 0~.
\eeq
In our current setup, we solve $d (e^{-2 \hat{\Phi}} \omega^2) = 0$ by \eqref{eq:trT-improved}. To show \eqref{eq:starT}, we can first use the primitive decomposition to derive the general formula
\beq \label{eq:genstar}
\star \chi \= J \chi - J(\Lambda_\omega \chi) \wedge \omega~, \quad \chi \in \Omega^3(X)~.
\eeq
Here $J$ acts by differential forms by duality, so that for example in holomorphic coordinates $J dx = i dx$ and $J d \bar{x} = - i d \bar{x}$. Equation \eqref{eq:starT} then follows from \eqref{eq:genstar} and \eqref{eq:trT-improved}. From here, we can derive the $H$-equation of motion by expanding
\beq\notag
d \star (e^{-2 \Phi} H) \= d \star (e^{-2 \Phi} T) + 2 \ap d \star (e^{-2 \Phi} P)~.
\eeq
For the first term
\beq\notag
d \star (e^{-2 \Phi} T)  \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~,
\eeq
by \eqref{eq:starT} and using $\hat{\Phi}-\Phi = \cO(\ap^2)$ and $T = \cO(\ap)$. For the second term,
\beq\notag
2 \ap d \star (e^{-2 \Phi} P) \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~,
\eeq
since $\ap P = \cO(\ap^2)$, $\partial \Phi = \cO(\ap)$, and $d \star P = 0$ since $(d^\dagger)^2 = 0$.



\subsection{Einstein equation}
In this subsection, we show that if $\Phi = {\rm const} + \cO(\ap^2)$, then
\beq \label{eq:einstein}
\Ric_{mn}+ 2 \nabla_m \nabla_n \Phi - \frac{1}{4} H_{mpq} H_n{}^{pq}  +\frac{\ap}{4}  \tr F_{mp} F_n{}^p - \frac{\ap}{4}  \tr R^\H_{mp} R^\H_n{}^p \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~.
\eeq
We will pullback by a certain family of diffeomorphisms along the flow of $\ap$ to arrange that $\Phi$ is a constant plus quadratic terms in $\ap$. This will be done later in Section \ref{s:gaugefixing}. For now, we conclude that if $\Phi = {\rm const} + \cO(\ap^2)$, then the Einstein equation is satisfied to order $\ap^2$. In the detailed calculation below, we will see the extra terms that arise if $\Phi$ is not constant to this order.


\subsubsection{Double holomorphic directions}
We start by examining \eqref{eq:einstein} when $m,n$ are both holomorphic indices. The first step is to note the general formula for the Ricci curvature in hermitian geometry along two holomorphic indices:
\beq\notag
\Ric_{\alpha \beta} \= \half  (\nabla_\alpha^{\Ch} T_\mu{}^\mu{}_\beta + \nabla_\beta^{\Ch} T_\mu{}^\mu{}_\alpha) +\half   T_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} T^{\mu \bar{\nu}}{}_\beta~, \quad T \= \ii (\partial-\bar{\partial})\omega~.
\eeq
We now substitute \eqref{eq:trT-improved}.
\beq\notag
\Ric_{\alpha \beta} \=\! - \nabla_\alpha^{\Ch} \partial_\beta \hat{\Phi} - \nabla^{\Ch}_\beta \partial_\alpha \hat{\Phi} + \half  T_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} T^{\mu \bar{\nu}}{}_\beta~.
\eeq
Converting the Chern connection $\nabla^\Ch$ to the Levi-Civita connection $\nabla$ gives:
\beq\notag
\Ric_{\alpha \beta} + 2 \nabla_\alpha \nabla_\beta \hat{\Phi} \=   \half  T_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} T^{\mu \bar{\nu}}{}_\beta~.
\eeq
Next, we note that
\beq\notag
\frac{1}{4} H_{\alpha pq} H_\beta{}^{pq} \overset{\ \ap^2}{=}  \frac{1}{2} T_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} T_\beta{}^{\mu \bar{\nu}}~.
\eeq
Therefore
\beq \label{eq:einstein-ab}
\Ric_{\alpha \beta} + 2 \nabla_\alpha \nabla_\beta \hat{\Phi}  -\frac{1}{4} H_{\alpha pq} H_\beta{}^{pq} \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~.
\eeq
If we compare with \eqref{eq:einstein}, there is a term like
\beq \notag
\frac{\ap}{4}  \tr F_{\alpha m} F_\beta{}^m - \frac{\ap}{4}  \tr R^\H{}_{\alpha m} R^\H{}_\beta{}^m \=\!  - \frac{\ap}{4} g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} \tr R^\H{}_{\alpha \mu} R^\H{}_{\beta \bar{\nu}}  - \frac{\ap}{4}  g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} \tr R^\H{}_{\alpha \bar{\nu}} R^\H{}_{\beta \mu}~.
\eeq
This is order $\cO(\ap^3)$. Indeed, we can compute the curvature $\ap R^\H$ using the explicit formulas in \eqref{defn:RH}. The only non-zero component of $R^{\Tplus}{}_{\alpha \beta}{}^m{}_n$ is $R^{\Tplus}{}_{\alpha \beta}{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\nu}}$. The full Hull curvature $R^\H$ differs by $R^{\Tplus}$ by errors of $\cO(\ap^2)$. Hence
\beq \label{eq:RHjunk}
- \frac{\ap}{4}  \tr R^\H{}_{\alpha m} R^\H{}_\beta{}^m \overset{\ \ap^2}{=}\!  - \frac{\ap}{4} g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} \tr R^\H{}_{\alpha \mu}{}^\sigma{}_{\bar{\delta}} R^\H{}_{\beta \bar{\nu}}{}^{\bar{\delta}}{}_\sigma  - \frac{\ap}{4}  g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} \tr R^\H{}_{\alpha \bar{\nu}}{}^{\bar{\delta}}{}_\sigma R^\H{}_{\beta \mu}{}^\sigma{}_{\bar{\delta}}~.
\eeq
From here we can use
\beq\notag
R^{\Tplus}{}_{\mu \nu}{}^\alpha{}_{\bar{\beta}} \=\! - (\ii \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)_{\mu \nu}{}^\alpha{}_{\bar{\beta}}~, \quad R^{\Tplus}{}_{\beta \bar{\nu}}{}^{\bar{\delta}}{}_\sigma \= \nabla^{\Ch}_\beta H_{\bar{\nu}}{}^{\bar{\delta}}{}_\sigma~,
\eeq
to reduce \eqref{eq:RHjunk} to $\cO(\ap^3)$. Thus
\beq \notag
\Ric_{\alpha \beta}+ 2 \nabla_\alpha \nabla_\beta \hat{\Phi} - \frac{1}{4} H_{\alpha pq} H_\beta{}^{pq}  +\frac{\ap}{4}  \tr F_{\alpha m} F_\beta{}^m - \frac{\ap}{4}  \tr R^\H_{\alpha m} R^\H_\beta{}^m \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~.
\eeq
This is not quite the Einstein equation due to the difference between $\Phi$ and $\hat{\Phi}$. However, if $\Phi = {\rm const} + \cO(\ap^2)$, then by \eqref{eq:madhatter} we have $\partial \Phi \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} \partial \hat{\Phi}$ and the Einstein equation holds on double holomorphic indices.


\subsubsection{Mixed directions}

Next, we return to \eqref{eq:einstein} and consider the case when $m,n$ are mixed barred/unbarred indices. As before, the starting point is the general formula for the Ricci curvature in complex geometry, which relates the Ricci curvature in terms of the first Chern class representative $\tr R^{\rm Ch}$:
\begin{align} \label{eq:einstein1}
  \Ric_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} &\= R^{\rm Ch}{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}}{}^\mu{}_\mu + \frac{1}{2} (dT)_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\beta} \alpha} + \frac{1}{2} \nabla_\alpha^{\rm Ch} T_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\beta}} - \frac{1}{2} \nabla_{\bar{\beta}}^{\rm Ch} T_\mu{}^\mu{}_\alpha \\
  &+ \frac{1}{2} T_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} T^{\mu \bar{\nu}}{}_{\bar{\beta}} + \frac{1}{4} T_{\alpha \bar{\mu} \bar{\nu}} T^{\bar{\mu} \bar{\nu}}{}_{\bar{\beta}} - \frac{1}{2} T_\alpha{}^\mu{}_\beta T_\nu{}^\nu{}_\mu - \frac{1}{2} T_\alpha{}^{\bar{\mu}}{}_\beta T_\nu{}^\nu{}_{\bar{\mu}} ~.
\end{align}
This holds on any complex manifold and can be calculated directly from the conventions stated in the appendix. We can then rewrite \eqref{eq:einstein1} in our particular setup, where we substitute $R^{\rm Ch}{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}}{}^\mu{}_\mu= \partial_\alpha \partial_{\bar{\beta}} \log |\Omega|^2_\omega$, $\partial \log \norm{\Omega}_g = -2 \partial \hat{\Phi}$ and $T_\mu{}^\mu{}_i$ using \eqref{eq:trT-improved}.
\beq
\begin{split}
   \Ric_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} &\=\! -2 \partial_\alpha \partial_{\bar{\beta}} \hat{\Phi} + \frac{1}{2} (dT)_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\beta} \alpha}  \\
  &+ \frac{1}{2} T_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} T^{\mu \bar{\nu}}{}_{\bar{\beta}} + \frac{1}{4} T_{\alpha \bar{\mu} \bar{\nu}} T^{\bar{\mu} \bar{\nu}}{}_{\bar{\beta}} + T_\alpha{}^\mu{}_\beta \hat{\Phi}_\mu - T_\alpha{}^{\bar{\mu}}{}_\beta \hat{\Phi}_{\bar{\mu}}~ .
\end{split}
\eeq
The Hessian of the Levi-Civita connection in holomorphic coordinates is
\beq\notag
\nabla_\alpha \nabla_{\bar{\beta}} \hat{\Phi} \= \partial_\alpha \partial_{\bar{\beta}} \hat{\Phi} - \frac{1}{2} T_\alpha{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\beta}} \hat{\Phi}_\mu + \frac{1}{2} T_\alpha{}^{\bar{\mu}}{}_{\bar{\beta}} \hat{\Phi}_{\bar{\mu}}~,
\eeq
and so using equation \eqref{eq:iddbo} for $i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega$ gives
\beq \label{eq:road2einstein}
  \Ric_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} + 2 \nabla_\alpha \nabla_{\bar{\beta}} \Phi +\frac{1}{4} H_{\alpha mn} H_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{mn} - \frac{\ap}{8} (\tr F \wedge F - \tr R^\Ch \wedge R^\Ch)_\mu{}^\mu{}_{\bar{\beta} \alpha}  \= 0~.
  \eeq
We can now apply \eqref{eq:trFFRR} to the last term. Substituting \eqref{eq:trFFRR} into \eqref{eq:road2einstein} gives
\begin{align} 
  & \ \Ric_{\alpha \bar{\beta}} + 2 \nabla_\alpha \nabla_{\bar{\beta}} \hat{\Phi} +\frac{1}{4} H_{\alpha mn} H_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{mn} + \frac{\ap}{4} \bigg[ F_{\alpha \bar{\mu}} F_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{\bar{\mu}} -R^\H{}_{\alpha \bar{\mu}} R^\H{}_{\bar{\beta}}{}^{\bar{\mu}} \bigg] \nonumber\\
  & \overset{\ \ap^2}{=}\! - \frac{\ap}{4}  (\Lambda_\omega \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)^\lambda{}_\gamma R^\Ch{}_{\alpha \bar{\beta}}{}^\gamma{}_\lambda~ . \label{eq:troublesome}
\end{align}
We are left with two differences compared to the Einstein equation: there is a $\hat{\Phi}$ rather than $\Phi$, and there is a troublesome term on the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:troublesome}. At linear in $\ap$, these terms are negligeable and the equations of motion are satisfied.

The subtlety arises at order $\ap^2$. To remove these term, we will pullback by a certain family of diffeomorphisms along the flow of $\ap$ to arrange that $\Phi$ is a constant plus quadratic terms in $\ap$. This will be done later in Section \ref{s:gaugefixing}. For now, we conclude that if $\Phi = {\rm const} + \cO(\ap^2)$ then \eqref{eq:trddc-o} and \eqref{eq:madhatter} imply
\beq \label{eq:troublesome3}
\ap \Lambda \partial \bar{\partial} \omega \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~, \quad \hat{\Phi} - \Phi \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~.
\eeq
With this, equation \eqref{eq:troublesome} becomes the Einstein equation to order $\ap^2$.


\subsection{Dilaton equation}
\label{s:dilatoneom}
Finally, we must derive the dilaton equation
\beq \label{eq:dilaton}
 R - 4(\nabla \Phi)^2 + 4 \nabla^2 \Phi - \frac{1}{2} |H|^2 + \frac{\ap}{4} \tr |F|^2  -  \frac{\ap}{4} \tr |R^\H|^2 \overset{\ \ap^2}{\=} 0~.
 \eeq
Taking the trace of the Einstein equation \eqref{eq:einstein} does not immediately produce the dilaton equation. So instead we start by deriving an identity for $\nabla^2 \Phi$ by using special identities from conformally balanced metrics on complex manifolds. 

We start with the definition of the Laplacian in holomorphic coordinates:
\beq\notag
\nabla^2 \log \norm{\Omega}_g \= g^{\alpha \bar{\beta}} \partial_\alpha \partial_{\bar{\beta}} \log |\Omega|^2_\omega - T_\alpha{}^{\mu \alpha} \partial_\mu \log \norm{\Omega}_g + T_\alpha{}^{\bar{\mu} \alpha} \partial_{\bar{\mu}} \log \norm{\Omega}_g~.
\eeq
On a general conformally balanced geometry $(X,\omega,\Omega)$ satisfying $d (\norm{\Omega}_g \omega^2)=0$, we can apply \eqref{eq:RCH-hol} and \eqref{eq:R-inst}, hence there holds
\beq\notag
 \nabla^2 \log \norm{\Omega}_g \=\! - (\ii \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)_\alpha{}^{\alpha \mu}{}_\mu + T_{\alpha \mu \bar{\nu}} T^{\mu \bar{\nu} \alpha}- T_\alpha{}^{\mu \alpha} \partial_\mu \log \norm{\Omega}_g + T_\alpha{}^{\bar{\mu} \alpha} \partial_{\bar{\mu}} \log \norm{\Omega}_g~.
\eeq
In our setup, the $\log \norm{\Omega}_g$ is related to the dilaton via $\partial_i \log \norm{\Omega}_g = - 2 \partial_i \hat{\Phi}$ and our conventions are $|T|^2=\frac{1}{6} T_{mnp} T^{mnp}$.
\beq\notag
 \nabla^2 \hat{\Phi} \= \frac{1}{2} (i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)_\alpha{}^{\alpha \mu}{}_\mu - \half  |T|^2 + T_\alpha{}^{\mu \alpha} \partial_\mu \hat{\Phi} - T_\alpha{}^{\bar{\mu} \alpha} \partial_{\bar{\mu}} \hat{\Phi}~.
 \eeq
 Since $T_\mu{}^\mu{}_\lambda = - 2 \partial_\lambda \hat{\Phi}$, this becomes
 \beq\notag
 \nabla^2 \hat{\Phi} - \frac{1}{2} (\ii \partial \bar{\partial} \omega)_\alpha{}^{\alpha \mu}{}_\mu + \half  |T|^2 - 2 |\nabla \hat{\Phi}|^2\=0~.
 \eeq
We now substitute the equation \eqref{eq:iddbo} for $i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega$ and \eqref{eq:trFFRR0}, and replace $|T|^2$ with $|H|^2$ up to lower order terms.
 \beq \label{eq:dilaton1}
 \nabla^2 \hat{\Phi} - \frac{\ap}{8} \tr |F|^2 + \frac{\ap}{8} \tr |R^\H|^2+ \half  |H|^2 - 2 |\nabla \hat{\Phi}|^2 \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~.
\eeq
Here we used 
\beq\notag
\ap \tr (g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} R^{\Ch}{}_{\mu \bar{\nu}})^2 \= \cO(\ap^3)~,
\eeq
which follows from \eqref{eq:badguy}. We can now combine identity \eqref{eq:dilaton1} with the trace of the Einstein equation to obtain the dilaton equation. The trace of the Einstein equation \eqref{eq:troublesome} is
\beq \label{eq:dilaton2}
 R+ 2 \nabla^2 \hat{\Phi} -\frac{3}{2} |H|^2 + \frac{\ap}{2}  \tr |F|^2 - \frac{\ap}{2}  |R^\H|^2  \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~.
 \eeq
 Add twice equation \eqref{eq:dilaton1} to equation \eqref{eq:dilaton2} to obtain
 \beq\notag
  R+ 4 \nabla^2 \hat{\Phi} - 4 |\nabla \hat{\Phi}|^2 -\frac{1}{2} |H|^2 + \frac{\ap}{4}  \tr |F|^2 - \frac{\ap}{4}  |R^\H|^2 \overset{\ \ap^2}{=} 0~.
 \eeq
We conclude from \eqref{eq:troublesome3} that if $\Phi = {\rm const} + \cO(\ap^2)$, then $\hat{\Phi}$ can be replaced by $\Phi$ and the dilaton equation is satisfied to appropriate order.


 \subsection{Gauge fixing}
 \label{s:gaugefixing}
To complete the proof of Proposition \ref{prop:herm2eom}, it remains to show that after pulling back by diffeomorphisms flowing along $\ap$, we can assume that the dilaton function $\Phi$ is a constant plus terms of order $\cO(\ap^2)$. It is automatic that $\Phi = {\rm const} + \cO(\ap)$, and the task is to upgrade this by gauge fixing to $\Phi = {\rm const} + \cO(\ap^2)$. This procedure is well-known in the literature: this particular choice of gauge was used by Witten-Witten \cite{Witten:1986kg} and further refined by Anguelova-Quigley-Sethi \cite{Anguelova:2010ed} (see \citeSG for a summary). To end this section, we give here a self-contained presentation of these known results.

 Let $X$ be a compact manifold. Suppose we have a smooth family
\[
g_\ap \= g^{(0)}+\ap g^{(1)} + \ap^2 g^{(2)} + \dots~, \quad J_\ap \= J^{(0)} + \ap J^{(1)} + \ap^2 J^{(2)} + \dots~,
\]
parametrized by $\ap \in [0,1)$. Let $\varphi_\ap: X \rightarrow X$ be a 1-parameter family of diffeomorphisms which is the flow of a vector field $v$, so that
\[
\frac{\dd}{\dd \ap}  \bigg|_{\ap=0} \varphi_\ap \= v~, \quad \varphi_0 \= id~.
\]
The Lie derivative acting on a metric tensor $g^{(0)}$ satisfies
\[
\frac{\dd}{\dd \ap}  \bigg|_{\ap=0} (\varphi_\ap^* g^{(0)})_{ij} \= (L_v g^{(0)})_{ij} \= \nabla_i v_j + \nabla_j v_i~,
\]
with $\nabla$ the Levi-Civita connection of $g^{(0)}$. If $g$ also varies with $\ap$, the formula is
\[
\frac{\dd}{\dd \ap}  \bigg|_{\ap=0} (\varphi_\ap^* g_\ap)_{ij} \= L_v g^{(0)} + g^{(1)}~.
\]
It follows that altering $g$ by a moving reference frame leads to the expansion
\[
\varphi_\ap^* g_\ap \= g^{(0)} + \ap (g^{(1)} + L_v g^{(0)}) + \dots~.
\]
Suppose we also have a family of scalar functions $\Phi_\ap = \Phi^{(0)} + \ap \Phi^{(1)} + \dots$ which solve
\[
\partial_m \Phi_\ap \= \half  g^{ij} (\dd^c \omega)_{ijm} + \cO(\ap^2)~, \quad \Phi^{(0)} \= {\rm const}~.
  \]
  We want to choose the vector field $v$ so that $\varphi_\ap^* \Phi_\ap = \Phi^{(0)} + \cO(\ap^2)$. We can rewrite the dilaton equation as
  \[
\partial_{\rho} \Phi \= g^{\mu \bar{\nu}} (\nabla_\mu g_{\rho \bar{\nu}} - \nabla_\rho g_{\mu \bar{\nu}}) + \cO(\ap^2)~.
  \]
  These holomorphic coordinates move along the deformation, so we write the equation in terms of fixed real coordinates.
 \[
\partial_{k} \Phi \= g^{ij} \nabla_i g_{k j} - \half  g^{ij} \nabla_k g_{ij} + \cO(\ap^2)~.
  \]
  If we expand $g$ in $\ap$ and set $\partial \Phi^{(1)}$ to zero, we obtain the condition
  \beq \label{eq:gaugecond}
\nabla^{i} (g^{(1)})_{i k} - \half  \nabla_k (g^{(1)})_i{}^i \= 0~.
\eeq
  Witten-Witten \cite{Witten:1986kg} showed that there exists a vector field $v$ such that replacing
  \[
g^{(1)} \mapsto g^{(1)} + L_v g^{(0)}~,
  \]
  satisfies \eqref{eq:gaugecond}. Indeed, the equation for $v$ is
  \beq
\nabla^{i} \nabla_i v_k \=\! -\nabla^{i} (g^{(1)})_{i k} + \half  \nabla_k (g^{(1)})_i{}^i~,
  \eeq
  after using $[\nabla_i,\nabla_k] v^i = 0$ by Ricci-flatness. Since $L = \nabla^i \nabla_i$ is a self-adjoint elliptic operator acting on vector fields, by the theory of elliptic PDE on manifolds the equation is solvable if the right-hand side is $L^2$ orthogonal to $\ker L$.
  \beq
L v^k \=\! - \nabla_i \bigg( (g^{(1)})^{ik} + \frac{1}{2} g^{k i} (g^{(1)})_r{}^r \bigg)~.
  \eeq
Integration by parts shows that $\ker L = \{ w \in \Gamma(TX) : \nabla w = 0 \}$. Pairing the right-hand side with $w \in \ker L$ and integrating by parts gives zero, hence the equation for $v$ is solvable.

  In summary, $\check{\Phi} = \varphi_\ap^* \Phi$ satisfies
  \[
\partial \check{\Phi} \= 0 + \cO(\ap^2)~.
  \]
  In fact, \cite{Anguelova:2010ed} showed that $v$ can be taken to even satisfy
  \[
\partial \check{\Phi} \= 0 + \cO(\ap^3)~,
\]
but we will not need this stronger result in the present work.



 \section{Conclusions}


\subsection{Geometric flows}
There is a version of Ricci flow adapted to heterotic string compactifications. The idea is as follows: in practice, one can construct various explicit examples of complex geometries $(X,\Omega,\omega)$ and bundles $E \rightarrow X$ solving
\beq \label{eq:partialsusy}
F^{0,2} \=0~, \quad d \Omega \= 0~, \quad \dd (\norm{\Omega}_g \omega^2)\= 0~.
\eeq
There are in a sense intermediate supersymmetric configurations; the full supersymmetry equations \eqref{eq:Fo2}, \eqref{eq:iddbo} are not satisfied, as we are missing
\beq \label{eq:partialsusy2}
F \wedge \omega^2 \= 0~, \quad \ii \partial \bar{\partial} \omega \= \frac{\ap}{8} [\tr R^\Ch \wedge \tr R^\Ch - \tr F \wedge F ]~.
\eeq
The method then is to start with concrete examples solving the partial constraints \eqref{eq:partialsusy} and deform them along a flow which seeks to reach an on-shell configuration solving the full equations of motion. The anomaly flow, introduced in \cite{Phong2018,Phong2018B}, is a type of Ricci flow which does this: it is a flow that remains in the class of complex geometries solving \eqref{eq:partialsusy} and whose fixed-points solve \eqref{eq:partialsusy2}. The flow equations are:
\begin{align}
  \partial_t ( \norm{\Omega}_{g(t)}  \omega(t)^2) &\= \ii \partial \bar{\partial} \omega(t) - \frac{\ap}{8} ( \tr R^{\Ch} \wedge R^\Ch - \tr F_{h(t)} \wedge F_{h(t)})~,\\
  h^{-1} \partial_t h &\=\! - \ii \Lambda_{\omega(t)} F_{h(t)}~,
\end{align}
where the pair $(\omega(t),h(t))$ is flowing, and the holomorphic volume form $\Omega$ on $X$ and complex structure on $E$ remains fixed. Here $h(t)$ is a hermitian metric on the holomorphic bundle $E$ with Chern curvature $F_h = \bar{\partial}( h^{-1} \partial h)$ and its flow is given by the Donaldson heat flow \cite{0529.53018}. The initial metric $\omega_0$ solves $d ( \norm{\Omega}_{g(0)} \omega(0)^2)=0$ and the equations are such that $d ( \norm{\Omega}_{g(t)} \omega(t)^2)=0$ at all times. As the flow is nonlinear, there is a requirement on the scale: $|\ap R_{g(t)}| \ll 1 $ must be satisfied for the flow to be mathematically well-defined \cite{Phong2018, 2408.15514}. Explicit examples of this flow can be tested on $T^4$ fibrations over Riemann surfaces \cite{Fei2021} and $T^2$ fibrations over $K3$ surfaces \cite{Phong2018-sc}. We note that there is also another version of Ricci flow \cite{mariomolinastreets2024} adapted to heterotic string theory without the gravitational term $\tr R \wedge R$.

If the flow converges as $t \rightarrow \infty$, then limiting fixed point configurations solve \eqref{eq:partialsusy} and \eqref{eq:partialsusy2}. Our current work has demonstrated that these equations of complex geometry are consistent with supersymmetry at $\ap^2$ and solve the equations of motion to 2-loops after possibly gauge-fixing, where the fields $H$ and $\Phi$ are obtained from the complex geometry via \eqref{eq:truncatedH} and \eqref{eq:truncatedPhi}. It is unclear how the anomaly flow interacts with the gauge fixing condition $\Phi = {\rm const} +\cO(\ap^2)$. For the string theoretic study of the anomaly flow at 1-loop, see \cite{Ashmore2024}. 

From the mathematical perspective, the major question on the anomaly flow is to understand the class of initial data which leads to long-time existence and convergence; specific explicit examples have been constructed with a wide range of behaviour including finite-time divergence \cite{Fei2021} as well as infinite-time convergence \cite{Phong2018-sc}. Regardless of the behavior of this particular flow, an outstanding question is to determine when an intermediate configuration solving \eqref{eq:partialsusy} can be deformed to solve the full system including \eqref{eq:partialsusy2}; the significance of this problem in pure mathematics and differential geometry has been underscored by S.-T. Yau \cite{liyau2005, Fu:2006vj}. 

\subsection{Torsional \texorpdfstring{$K3$}{K3}  solutions}
Let us comment on the assumption of a smooth $\ap \to 0$ limiting metric, which is used throughout our analysis. There are in fact examples in string theory (and not  supergravity) that do not satisfy this assumption. But if this $\ap \to 0$ limit does not produce genuine limiting fields on the threefold, such solutions do not have sigma model descriptions and must be described by other methods such as duality. 

Nonetheless, the example of a $T^2$ fibration over a $K3$ receives interest and so we comment very briefly on the set-up. The Calabi-Yau threefold $X$ is a $T^2$ fibered over a $K3$ base $S$, and there are many works on its analysis in string theory and pure mathematics \cite{Dasgupta:1999ss, Fu:2006vj, Becker:2006et, Becker:2009df,Melnikov:2014ywa,Garcia-Fernandez:2018qcl}. In particular, Fu-Yau \cite{Fu:2006vj} constructed solutions to the nonlinear constraint \eqref{eq:iddbo} for $i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega$ over these geometries.

Let $X$ be the total space of a $U(1) \times U(1)$ principal bundle over a $K3$ surface $S$. Let $(\o_S,\O_S)$ be a Calabi-Yau structure on $S$. The hermitian metric and holomorphic volume form on the threefold $X$ are:
\beq\label{eq:K3T2herm}
\o \= e^{2\phi} \o_S + \frac{\ii a}{2}\, \ITh \w  \IThb~, \quad \O_X \= \sqrt{a}\, \O_S \w \ITh~,
\eeq
where $a$ is a constant denoting the volume of the torus. Here $\phi: S \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is a function on the base and $\ITheta = \ITheta^1 + \ii \ITheta^2$ and $\ITheta^i$ is a connection 1-form on each $U(1)$-bundle factor. Flux quantization has the implication that $a$ is a multiple of $2\pi \ap$ \cite{Melnikov:2014ywa}. This setup is such that the metric and Hermitian form on the threefold are not well-defined in the large-radius (i.e., $\ap \to 0$) limit as the geometry collapses down to the base $(S, e^{2 \phi} \o_S)$.

If the supergravity and supersymmetry equations studied here are to approximate the string theory coming from the sigma model, one must have a smooth $\ap \to 0$ limit. As a result, these backgrounds do not correspond to weakly coupled sigma models—linear or non-linear—that flow to conformal field theories. Instead, they are believed to be defined only indirectly, through dualities with type IIB string theory or M-theory. Consequently, without a sigma model to rely on, further insight is required to develop the $\ap^2$-level analysis to these geometries, and we leave this open for future investigation.


\subsection{Conclusions}
Bergshoeff-de Roo \citeBdR constructed the supersymmetry algebra for the heterotic string and the action it preserves up to and including the $\ap^2$ corrections. Starting from these equations, we analyze the resulting geometry on the compact six-dimensional manifold $X$. We find that $X$ is a complex manifold with $SU(3)$ holonomy with respect to the Bismut connection, and that $X$ is a non-K\"ahler Calabi-Yau threefold with conformally balanced metric $\omega$ satisfying a nonlinear constraint on $i \partial \bar{\partial} \omega$.

Furthermore, we find the following:

(a) Supersymmetry is integrable if and only a modified graviton equation of motion holds. The modification turns out to be pure gauge with respect to diffeomorphisms. In the gauge of constant dilaton considered in \citeAQS, the algebra is integrable on-shell without additional constraints.

(b) We observe that $\ap$-order corrections modify the relations between the torsion, the three-form $H$, and the hermitian form.
\beq\label{eq:HP2}
H\= \ii (\del-\delb)\o +2 \ap P~, \qquad P \=\!-\frac{1}{4} e^{2\Phi} \nabla^{\Bi\,\dag} (e^{-2\Phi} \dd H)~,
\eeq
with $H$ satisfying
$$
\dd H \= \frac{\ap}{4} \tr F\w F -\frac{\ap}{4}  \tr R^\H \w R^\H~.
 $$
 Here $R^\H$ is the field strength for $\Th^\H = \Th^\LC + \half H$, and in constant dilaton gauge, we show:
 $$
 R^{\H\,0,2}{\,}^\sb{}_\r \= \dd x^{\mb\nb} \nabla^\Ch_\mb H_\nb{}^\sb{}_\r \= \cO(\ap)~, \quad R^\H{}_\m{}^\m \= \cO(\ap^2)~.
 $$
 So while $R^\H$ is traceless, it is not an instanton beyond leading order. Outside constant dilaton gauge, the trace of $R^\H$ is not zero and is in fact proportional to the hessian of the dilaton.

 (c) The supersymmetry equations rewritten in terms of the holomorphic structure on the manifold $X$ are given by \eqref{eq:partialsusy} and \eqref{eq:partialsusy2}. We find that these do not receive corrections from first order in $\ap$ to second order $\ap^2$, and hence are protected from $\ap$ corrections at this level. Indeed, the zeroth order geometry is K\"ahler Calabi-Yau, while the first and second order non-K\"ahler complex geometries match the equations from Strominger's first order analysis \cite{Strominger:1986uh}. Assuming \eqref{eq:partialsusy} and \eqref{eq:partialsusy2}, we derive the equations of motion for the $H$-flux and gauge field strength $F$, and we derive the graviton and dilaton equations of motion after fixing to constant dilaton gauge by applying a sequence of diffeomorphisms flowing along $\ap$. 






It is natural to ask how the the quantum corrections at order $\ap^2$ modify equations governing small deformations. These equations derive from  studying small deformations of equation such as the Bianchi identity combined with \eqref{eq:HP2}.  Of particular interest is how quantum corrections modify the moduli space metric and its associated \K potential governing the moduli and matter field dynamics, as constructed in \cite{Candelas:2016usb,McOrist:2016cfl,McOrist:2019mxh}. It would be of interest to further investigate the associated universal geometric structures  \cite{Candelas:2018lib,McOrist:2024glz} and their capacity to encode the effects of $\ap$-corrections on the background geometry. 

 The conditions that derive from an action functional (labelled the superpotential see e.g. \cite{LopesCardoso:2003dvb,delaOssa:2015maa,McOrist:2016cfl,Ashmore:2018ybe})
 $$
 W \= \int \O \w (H+\ii \dd \o)~,
 $$
  include $\dd \O = 0$, $F^{0,2} = 0$, $H=\ii (\del-\delb)\o$ and $R^{0,2} = 0$~. While $W$ captures a subset of the first order $\ap$ supersymmetry equations, two of these equations are  modified at $\ap^2$, and so it suggests $W$ receives corrections.   
  Investigating these issues, and how the finite deformations of $W$ described in \cite{Ashmore:2018ybe} are affected, would be interesting to pursue as future work. 



It would also be of interest to investigate the structure and implications of the $\ap^2$ supersymmetry algebra in the context of compactifications on $\text{G}_2$-- and $\text{Spin}(7)$--holonomy manifolds, as well as on special geometries such as $\text{K3} \times T^2$, which preserve extended supersymmetry. For $\text{G}_2$ and $\mathrm{Spin}(7)$ backgrounds, the geometry is governed by a closed (or co-closed) defining form—either the associative 3-form in the case of $\text{G}_2$, or the Cayley 4-form in the $\text{Spin}(7)$ case—leading to nontrivial torsion classes when fluxes are introduced. In such settings, the preservation of supersymmetry often requires gauge fields to satisfy an instanton condition, i.e., that their curvature lies in a specific subbundle of two-forms determined by the holonomy. Understanding how this condition is modified in the presence of $\ap$-corrections, and how it is reflected in the structure of the BdR algebra, is an important  question for future work.






