\date{}

\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Online advertising systems have recently transitioned to autobidding, enabling advertisers to delegate bidding decisions to automated agents. Each advertiser directs their agent to optimize a valuation-dependent objective function subject to \emph{return-on-investment (ROI)} or \emph{budget  constraints}.  Given their relevance, there has been a surge in literature studying the \emph{liquid welfare price of anarchy (POA)} of fundamental auction formats in autobidding, among which \emph{simultaneous first-price auctions (FPA)}. These models capture a large range of heterogeneous agent behaviors, requiring advanced proofs to derive tight POA bounds. Recently, Deng et al. (NeurIPS 2024) showed that the POA of FPA for mixed autobidders (i.e., value and utility maximizers) under ROI is 2.18 for additive valuations. 

We extend the smoothness framework of Syrgkanis and Tardos (STOC 2013) to autobidding. A key contribution in our Extension Theorem is a new technique to balance smoothness parameters across heterogeneous agent types. Determining the best POA bound then reduces to solving a POA-revealing mathematical program. Our approach has three main strengths: (1) Simplicity: We prove smoothness for single-item FPA only. The results for simultaneous FPA follow via our Extension Theorem.  For example, by showing smoothness for value and utility maximizers, we obtain the tight POA of $2.18$ for mixed autobidding. (2) Extendibility: Our Extension Theorem adapts to simultaneous FPA with reserve prices and agents with fractionally subadditive valuations and heterogeneous payment sensitivities and target ROI parameters. We establish the first (mostly) tight POA bounds for several models beyond the current autobidding state of the art. (3) Generality: Our Extension Theorem bounds the POA of \emph{coarse correlated equilibria (CCE)}, which are especially relevant as they naturally arise when agents employ regret-minimizing algorithms. Building on Kolumbus and Nisan (WWW 2022), we show theoretically that CCE arising from hybrid regret-minimizing agents exhibit properties that keep their POA low. 
\end{abstract}



\section{Introduction}

Over the past few decades, the online advertising landscape has undergone a significant transformation with the emergence of autobidding. This paradigm shift enables advertisers to delegate complex bidding strategies to automated agents that consider various factors such as ad performance, campaign constraints, and market dynamics. As a result, advertisers can efficiently manage their campaigns and maximize their return on investment.
Autobidding has become increasingly important in the online advertising ecosystem, with a growing number of platforms offering this functionality and the vast majority of advertisers adopting it. According to recent estimates, more than 80\% of online advertising traffic is now managed through autobidding agents (\cite{deng2024}). The widespread adoption of autobidding has far-reaching implications for the online advertising industry, influencing the behavior of advertisers, publishers, and ad exchanges alike.

In the autobidding world, agents are primarily concerned with reporting high-level constraints for their campaigns, including budget and return-on-investment (ROI) constraints. The budget constraint specifies the maximum payment an agent is willing to make for a given campaign, while the ROI constraint determines the maximum amount an agent is willing to pay for a conversion or impression. This constraint is distinct from the traditional notion of an agents's valuation, which represents the true value they place on an item. In contrast, the ROI constraint reflects the agent's budgetary limitations and their desire to achieve a positive return on their investment.

Beyond constraints, agents may also be of different types with respect to the objective they want to maximizes. The two most prominent types that have been considered in the autobidding literature are value maximizer and utility maximizers. Value maximizers focus on maximizing the value of the outcome (e.g., high-value conversions), while controlling costs through budget and ROI constraints. 
On the other hand, utility maximizers want to maximize the difference between the value obtained and price paid. These objectives sit at the extremes of a spectrum that defines the agents' trade-off between value and payment. Therefore, we also allow agents to express their objectives continuously along this spectrum, enabling them to choose an objective that sits anywhere between value-maximization and utility-maximization.

In addition to the shift in bidding strategies, the online advertising industry has also seen a significant transition in auction formats in recent years, particularly with the move from second-price to first-price auctions ($\fpa$). This shift has been especially pronounced in display ad markets, culminating in the adoption of first-price auctions by Google Ad Exchange in September 2019 (\cite{PST20}). The combination of first-price auctions and the increasing reliance on autobidding raises essential questions about the efficiency and performance of $\fpa$ within this new autobidding framework.

To analyze the efficiency of $\fpa$ in the autobidding world, we consider the Price of Anarchy (\poa) initially introduced by \cite{koutsoupias1999worst}. Given a valuation and equilibrium class, the \poa\ measures the ratio of the optimal liquid welfare against the liquid welfare achieved in the worst equilibrium. This metric provides a quantitative assessment of the efficiency loss due to selfish behavior in auctions.
The notion of liquid welfare was first introduced by \cite{dobzinski2014efficiency}. In the context of mechanism design, liquid welfare serves as a widely accepted efficiency metric that extends social welfare to scenarios where players are subject to budget constraints. Specifically, a player's liquid welfare is defined as the minimum value between the utility they derive from an outcome and their available budget. This metric provides a more nuanced understanding of a player's ability to achieve high values, taking into account their limited financial resources.
Encoding the notion of budget in its definition, the optimal liquid welfare represents a meaningful benchmark for measuring the social value of an auction allocation. It acknowledges that players with limited budgets cannot be expected to pay for outcomes that yield extremely high values. By considering the interplay between a player's budget and the value they receive, liquid welfare offers a more realistic assessment of the social welfare achieved by a mechanism.

The \poa\ of $\fpa$ in the autobidding world was recently studied by \cite{deng2024}, both for the model with only value maximizers and the \emph{mixed model} comprising both value and utility maximizers. Surprisingly, they established a tight bound of 2.18 on the $\poa$ for mixed Nash equilibria (MNE) in the mixed-agent model. Their proof employs advanced techniques and leverages deep structural insights into mixed Nash equilibria to achieve this result.

A powerful general technique for bounding the $\poa$ of games is the \emph{smoothness technique}. Originally introduced by \citet{Rou15} for strategic games and later extended by \citet{ST13} to composable auctions and mechanism design, its strength lies in its \emph{Extension Theorems}. In the context of composable mechanisms \cite{ST13}, these theorems allow simple $\poa$ proofs for pure Nash equilibria in a `base mechanism' (e.g., a single-item first-price auction) to extend to more complex compositions (e.g., simultaneous or sequential auctions) and broader equilibrium concepts (e.g., mixed and correlated equilibria). 
The combination of being easily applicable and providing strong general bounds makes it particularly appealing. As a result, the smoothness technique has been successfully applied in various settings. However, its application to the autobidding world remains elusive.



\subsection{Our Contributions}

We derive a smoothness framework to extend the study of the price of anarchy of simultaneous first-price auctions in the autobidding world in various ways: 
\begin{enumerate}\itemsep3pt
    \item We consider the general \emph{hybrid agent model} in which agents have different \emph{types} depending on their payment sensitivity and ROI constraint. This includes the previously studied mixed agent model as a special case, in which all agents are either value or utility maximizers.

    \item We significantly broaden the class of valuation functions considered in autobidding by studying fractionally subadditive (or equivalently, \emph{XOS}) valuations (see \citet{ST13}). In particular, monotone submodular functions form an important special case within this class. To the best of our knowledge, all previous work on autobidding has solely focused on additive valuation functions.

    \item We extend our POA bounds to more general equilibrium concepts, including correlated (CE) and coarse correlated equilibria (CCE).  The latter is particularly relevant in the context of autobidding, as it naturally emerges when agents use regret-minimizing algorithms. 

    \item We extend our results to simultaneous first-price auctions with reserve prices. This is particularly relevant when the seller can leverage machine-learned advice to approximate buyers' valuations (see also the framework by \citet{BDM21}). Using these value signals as reserve prices leads to an improved bound on the POA.
\end{enumerate}


\minisec{Our Smoothness Framework.}
We derive our results for the aforementioned extensions through a unifying framework, adapting the smoothness framework of \citet{ST13} for utility maximizers to our setting in a non-trivial way. However, unlike the original framework, a key technical challenge in our case is the incorporation of different agent types.
To the best of our knowledge, prior works leveraging the smoothness technique assume agents are ‘alike’ in their strategic behavior.
Consequently, the $(\lambda, \mu)$-smoothness parameters established for the base mechanism can be directly lifted to the composed mechanism.
In contrast, our setting allows for different agent types, each with its own smoothness parameters $(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$ that must be integrated. 
A crucial element of our approach is introducing \textit{calibration vectors} that enable us to balance the different smoothness parameters. An upper bound on the POA is then determined by a \emph{$\poa$-revealing mathematical program $(\poarmp)$}. While solving this program presents its own analytical challenges, we are able to find feasible (sometimes optimal) solutions and bound the objective value of the \poarmp, thereby deriving bounds on the POA.

Once the above machinery is established, we can focus on proving the smoothness of a single-item first-price auction with reserve price for the different agent types. 
Similar to the original smoothness approach, our Extension Theorem then allows us to derive (sometimes tight) bounds on the POA of coarse correlated equilibria for simultaneous single-item first-price auctions with reserve prices and fractionally subadditive valuation functions.


