\minisec{Overview of Results.} 
An overview of the POA bounds that we derive for CCE of simultaneous FPA with fractionally subadditive valuations is provided in Table~\ref{tab:poa-bounds}. Here, $W_{0}(\cdot)$ denotes the principal branch of the Lambert $W$ function, introduced at the end of Section \ref{sec:preliminariesPOA}.
Note that the upper bound on the POA is equal to 2 for settings in which all agents have a payment sensitivity ($\sens_{\max}$) of at most 0.79. If $\sens_{\max} > 0.79$, then the upper bound on the POA increases from 2 to 2.18 as $\sens_{\max}$ increases to 1. 
Note that, among other things, this generalizes the result of \citet{deng2024} who, for mixed Nash equilibria and additive valuations, prove an upper bound on the POA of 2 when only considering value maximizers and of 2.18 in the mixed agent model, to CCE and fractionally subadditive valuations.

For simultaneous FPA with feasible reserve prices and additive valuations, we prove upper bounds on the POA for specific agent types as a function of $\eta$. 
Here, $\eta \in [0,1)$ indicates the quality of the reserve prices where a larger $\eta$ indicates a better quality. More specifically, $\eta$ measures the worst relative gap between the reserve price of an auction and the maximum payment that could be extracted. 
For the homogeneous case of utility maximizers and value maximizers, we prove an upper bound on the POA of $\nicefrac{e}{e-1+\eta}$ and $2 - \eta$ respectively (see Table~\ref{tab:poa-bounds-with-r}).
Additionally, we prove that these bounds are tight in Theorems \ref{lemma:LB-BigTauSens-withReserve} and \ref{lemma:LB-POA-Reserve-value-maximizers}. 
To see how the POA decreases as $\eta$ improves, we refer to Figure \ref{fig:upper-bound}. Note that when the quality of the reserve prices is almost perfect, i.e., $\eta \rightarrow 1$, the POA approaches 1. 
We also provide bounds for the mixed agent model and for the homogeneous case of $\sens \in (0, \nicefrac{e-1}{e})$ (see also Table~\ref{tab:poa-bounds-with-r}).

We note that, in our setting with reserve prices, it is required that all items are sold under equilibrium outcomes (which we refer to as \emph{well-supported equilibria}). As we demonstrate, for additive valuations this property holds up to and including CE, but fails for the class of CCE. However, building on the work of \cite{KN22}, we show that for a repeated single-item first-price auction with feasible reserve price, CCE arising from regret-minimizing agents are well-supported, regardless of the combination of agent types. 


