\subsection{Related Work}

\paragraph{Auction design for auto-bidding.}
Motivated by the rise of autobidding in online advertising, recent research has focused on auction design tailored for value-maximizing agents in an autobidding environment. \cite{ABM19} analyze optimal bidding strategies in truthful auctions, establish the existence of equilibrium, and derive welfare price of anarchy (PoA) bounds. \cite{DMM21} and \cite{BDM21A} explore how mechanisms with budget boosts and reserve prices can enhance welfare efficiency guarantees when leveraging machine-learned predictions of buyers' values. \cite{BDM21, BDM22} characterize revenue-optimal auctions under various informational settings, while \cite{aggarwal2025multi} focus on finding optimal bidding strategies in a multi-platform auction setting under budget and ROI constraints. Further, \cite{M22} and \cite{LMP23} investigate how randomized and non-truthful mechanisms can improve welfare efficiency, particularly in auctions with two agents. Notably, \cite{LMP23} show that when restricting to equilibria with deterministic bids, the PoA of the first-price auction is 2. Expanding on this, \cite{deng2024} analyze more general equilibria with randomized bidding strategies, demonstrating that the PoA remains 2 for value-maximizing agents but declines to approximately $2.18$ when considering both value-maximizing and utility-maximizing agents. Our work extends these results to broader auction formats and coarse correlated equilibria ($\cce$). For further details, we refer the interested reader to the survey of \citet{aggarwalSurvey}.


\sloppy
\paragraph{Price of Anarchy of First-Price Auction.}
Under the standard model of quasilinear utilities, the inefficiency of the first-price auction has been considered in the economics literature since the seminal work of \cite{vickrey}. Naturally, due to its simplicity, it has also been well-studied for simultaneous simple auctions. The $\poa$ of $\cce$~~was shown by \cite{ST13} to be at most $\frac{e}{e-1}$  for simultaneous auctions with XOS valuations. This bound was shown to be tight even for a single auction by \cite{syrgkanis-thesis} and for $\mne$~~in simultaneous auctions with submodular valuations by \cite{christodoulou16}. Beyond the smoothness framework, \cite{feldmanfu} showed a \(\poa\) upper bound of \(2\) for simultaneous auctions with subadditive valuations, while \cite{feldman16} have considered $\ce$ and $\cce$ and their properties. More recently, \cite{JL23} showed, that the \emph{Bayesian} $\poa$ for the single-item first-price auction is exactly $ \frac{e^2}{e^{2}-1}$, a breakthrough result.

\fussy
\paragraph{CCE and regret minimization.}
\cite{KN22} investigate a setting where autobidding agents employ regret-minimizing algorithms in repeated auctions for utility-maximizing agents. Their findings show that in first-price auction dynamics, when agents use mean-based regret-minimizing algorithms \cite{BMS18}, convergence to co-undominated equilibria—a refinement of CCE. Our work extends these insights to settings involving both value-maximizing and utility-maximizing agents. Finally, regret minimization has also been considered in the context of autobidding by other works (see e.g., \citet{aggarwal2025noR, lucier2024autobidders,balseiro2019learning}).

