\section{Preliminaries} \label{sec:preliminariesPOA}

We consider the setting in which $n \ge 2$ agents simultaneously participate in $m \ge 1$ single-item auctions.
We use $N = [n]$ and $M = [m]$ to denote the set agents and auctions, respectively, and $\mech_j$ to denote the mechanism that is used to sell the item of auction $j \in M$. 
%The item of auction $j \in M$ is sold by running a single-item mechanism $\mech_j$. 
Each agent $i \in N$ submits to each auction $j \in M$ a non-negative bid $b_{ij} \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$. 
The corresponding bid profile of agent $i$ is denoted by $\b_i = (b_{ij})_{j \in M}$. 
Let $D_i = \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^m$ be the set of all bid profiles of agent $i$. 
The aggregated bid profile of all agents is denoted by $\b = (\b_i)_{i \in N} \in D$, where $D = \times_{i \in N} D_i$.

\paragraph{First-Price Auctions with Reserve Prices.}
We solely focus on first-price auctions with reserve prices. 
So, given reserve prices $\vec{r} = (r_j)_{j \in M} \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^m$, each mechanism $\mech_j$ implements a first-price auction with reserve price $r_j$, denoted by $\fpar{}{r_j}$: Given the bid profile $\b_j = (b_{ij})_{i \in N}$ submitted to auction $j$, $\fpar{}{r_j}$ only considers the bids that are at least as large as the reserve price $r_j$, i.e., $b_{ij} \ge r_j$.
Among these bids, and using a fixed tie-breaking rule, $\mech_j$ allocates the item to the highest bidder, also referred to as the \emph{actual winner} and denoted by $\aw(j) \in \arg\max_{i \in N: b_{ij} \ge r_j} b_{ij}$. 
The price of the actual winner is equal to their bid $b_{\aw(j)j}$. 
Note that the item can remain unassigned if $b_{ij} < r_j$ for all $i \in N$. 

Let $\vec{x}_j(\b) = (x_{ij}(\b))_{i \in N}$ and $\vec{p}_j(\b) = (p_{ij}(\b))_{i \in N}$ refer to the respective allocation and payments of $\fpar{}{r_j}$, i.e., for $i = \aw(j)$ we have that $x_{ij}(\b) = 1$ and $p_{ij}(\b) = b_{ij}$, and for $i \neq \aw(j)$ we have that $x_{ij}(\b) = 0$ and $p_{ij}(\b) = 0$. 
Note that both $\vec{x}_j$ and $\vec{p}_j$ only depend on the input profile $\b_j$; we sometime only use $\vec{b}_j$ as the argument for notational convenience.

Our global mechanism implements the above mechanisms with reserve prices $\vec{r} = (r_j)_{j \in M}$ simultaneously. 
That is, given a bid profile $\b$, the outcome $\mech(\vec{r}, \b) = (\vec{x}(\b), \vec{p}(\b))$ is determined by the allocation $\vec{x}(\b)= (\vec{x}_{j}(\b))_{j \in M}$ and the payments $\vec{p}(\b) = (\vec{p}_{j}(\b))_{j \in M}$ obtained by running $m$ first-price auctions with reserve prices $\vec{r}$ simultaneously.  
We define $p_i(\b) = \sum_{j \in M} p_{ij}(\b)$ as the total payment of an agent $i$ over all $m$ auctions and $P(\b) = \sum_{i \in N} p_i(\b)$ as the sum of all payments. 
Further, we define $\vec{x}_i(\vec{b}) = (x_{ij}(\vec{b}))_{j \in M} \subseteq \set{0,1}^{m}$ to denote the allocation of an agent $i \in N$ and $\vec{X}$ to denote the set of feasible allocations, i.e., $\vec{X} = \set{\vec{x}(\vec{b}) = (\vec{x}_{i}(\vec{b}))_{i \in N} \mid \sum_{i \in N} x_{ij}(\vec{b}) \le 1, \forall j \in M}$. 
We slightly overload notation and use $\vec{x}_i(\vec{b})$ also to refer to the set of items assigned to an agent $i$, i.e., $\vec{x}_i(\vec{b}) = \sset{j \in M}{x_{ij}(\b) = 1} \subseteq M$. Additionally, we sometimes omit the argument $\vec{b}$ when it is irrelevant or clear from context. 

For ease of notation, we use $\fpar{m}{\vec{r}}$ and $\fpar{m}{}$, respectively, to refer to $m$ simultaneous first-price auctions with reserve prices $\vec{r}$ and without reserve prices. 
We use $\fpar{}{r}$ to indicate that we consider a single-item first-price auction with reserve price $r$.
If $m = 1$, we drop the auction index $j = 1$ from all our notation.


\paragraph{Valuation Functions.} 
Each agent $i \in N$ has a valuation function $v_i: 2^M \rightarrow \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ over the subsets of the auctioned items, where $v_i(S)$ is the value that agent $i$ obtains when receiving the items in $S \subseteq M$. We assume w.l.o.g. that $v_i(\emptyset) = 0$. Also, we assume that $v_i$ is \emph{monotone}, i.e., $v_i(S) \le v_i(T)$ for all $S \subseteq T \subseteq M$.
We use $\mathcal{V}_i$ to denote the class of valuation functions of agent $i$ and let $\mathcal{V} = \times_{i \in N} \mathcal{V}_i$ be the set of all valuation functions of the agents.
We use $\vec{v} = (v_i)_{i \in N} \in \mathcal{V}$ to refer to the profile of valuation functions of the agents. 
%
We consider different classes of valuation functions:

\begin{definition}\label{def:val-classes}
Let $v_i: 2^M \rightarrow \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ be a valuation function. 
\begin{itemize}
\item $v_i$ is \emph{additive} if there exist additive valuations $(v_{ij})_{j \in M} \in \mathbb{R}^m_{\ge 0}$ such that for every subset $S \subseteq M$, it holds that $v_i(S) = \sum_{j \in S} v_{ij}$. 
\item $v_i$ is \emph{submodular} if $v_i(S \cup \set{j}) - v_i(S) \ge v_i(T \cup \set{j}) - v_i(T)$ for all $S \subseteq T \subseteq M$. 
\item $v_i$ is \emph{fractionally subadditive} (or, \emph{XOS}), if there exists a class $\mathcal{L}_{i} = \set{(v^\ell_{ij})_{j \in M} \in \mathbb{R}^m_{\ge 0}}$ of additive valuations such that for every subset $S \subseteq M$, it holds that $v_i(S) = \max_{\ell \in \mathcal{L}_{i}} \sum_{j \in S} v^\ell_{ij}$. 
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Let $\mathcal{V}_{\add}$, $\mathcal{V}_{\sub}$ and $\mathcal{V}_{\xos}$ refer to the set of additive, submodular and fractionally subadditive (XOS) valuation functions, respectively.


\paragraph{Randomized Bid Profiles.}
Let $\dist$ be a probability distribution over the set of bid profiles in $D$. 
Throughout the paper, we use $\B \sim \dist$ to denote a random bid profile that is drawn from $\dist$. For ease of notation, we often omit the explicit reference to $\dist$ and identify $\B$ with $\dist$. 
Further, we define $\Delta_i$ as the space of randomized bid profiles of an agent $i$ and use $\b \in D$ to refer to a deterministic (or pure) bid profile.
For a randomized bid profile $\B$, we use $f_{\B}$ and $F_{\B}$ to refer to the probability density function (PDF) and cumulative distribution function (CDF) of $\B$, respectively.
The support of $\B$, denoted by $\supp(\B)$, refers to the set of bid profiles that have positive density under $\B$, i.e., $\supp(\B) = \sset{\b \in D}{f_{\B}(\b)>0}$.
If $\supp(\B) = \set{\b}$ then $\B$ chooses $\b$ deterministically; we write $\B = \b$ to indicate this.
We use $\supp_i(\B)$ to refer to the set of bid profiles $\b_i$ of agent $i$ that have positive density under $\B$. 
The \emph{marginal} $\vec{B}_{-i}$ of $\B$ is defined by the following PDF: 
\[
\forall \b_{-i} \in D_{-i}: \qquad
f_{\B_{-i}}(\b_{-i}) = \int_{D_i} f_{\B}(\b_i, \b_{-i}) d \b_i.
\]
Given a bid profile $\b'_i$ of agent $i$, we denote by $(\b'_i, \B_{-i})$ the random bid profile that we obtain from $\B$ when agent $i$ bids $\b'_i$ deterministically and the other agents bid according to the marginal $\B_{-i}$.


\paragraph{Hybrid Agent Model and Return-On-Investment.}
Each agent $i \in N$ is associated with a \emph{gain function} $\z_i: D \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ that $i$ wants to maximize; for each bid profile $\b$, $\z_i$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:agent-objective}
\z_i(\b) = 
v_i(\vec{x}_i(\b)) - \sens_i \cdot p_{i}(\b).
\end{equation}
Here, $\sens_i \in [0,1]$ is the \emph{payment sensitivity} (or, \emph{sensitivity}) parameter of agent $i$. Intuitively, a larger sensitivity $\sens_i$ indicates that agent $i$ is more sensitive towards the payments (in the sense of being negatively affected). In particular, agent $i$ is a value maximizer if $\sens_i = 0$, and a utility maximizer if $\sens_i = 1$. Our model thus allows us to capture a large spectrum of agents' sensitivities, ranging from value maximizers to utility maximizers. 
This general model is also termed the \emph{hybrid agent model} (see, e.g., \cite{balseiro2021robust}, \cite{survey}). 
Most models studied in the literature consider a mix of value and utility maximiers only, i.e., $\sens_i \in \set{0,1}$ for all $i \in N$; we refer to this special case as the \emph{mixed agent model}. 

%\minisec{Return-on-Investment Constraint}
Each agent $i$  has a \emph{return-on-investment (ROI)} constraint that must be satisfied (see \cite{survey}). 
Given a random bid profile $\vec{B}$, the ROI constraint of an agent $i$ enforces that the expected total payment of $i$ is at most a factor $\tau_i \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ of their expected valuation for the received items:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ROI}
\pe \left[p_{i}(\B)\right]\le \tau_i \cdot \pe[v_i(\vec{x}_i(\B))].
\end{equation}
Here, $\tau_i$ is also called the \emph{target} parameter of agent $i$.
We define $\mathcal{R}_i$ as the set of random bid profiles $\B$ that satisfy the ROI constraint \eqref{eq:ROI} for agent $i$. 
Note that for a deterministic bid profile $\vec{B} = \vec{b}$, the ROI constraint \eqref{eq:ROI} reduces to $p_i(\vec{b}) \le \tau_i v_i(\vec{x}_i(\vec{b}))$.

We assume w.l.o.g. that $\tau_i \sens_i \le 1$ for all agents $i \in N$.
Basically, if an agent with sensitivity $\sens_i > 0$ has a target parameter $\tau_i > 1/\sens_i$, then $i$ prefers to withdraw whenever they are asked to pay more than $1/\sens_i$ their valuation. 
In other words, there are no equilibria in which the expected payment of agent $i$ exceeds $1/\sens_i$ times their expected value, as agent $i$ would have a negative expected gain and be better off by bidding 0 deterministically in each auction.
In essence, this has the same effect as capping the target parameter at $1/\sens_i$. Note that this argument applies only to agents with sensitivity $\sens_i > 0$. 
In particular, the target parameter $\tau_i$ of each value maximizer $i$ with $\sens_i = 0$ remains unconstrained as $\tau_i \sens_i \le 1$ is always satisfied. 


\paragraph{Equilibrium Notions.}
The objective of each agent $i$ is to determine a random bid profile $\B_i$ that maximizes their gain, subject to their ROI constraint and the strategy $\B_{-i}$ of the other agents. That is, each agent $i$ solves the following optimization problem, which is often defined as the \emph{agent's optimization problem}:
\[
\max_{\B_i} \ \pe[\z_i(\B_i, \B_{-i})] \quad \text{subject to \quad $(\B_i, \B_{-i}) \in \mathcal{R}_i$.}
\]
The resulting bid profile $\B$ can be interpreted as an equilibrium, when an important refinement is made to the equilibrium notions as defined in Appendix \ref{sec:appequilibria}. That is, for each agent $i$ only deviations that satisfy the ROI constraint are considered.

\begin{definition} \label{def:equilibria}
Let $\vec{B}$ be a random bid profile satisfying the ROI constraint of each agent, i.e., 
$\B \in \mathcal{R}_i$ for all $i \in N$.

\begin{itemize}
\item 
$\vec{B}$ is a \emph{\cce} if for every agent $i \in N$ we have: %\emph{coarse correlated equilibrium (\cce)}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cce-def}
\pe [\z_i(\vec{B})] \ge \pe [\z_i(\vec{B}'_i, \vec{B}_{-i})] \qquad \forall (\vec{B}'_i, \vec{B}_{-i}) \in {\mathcal{R}_i}.
\end{equation}

\item 
$\vec{B}$ is a \emph{\ce} if for every agent $i \in N$ and every swapping function $\swap: \supp_{i}(\vec{B}) \rightarrow \Delta_i$ we have: %\emph{correlated equilibrium (\ce)}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ce-def}
\pe [\z_i(\vec{B}) ] \ge \pe [\z_i( \swap(\vec{B}_i), \vec{B}_{-i}) ] \qquad \forall (\swap(\vec{B}_i), \vec{B}_{-i}) \in {\mathcal{R}_i}.
\end{equation}

\item $\vec{B}$ is a \emph{\mne} if $\vec{B} = \prod_{i \in [n]} \vec{B}_i$ and for every agent $i \in N$ we have: %\emph{mixed Nash equilibrium (\mne)}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mne-def}
\pe [\z_i(\vec{B})] \ge \pe [\z_i(\vec{B}'_i, \vec{B}_{-i})] \qquad \forall (\vec{B}'_i, \vec{B}_{-i}) \in {\mathcal{R}_i}.
\end{equation}
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\bigskip
Note that for a correlated equilibrium $\B$, $(\swap(\vec{B}_i), \vec{B}_{-i}) \in {\mathcal{R}_i}$ implies that:
\[
\pe \left [ \pe \left[p_{i}(\swap(\b_i), \B_{-i}) \mid \B_i = \b_i \right] \right ] \le \tau_i \cdot \pe \left [ \pe \left [v_i(x_i(\swap(\b_i), \B_{-i})) \mid \B_i = \b_i \right ] \right],
\]
where the outer expectation is for $\B_i$ and the inner expectation is for $\swap(\b_i)$ and $\B_{-i}$. Also note that the gain in the definition of a \ce\ $\B$ is evaluated for the entire support of $\B$.
Note that it holds that $\mne \subseteq \ce \subseteq \cce$, where $\cce$, $\ce$ and $\mne$ refer to the sets of equilibria. Further details on the equilibrium hierarchy and why pure Nash equilibria are not considered\footnote{As the ROI constraints must be satisfied, it is not true that a
pure Nash equilibrium w.r.t. deterministic deviations survives when randomization is allowed.} can be found in Appendix \ref{sec:appequilibria}. 
We say that an equilibrium $\B$ is \textit{well-supported} if all the items are always sold.
This is an important property that we need to exploit in our smoothness lemmas for auctions with reserve prices. 
As we prove in Section \ref{sec:well-supported-eq}, for most equilibrium notions and additive valuations this is not a restriction. 
We write $\vec{x}_j(\b_j) \neq \vec{0}$ to indicate that the item is sold in auction $j$, and we write $\vec{x}(\b) \neq \vec{0}$ to indicate that all items are sold under $\b$, i.e., $\vec{x}_j(\b_j) \neq \vec{0}$ for all $j \in M$. \\

\paragraph{Liquid Price of Anarchy.}
We use \emph{liquid welfare} as the social welfare objective (see, e.g., \cite{survey}), which is also the standard benchmark in the autobidding literature. 
Intuitively, the liquid welfare measures the maximum amount of payments one can extract from the agents.
Given an allocation $\vec{x}$, the liquid welfare of agent $i$ with $\sens_i > 0$ is $\min(v_i(\vec{x}_i)/\sens_i, \tau_i v_i(\vec{x}_i))$, where the first term reflects that the gain $\z_i$ of agent $i$ always remains non-negative and the second term is due to the ROI constraint. 
Note that because $\tau_i \sens_i \le 1$ (as discussed above), the liquid welfare of $i$ is $\tau_i v_i(\vec{x}_i)$. 
The liquid welfare of agent $i$ with $\sens_i = 0$ is $\tau_i v_i(\vec{x}_i)$, i.e., due to the ROI constraint only.
As a consequence, the liquid welfare of an agent evaluates to $\tau_i v_i(\vec{x}_i)$, independently of their type.
So given a feasible allocation $\vec{x} \in \vec{X}$, the liquid welfare of $\vec{x}$ is defined as:
$$
\sw(\vec{x}) = \sum_{i \in N}  \tau_i v_i(\vec{x}_i). 
$$
We use $\vec{\tau}\vec{v}$ to refer to the vector of $\tau_i$-adjusted agent valuations, i.e., $\vec{\tau}\vec{v} = (\tau_i v_{i})_{i \in N}$. 
An optimal allocation $\vec{x}^* = \vec{x}^*(\vec{\tau}\vec{v}) \in \vec{X}$ maximizes the liquid welfare over all feasible allocations. %, i.e., $\sw(\vec{x}^*) \ge \sw(\vec{x})$ for all $\vec{x} \in \vec{X}$. 
We use $\opt(\vec{\tau}\vec{v}) = \sw(\vec{x}^*)$ to denote the maximum liquid welfare with respect to $\vec{\tau}\vec{v}$. 

For additive valuation functions $\vec{v} = (v_{ij})_{i \in N, j \in M}$, we say that an agent $i \in N$ is the \emph{rightful winner} of auction $j \in M $ if $i$ is an agent with maximum $\tau_i$-adjusted valuation. We denote this agent by $\rw(j) \in \arg\max_{i \in N} \tau_i v_{ij}$ and in case of ties, we let $\rw(j)$ denote the winner of auction $j$ in the considered optimal allocation. 
Thus, in the additive case, the optimal liquid welfare is $\opt(\vec{\tau}\vec{v}) = \sum_{j \in M} \tau_{\rw(j)} v_{\rw(j)j}$.

The \emph{price of anarchy} with respect to a given class of valuation functions and an equilibrium notion is defined as the worst-case ratio of the optimal liquid welfare over the expected liquid welfare of any equilibrium. More formally, given a class of valuation functions $\mathcal{V}_{\val}$ with $\val \in \set{\add, \sub, \xos}$, let $I=(N, M, \vec{\sigma}, \vec{\tau}, \vec{v}) \in \mathcal{I}_{\val}$ denote an instance with $\vec{v} \in \mathcal{V}_{\val}$. 
Then, given an equilibrium notion $\equ \in \set{\pne, \mne, \ce, \cce}$, we define the \emph{price of anarchy} of $\equ$ with respect to $\mathcal{V}_{\val}$ as:
\[
\equ\text{-}\poa(\mathcal{V}_{\val}) =
\sup_{I \in \mathcal{I}_{\val}} \sup_{\vec{B} \in \equ(I)}
\frac{\sum_{i \in N} \tau_i v_i(x^*_i(\vec{\tau}\vec{v}))}{\pe[\sum_{i \in N} \tau_i v_i(x_i(\B))]}.
\]



\paragraph{Reserve Prices.}
In auctions with reserve prices, an item might not be sold simply because the reserve price is too
high.
As in previous works (\cite{balseiro2021robust}, \cite{deng2024}), we therefore make the assumption that for additive valuations the reserve price $r_j$ is at most the $\tau_{\rw(j)}$-adjusted value of the rightful winner $\rw(j)$ for each auction $j \in M$, i.e., $\tau_{\rw(j)} v_{\rw(j)j}$. 
We introduce a parameter $\eta_j$ that measures the relative gap between $r_j$ and $\tau_{\rw(j)} v_{\rw(j)j}$. More formally, we define $\eta_j \in [0,1)$ such that $r_j = \eta_j \tau_{\rw(j)} v_{\rw(j)j}$ and define $\eta = \min_{j \in N} \eta_j$ as the smallest relative gap.
Note that the price of anarchy could be unbounded if $\eta=1$, as it is not guarantee that the optimal liquid welfare is achieved in each equilibrium. Specifically, consider a single auction with $r = \tau_{\rw} v_{\rw}$. Suppose that agent $\rw$ is a utility maximizer and the only agent that can meet the reserve price. Then $\rw$ deterministically bidding 0 is a \mne\ with a liquid welfare of $0$, while the optimal liquid welfare is $\tau_{\rw} v_{\rw} > 0$. This would lead to an unbounded \poa.
We therefore only consider reserve prices $\vec{r}$ that satisfy $\eta_j \in [0,1)$ for each auction $j \in M$ and call such call reserve prices \emph{feasible}.
For fractionally subadditive valuations $\vec{v} \in \mathcal{V}_{\xos}$, we say that reserve prices are feasible if, for all agents $i \in N$, they are feasible for the \emph{additive representatives} $(v^*_{ij})_{j \in M}$ with respect to their optimal allocation $\vec{x}^*_i$ (see Section~\ref{sec:extension-theorem} for more details).
As observed by \citet{BDM21}, a reserve price $r_{j}$ might be interpreted as a prediction of the $\tau_{\rw(j)}$-adjusted value of the rightful winner.
In this context, $\eta$ is an indicator of the quality of the prediction, and as $\eta$ increases so does the quality of the prediction.


\paragraph{Uniform Target Parameter.}
All previous works studying the mixed agent model assume that the ROI constraint is imposed on the value maximizers only, while the utility maximizers remain unconstrained. As argued in \cite{deng2024}, this is equivalent to assuming a uniform ROI target parameter of $\tau_i = 1$ for all agents $i \in N$.
Without loss of generality, we can extend the assumption of a uniform target parameter to our hybrid agent model.
To see this, consider an instance $I=(N, M, \vec{\sigma}, \vec{\tau}, \vec{v})$ with arbitrary valuations, sensitivities and target parameters. 
As mentioned earlier, we can assume w.l.o.g. that $\sens_i \tau_i \le 1$ for every agent $i \in N$. 
Now consider the instance $I'=(N, M, \vec{\sigma}', \vec{\tau}', \vec{v}')$ that is just a scaled version of $I$ with $\vec{v}' = \vec{\tau} \vec{v}$, $\vec{\sens}' = \vec{\tau}\vec{\sens}$ and $\vec{\tau}' = \vec{1}$. 
Note that $\sens'_i \le 1$ for each agent $i \in N$.
For the instance $I'$, the gain $\z_i'$ of an agent $i$ for a bid profile $\vec{b}$ is $\z'_i(\vec{b}) = v'_i(x_i(\vec{b})) - \sens'_i p_i(\vec{b}) = \tau_i v_i(x_i(\vec{b})) - \tau_i \sens_i p_i(\vec{b}) = \tau_i \z_i(\vec{b})$, which is just the gain function for the instance $I$ scaled by the target parameter of agent $i$. 
And for the instance $I'$, the ROI constraint of agent $i$ is $p_i(\vec{b}) \le \tau_i' v'_i(x_i(\vec{b})) = \tau_i v_i(x_i(\vec{b}))$, which coincides with the ROI constraint for the instance $I$. 
Therefore, when considering the instance $I'$, agents still want to maximize $\z_i(\vec{b})$ and their bidding space remains unchanged compared to $I$. And so, the set of equilibria for the instances $I$ and $I'$ coincide. Additionally, note that the liquid welfare also coincides, as for the instance $I'$ the liquid welfare of an agent $i$ given a bid profile $\vec{b}$ is $\tau_i' v_i'(x_i(\vec{b})) = \tau_i v_i(x_i(\vec{b}))$, which is equal to the liquid welfare of agent $i$ for the instance $I$.

In the subsequent sections, we will therefore consider a uniform target parameter $\tau_i = 1$ for all agents $i \in N$.
In this case, agents will behave differently depending on their sensitivity parameter. Depending on the sensitivity parameter $\sens_i$ of an agent $i$, we define the \emph{type} of agent $i$. 
The set of different agent types is defined as $T = \sset{\type}{\text{$\exists i \in N$ with $\sens_i = \type$}}$.
We use $N_{\type} \subseteq N$ to refer to the subset of agents having type $\type$, i.e., $N_{\type} = \sset{i \in N}{\sens_i = \type}$.
Also, given a type $\type \in T$, we use $\sens_{\type}$ to refer to the sensitivity of type $t$ for clarity.

\paragraph{The Lambert $W$ Function.} %\label{app:lambert}
In order to derive bounds on the POA analytically, we use the Lambert $W$ function, which is the multi-valued inverse of $f(z) = z e^z$. 
We exclusively use the primary real branch of the $W$ function, also called the \emph{principal branch} and commonly denoted by $W_0$, that we define below. We refer the interested reader to \cite{corless96}. 

\begin{definition}\label{def:lambert}
The principal branch of the Lambert $W$ function is the real function $W_{0}: [-\frac{1}{e}, \infty) \rightarrow [-1, \infty)$ such that $z = W_{0}(z) e^{W_{0}(z)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{fact}\label{fact:lambert-derivative}
For every $z > -\frac{1}{e}$, the derivative of $W_{0}(z)$ is $\frac{W_{0}(z)}{z(1+W_{0}(z))}$.
\end{fact}


\noindent For readability, the proofs of some auxiliary technical lemmas are omitted and can be found in the Appendix. 


\section{Smoothness and the Extension Theorem} \label{sec:smoothness:extension}

We introduce our smoothness framework for simultaneous first-price auctions with reserve prices under the hybrid agent model.
The advantage of the smoothness framework is that we can focus on proving smoothness for a single auction only. 
Our Extension Theorem then enables us to derive bounds on the POA for simultaneous auctions with fractionally subadditive valuation functions. 
In contrast to the original framework by \citet{ST13}, the technical challenge is to incorporate different agent types. 

We first need to introduce the notion of ROI-restricted bid profiles. 

\begin{definition}
    Let $\B'_i$ be a random bid profile of agent $i$ over $D_i$. We say that $\B'_i$ is \emph{ROI-restricted} if for each $\b_{-i} \in D_{-i}$, it holds that:
    \[
    \pe[p_i(\B'_i, \b_{-i})] \le  \pe[v_i(\vec{x}_i(\B'_i, \b_{-i}))].
    \]
\end{definition}

We can now introduce our smoothness notion. 

\begin{definition}[Smoothness]\label{def:smooth} 
Consider a first-price auction $\fpar{}{r}$ with a feasible reserve price. 
Let the rightful winner $i$ be of type $\type \in T$. 
Then, $\fpar{}{r}$ is \emph{$(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$-smooth for type $\type$} with $\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type} >0$, if there exists a random bid $B'_{i} = B'_{i}(\vec{v})$ such that $B'_{i}$ is ROI-restricted, and
for every bid profile $\b$ such that $\vec{x}(\b) \neq \vec{0}$ it holds that:\begin{equation}\label{eq:def:smoothness}
            \pe\left[\z_{i}\left(B'_{i}, \b_{-{i}}\right)\right] \ge \lambda_{\type}  v_{i} - \mu_{\type}  p_{\aw(\vec{b})}(\b).
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

We remark that, crucially, the random deviation $B'_{i}$ of the rightful winner $i$ may depend on the valuations $\vec{v}$ but \emph{not} on the bid profile $\b$. 
Note that \eqref{eq:def:smoothness} needs to hold only for bid profiles $\b$ under which the item is sold; this condition is redundant if the reserve price is $r = 0$. 

\subsection{Smoothness Lemmas} \label{sec:general-smoothness}

We present two smoothness lemmas for the general model of a first-price auction with a feasible reserve price and hybrid agents. 
We start with a simple smoothness lemma for value maximizers. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fpa-smoothness-key-2}
Consider a first-price auction $\fpar{}{r}$ with a feasible reserve price.
Let the rightful winner be of type $\type \in T$ with $\sens_{\type} = 0$. 
Then, for every $\mu \in (0,(1-\eta)^{-1}]$, % {1/(\tau - \tau \eta)}]$, 
$\fpar{}{r}$ is $(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$-smooth for type $\type$ with 
$\lambda_{\type} = \mu_{\type} = \mu$.
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}{Proof}
Assume that the rightful winner $i$ is of type $\type \in T$ with $\sens_{\type} = 0$. 
We need to show that there exists a ROI-restricted random bid $B'_{i}$ such that for every bid profile $\b$ with $\vec{x}(\b) \neq \vec{0}$ and $\aw = \aw(\b)$ the actual winner, it holds that:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:def:smoothness-repeat2}
    \pe\left[\z_{i}\left(B'_{i}, \b_{-{i}}\right)\right] \ge \mu v_{i} - \mu p_{\aw}(\b).
\end{equation}

Let $B'_{i} = B'_{i}(\vec{v})$ be a random unilateral deviation of $i$ drawn from $[\eta v_{i}, v_i]$ with CDF $F(z) =  F_{B'_{i}}(z) = \mu z / v_i + 1-\mu$. 
Note that the domain is well-defined as $\eta \in [0,1)$, and it is easy to verify that $F(\cdot)$ is non-negative and increasing over $[\eta v_{i}, v_i]$ and $F(v_{i}) = 1$.
Also, $B'_{i}$ is ROI-restricted as the condition is even pointwise satisfied, i.e., for every $z \in [\eta v_{i}, v_{i}]$ it holds that $p_{i}(z, \cdot) \le v_{i}(\vec{x}_{i}(z, \cdot))$. 

It remains to show that $B'_{i}$ satisfies \eqref{eq:def:smoothness-repeat2}.
Note that the expected gain of $i$ is always non-negative, as $i$ bids above $v_i$ with 0 probability. 
Thus, \eqref{eq:def:smoothness-repeat2} holds trivially if $v_{i} \le p_{\aw}(\b)$.
Therefore, assume that $v_{i} > p_{\aw}(\b)$ and define $\theta_{i} := \max( \eta v_{i}, \max_{j \neq i} b_j)$.
For every $z \ge \theta_{i}$, $i$ wins the item under the bid profile $(z, \vec{b}_{-i})$ and pays $p_{i}(z, \vec{b}_{-i}) = z$.
As the item is sold under the bid profile $\vec{b}$ by assumption, the actual winner under $\vec{b}$ either pays the reserve price or their maximum bid, i.e., $p_{\aw}(\b) = \max( \eta v_{i}, \max_{j} b_j)$.
We obtain: 
\[
\theta_{i} 
= \max \bigg( \eta v_{i}, \max_{j \neq i} b_j \bigg) 
\le \max \bigg ( \eta v_{i}, \max_j b_j \bigg)
= p_{\aw}(\b)
< v_{i}.
\]
This leads to the desired result as:
\begin{align*}
\pe\left[\z_{i}\left(B'_{i}, \vec{b}_{-{i}}\right)\right] 
& = v_{i} (1-F(\theta_{i})) 
= v_i \left(1- \left( \frac{\mu \theta_i}{v_i} + 1-\mu \right)\right) \\
&= \mu v_i - \mu \theta_i 
\ge \mu v_i - \mu p_{\aw}(\b).
\end{align*}
Note that the first equality holds because the sensitivity of $i$ is $\sens_{\type} = 0$. % and for every $z \in [\theta_{i}, v_i]$, the ROI constraint of $i$ is satisfied because $B'_{i}$ is ROI-restricted.  
\end{proof}

In comparison to the existing smoothness proofs without reserve price, the intuition is that a reserve price allows us to increase the probability mass of the random deviation for larger bids. This provides a better trade-off in terms of the smoothness parameters. Our smoothness lemma for agent types $\type$ with $\sens_{\type} > 0$ follows the same approach, but is technically more involved. 


\begin{lemma} \label{lem:fpa-smoothness-key-1}
Consider a first-price auction $\fpar{}{r}$ with a feasible reserve price.
Let the rightful winner $\rw$ be of type $\type \in T$ with $\sens = \sens_{\type} > 0$, so $r= \eta v_{\rw}$ with $\eta \in [0,1)$.
Then, for every $\mu$ satisfying:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:alpharestr}
\begin{cases}
	\mu \ge \sens \left( \ln\left(\frac{1-{\sens\eta}}{1- \sens} \right)\right)^{-1}, & \text{if \ $\sens < 1$,}\\
    \mu > 0 , & \text{if \ $\sens = 1$,}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
$\fpar{}{r}$ is $(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$-smooth for type $\type$ with $\mu_{\type} = \mu$ and:
\[
\lambda_{\type} =  \frac{\mu}{\sens}\left(1 - \frac{1 - {\sens\eta}}{e^{\sens/{\mu}}}\right).
\]
\end{lemma}
We elaborate on the expression in \eqref{eq:alpharestr} in Lemma \ref{lem:fpa-smoothness-key-1}.
First, note that $\sens \in (0, 1]$, as $\sens > 0$ and $\sens \le 1$ by assumption. 
The $\ln(\cdot)$ expression decreases as $\eta$ increases and converges to $0$ (from above) as $\eta \rightarrow 1$; the lower bound restriction on $\mu$ thus increases as $\eta$ increases. 
As $\eta < 1$, the $\ln(\cdot)$ expression is well-defined for all combinations of $\sens$ and $\eta$, except when $\sens = 1$. In the latter case, we only impose the restriction that $\mu > 0$.

Given $\mu$, we define a parameter $\gamma = \gamma(\mu)$ as follows: 
\begin{equation}\label{def:gamma}
    \gamma(\mu) 
    = \frac{1}{\sens}\left(1 - \frac{1 - {\sens\eta}}{e^{\sens/{\mu}}}\right),
\end{equation}
which will be useful in the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:fpa-smoothness-key-1}.
Note that $\gamma$ is well-defined because $\sens > 0$ by assumption. 

The following corollary is an immediate consequence of the definitions above. 
\begin{corollary} \label{cor:gamma}
Let $\mu$ satisfy \eqref{eq:alpharestr} and let $\gamma$ be defined as in \eqref{def:gamma}. 
Then, $\gamma \in [\eta,1]$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}{Proof} %[Proof of Corollary~\ref{cor:gamma}]
Note that the interval $[\eta, 1]$ is well-defined because $\eta \in [0, 1)$ by assumption. 
We first prove the lower bound on $\gamma$. 
Note that $e^{\sens/\mu} > 1$ as $\mu > 0$ and $\sigma >0$, and therefore:
\[
    \gamma = \frac{1}{\sens} \bigg(1 - \frac{1 - {\sens \eta}}{e^{{\sens}/{\mu}}}\bigg)
    > \frac{1}{\sens} \cdot \sens \eta
    = \eta.    
\]    
For the upper bound on $\gamma$, we have that:
\[  
    \gamma 
    = \frac{1}{\sens} \left(1 - \frac{1 - {\sens\eta}}{e^{{\sens}/{\mu}}}\right) 
    \leq \frac{1}{\sens} \left(1 - \frac{1 - {\sens\eta}}{e^{\ln \left(\frac{1 - {\sens\eta }}{1 - \sens}\right)}}\right) 
    = \frac{1}{\sens} \cdot  \sens = 1,
\]
where the inequality follows from \eqref{eq:alpharestr} and because $e^{x}$ is non-decreasing in $x$.
\end{proof}

We now continue with the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:fpa-smoothness-key-1}.
\medskip


\begin{proof}{Proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:fpa-smoothness-key-1}}
Assume that the rightful winner $i$ is of type $\type \in T$ with $\sens = \sens_{\type} > 0$. 
We need to show that there exists a ROI-restricted random bid $B'_i$ such that for every bid profile $\b$ with $\vec{x}(\b) \neq \vec{0}$ and $\aw = \aw(\b)$ the actual winner, it holds that:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:def:smoothness-repeat}
    \pe\left[\z_{i}\left(B'_{i}, \b_{-{i}}\right)\right] \ge \mu\gamma  v_{i} - \mu  p_{\aw}(\b).
\end{equation}

Let $B'_i = B'_i(\vec{v})$ be a random unilateral deviation of $i$ drawn from $[\eta v_i, \gamma v_{i}]$ with PDF $f(z) = f_{B'_i}(z) = \mu/(v_i - \sens z)$. 
Note that the domain is well-defined as $\gamma \in [ \eta, 1]$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:gamma}, and that $f(\cdot)$ is non-negative.
Also note that:
\begin{align*}
    \int_{\eta v_i}^{\gamma v_i}f(z) d z
    & 
    = \int_{\eta v_i}^{\gamma v_i}\frac{\mu}{v_{i}- \sens z} d z
    = \mu \int_{\eta v_i}^{\gamma v_i} \left(\frac{-\ln\left(v_i-\sens z \right)}{\sens}\right)' d z \\
    &=\frac{\mu}{\sens}\ln\left(\frac{1-\sens\eta }{1-\sens\gamma}\right)
    = \frac{\mu}{\sens}\ln\left(e^{{\sens}/{\mu}} \right)
    = 1,
\end{align*}
where the fourth equality follows from the definition of $\gamma$ in \eqref{def:gamma}.
Furthermore, $B'_i$ is ROI-restricted as the condition is even pointwise satisfied, i.e., for $z \in [\eta v_i, \gamma v_i]$ it holds that $p_i(z, \cdot) \le \gamma v_i(\vec{x}_i(z, \cdot)) \le v_i(\vec{x}_i(z, \cdot))$, as $\gamma \in [ \eta, 1]$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:gamma}. 

It remains to show that $B'_i$ satisfies \eqref{eq:def:smoothness-repeat}.
Note that the expected gain of $i$ is always non-negative, as $i$ bids above $v_i$ with 0 probability and $\sens \le 1$.
Thus, \eqref{eq:def:smoothness-repeat} holds trivially if $ \gamma v_i \le  p_{\aw}(\b)$.
Therefore, assume that $\gamma v_i >  p_{\aw}(\b)$ and define $\theta_i := \max( \eta v_i, \max_{j \neq i} b_j)$.
Then, for every $z \ge \theta_i$, $i$ wins the item under bid profile $(z, \vec{b}_{-i})$ and pays $p_i(z, \vec{b}_{-i}) = z$.
As the item is sold under bid profile $\vec{b}$ by assumption, the actual winner under $\vec{b}$ either pays the reserve price or their maximum bid, i.e., $p_{\aw}(\b) = \max( \eta v_i, \max_{j} b_j)$.
We obtain:
\[
\theta_i 
= \max \bigg ( \eta  v_i, \max_{j \neq i} b_j \bigg) 
\le \max \bigg ( \eta  v_i, \max_j b_j \bigg )
= p_{\aw}(\b)
< \gamma v_i.
\]
This leads to the desired result as:
\begin{align*}
\pe\left[\z_i\left(B'_i, \vec{b}_{-i}\right)\right] 
& 
= \int_{\theta_i}^{\gamma v_i} (v_i - \sens p_i(z, \vec{b}_{-i}) )f(z) d z 
= \int_{\theta_i}^{\gamma v_i} (v_i  - \sens z) f(z) d z  \\
& 
= \int_{\theta_i}^{\gamma v_i} \mu d z  
= \mu\gamma v_i - \mu\theta_i 
\ge \mu \gamma v_i - \mu p_{\aw}(\b).
\end{align*}
Note that the first equality holds because the sensitivity of $i$ is $\sens$. % and for every $z \in [\theta_i, \gamma v_i]$, the ROI constraint of $i$ is satisfied because $B'_i$ is ROI-restricted. 
\end{proof}


\subsection{Extension Theorem} \label{sec:extension-theorem}

In this subsection we present our Extension Theorem that uses our smoothness definition for a single auction and specific agent type, in order to derive bounds on the POA of simultaneous auctions with feasible reserve prices and fractionally subadditive valuation functions. 

We first introduce some additional notation. 
Given fractionally subadditive valuation functions $\vec{v} = (v_i)_{i \in N} \in \mathcal{V}_{\xos}$ and an optimal liquid welfare allocation $\vec{x}^* = \vec{x}^*(\vec{v})$. 
%Also, let $\opt(\vec{v}) = \sw(\vec{x}^*)$ denote the optimal liquid welfare.
As each valuation function $v_i$ satisfies the XOS property as defined in Definition~\ref{def:val-classes}, we can define $(v^*_{ij})_{j \in M}$ as the \emph{additive representatives} of $i \in N$ with respect to their optimal allocation $\vec{x}^*_i$.\footnote{Note that for additive valuations these representatives simply coincide with the input valuations.}
For each $i \in N$, the following two properties follow directly from Definition~\ref{def:val-classes}: 
\begin{itemize} 
\setlength\itemindent{2.5em} 
  \item[XOS1:] $v_i(\vec{x}^*_i) = \sum_{j \in M} v^*_{ij} x^*_{ij}$. 
  \item[XOS2:] for any allocation $x_i \subseteq M$, it holds that $v_i(\vec{x}_i) \ge \sum_{j \in M} v^*_{ij} x_{ij}$.
\end{itemize}
To see the latter, recall that the additive representatives $(v_{ij})_{j \in M}$ of $i$ with respect to $\vec{x}_i$ are chosen as maximizers from the class $\mathcal{L}_i \ni (v^*_{ij})_{j \in M}$, and thus $v_i(\vec{x}_i) = \sum_{j \in M} v_{ij} x_{ij} \ge \sum_{j \in M} v^*_{ij} x_{ij}$.
We will use $\vec{v}^{*} = (v^*_i)_{i \in N}$ to refer to the additive valuation functions obtained from the representatives, i.e., we define $v^*_i(\vec{x}_i) = \sum_{j \in M} v^*_{ij} x_{ij}$ for any allocation $\vec{x}_i$. 
Given these representatives, we can basically regard each valuation function $v_i$ as being additive in the proof of the Extension Theorem.
To this end, when we refer to an auction $\mech_j$ as $(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$-smooth for each type $\type \in T$, this is with respect to the valuations $\vec{v}^*$ in the remainder of this section. 

Before stating our Extension Theorem, we introduce the notion of \emph{calibration vectors}, which will be crucial in the proof. 

\begin{definition}[Calibration Vectors]\label{def:Xi}
Let $T$ be a set of different agent types with sensitivities $\vec{\sigma} = (\sigma_{\type})_{\type \in T} \in [0,1]^{|T|}$ and let $\vec{\mu} = (\mu_{\type})_{\type \in T}$ be such that $\mu_{\type} >0$ for each $\type \in T$. 
%Assume that each auction $\mech_j$ of $\mech$ is $(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$-smooth for each type $\type \in T$. 
We define the set of \emph{calibration vectors} $\mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$ as follows: 
 \begin{equation}\label{equation:Xi-def}
     \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma}) = 
     \ssetl{\vec{\delta} \in (0,1]^{|T|}}{  
     \max_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type} \mu_{\type} )  + \max_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type}(1-\sens_{\type})) 
     \leq 1}.
 \end{equation}
\end{definition}

We later show in Lemma~\ref{lemma:optimal-xi} in Section \ref{sec:POA-reveal} that $\mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$ is always non-empty.
We can now state the main result of this section:


\begin{theorem}[Extension Theorem]
\label{theorem:template}
Consider the class of simultaneous first-price auctions with fractionally subadditive valuations $\vec{v} \in \mathcal{V}_{\xos}$ and feasible reserve prices. 
Let $T$ be the set of different agent types with sensitivities $\vec{\sigma}$.
Assume that, for each type $\type \in T$, each auction $\mech_j$ of $\mech$ is $(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$-smooth.
Then, the price of anarchy of well-supported coarse correlated equilibria is:
\[
\cce\text{-}\poa(\mathcal{V}_{\xos}) \le 
\left(\max_{\vec{\delta} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})}\min_{\type \in T} \delta_{\type}\lambda_{\type}\right)^{-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
Note that the reserve price $r_j$ of an auction $j$ is assumed to be feasible with respect to the additive representatives, i.e., $r_j = \eta_j v_{\rw(j)j}^*$ for some $\eta_j \in [0,1)$.

The following corollary and the subsequent lemma will be useful in the proof of the Extension Theorem.

\begin{corollary} \label{cor:deviation-independent}
    Consider a random bid profile $\B'_i$ of agent $i$ that is ROI-restricted. 
    Let $\B_{-i}$ be an arbitrary probability distribution over $D_{-i}$ of the other agents. 
    Then, $(\B'_i, \B_{-i}) \in \mathcal{R}_i$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}{Proof}
We have:
\begin{align*}
\pe\left[p_{i}(\B'_i, \B_{-i})\right] 
& = \int_{D_{-i}} f_{\B_{-i}}(\b_{-i}) \cdot \pe[p_i(\B'_i, \b_{-i})] d \b_{-i} \\
& \le \int_{D_{-i}} f_{\vec{B}_{-i}}(\b_{-i}) \cdot \pe[ v_i(x_i(\B'_i, \b_{-i}))] d \b_{-i} 
= \pe
[v_i(\vec{x}_i(\B'_i, \vec{B}_{-i}))],
\end{align*}
where, the inequality follows because $\vec{B}'_i$ is ROI-restricted.
\end{proof}


\begin{lemma} \label{lem:comp}
Consider the simultaneous first-price auctions with fractionally subadditive valuations $\vec{v} \in \mathcal{V}_{\xos}$ and feasible reserve prices. 
Let $T$ be the set of different agent types.
Assume that, for each type $\type \in T$, each auction $\mech_j$ of $\mech$ is $(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$-smooth.
Let $\B$ be a well-supported coarse correlated equilibrium.
Then, for each $\type \in T$ it holds that:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lambda-mu-per-groupMT}
\sum_{i \in N_{\type} }\pe\left[g_i(\B)\right] 
\ge 
\sum_{j \in M: \rw(j) \in N_{\type}} \lambda_{\type} v^*_{\rw(j)j} - \mu_{\type} \pe\left[p_{\aw(j)j}(\vec{B})\right].
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}{Proof} 
Consider some agent $i \in N_{\type}$ of type $\type \in T$ and assume that $i$ is the rightful winner of auction $j$, i.e., $i = \rw(j)$. 
There exists a ROI-restricted random bid $B'_{ij}$ such that for each bid profile $\vec{b}_j$ with $x_j(\vec{b}_j) \neq \vec{0}$, we have:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:smooth-local-rw}
\pe\left[\z_{i}\left(B'_{ij}, (\vec{b}_{j})_{-i}\right)\right] \geq \lambda_{\type}  v^*_{\rw(j)j} - \mu_{\type}  p_{\aw(j)j}(\vec{b}_j),
\end{equation}
as $\mech_j$ is $(\lambda_{\type}, \mu_{\type})$-smooth by assumption.
We define a random deviation $\vec{B}'_i$ of agent $i$ for the global mechanism $\mech$ by simply drawing a bid $b'_{ij}$ for each auction $j \in M$ independently according to $B'_{ij}$ if $i = \rw(j)$ is the rightful winner of $j$, and let $B'_{ij} = b'_{ij} = 0$ deterministically if $i \neq \rw(j)$. 
For each bid profile $\b_{-i}$, we have:
\begin{align*}
\pe\left[p_{i}(\B'_{i}, \b_{-i})\right] 
&= \pe\left[\sum_{ \ j \in M} p_{ij}(B'_{ij}, (\b_j)_{-i})\right] 
\le \pe\left[\sum_{ \ j \in M} v^*_{ij} x_{ij}(B'_{ij}, (\b_j)_{-i})\right] \\
&= \pe\left[v^*_{i}(x_{i}(\B'_{i}, \b_{-i}))\right] \le \pe[ v_{i}(x_{i}(\B'_{i}, \b_{-i}))],
\end{align*}
where the inequality holds because $B'_{ij}$ is ROI-restricted for each $j$ (which holds trivially also for all auctions $j$ with $i \neq \rw(j)$). 
Note that the second equality holds by the definition of $v^*_i$, and the last inequality holds by the property XOS2.
We conclude that $\B'_i$ is ROI-restricted for each agent $i$. 

Given an arbitrary bid profile $\b$ with $\vec{x}(\vec{b}) \neq \vec{0}$, from the above we conclude that:
\begin{align}
\pe[\z_{i}(\vec{B'}_{i}, \vec{b}_{-i})] 
& 
 = \sum_{j \in M}\pe\left[ \z_{i}\left(B'_{ij}, (\vec{b}_{j})_{-i}\right)\right] 
\geq \sum_{{j \in M: \rw(j) = i}} \lambda_{\type}  v^*_{\rw(j)j} - \mu_{\type}  p_{\aw(j)j}(\vec{b})\label{eq:lambda-mu-per-agent}.
\end{align}
Here, the inequality holds by applying \eqref{eq:smooth-local-rw} for all auctions $j$ in which $i = \rw(j)$, and using that the expected gain of $i$ is non-negative for all auctions $j$ in which $i \neq \rw(j)$. 

Let $\vec{B}$ be a well-supported CCE.
As argued above, the random deviation $\vec{B}_i'$ is ROI-restricted for every $i \in N_{\type}$. By Corollary \ref{cor:deviation-independent}, we have that $(\vec{B}_{i}', \vec{B}_{-i}) \in \mathcal{R}_i$, and so the CCE condition \eqref{eq:cce-def} of agent $i$ with respect to the deviation $\B'_i$ can be applied. Using this, we obtain:
\begin{align*}
\pe\left[g_i(\B)\right] 
&\ge \pe\left[g_i(\B'_i, \B_{-i})\right] 
= \pe\left[\pe_{\B'_i}\left[g_i(\B'_i, \B_{-i})\right]\right] \\
&\ge 
\sum_{\substack{j \in M:\rw(j) = i}} \lambda_{\type}  v^*_{\rw(j)j} - \mu_{\type}  \pe\left[p_{\aw(j)j}(\B)
\right].
\end{align*}
Here, the second inequality follows because \eqref{eq:lambda-mu-per-agent} holds for every $\b \in \supp(\B)$ (note that $\vec{x}(\b) \neq \vec{0}$ because $\B$ is well-supported), and we can thus take the expectation with respect to $\B$ on both sides. 
Finally, summing the above inequality over all agents $i \in N_{\type}$ proves the claim. 
\end{proof}

We can now complete the proof of the Extension Theorem.
\medskip

\begin{proof}{Proof of Theorem~\ref{theorem:template}}
Let $\B$ be a well-supported CCE. 
Let $\vec{\delta} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma}) \subseteq (0,1]^{|T|}$ be an arbitrary calibration vector. Note that such a vector is guaranteed to exist by Lemma \ref{lemma:optimal-xi}. 
We have:
\begin{align}
    \pe[\sw(\vec{B})] 
    & = \sum_{\type \in T}  \sum_{i \in  N_{\type}} \delta_{\type} \pe \left[v_i(x_i(\B))\right] + (1-\delta_{\type}) \pe \left[ v_i(x_i(\B))\right] \nonumber \\
    & 
    \ge \sum_{\type \in T} \sum_{i \in  N_{\type}} \delta_{\type} \pe \left[v_i(x_i(\B))\right] + (1-\delta_{\type}) \pe \left[p_i(\B)\right] \nonumber \\   
    & = \sum_{\type \in T} \sum_{i \in  N_{\type}} \delta_{\type}  \pe \left[g_i(\B)\right] + (1-\delta_{\type} + \delta_{\type}\sens_{\type}) \pe \left[p_i(\B)\right] \nonumber \\       
    & \ge \sum_{\type \in T} \sum_{i \in  N_{\type}} \delta_{\type}  \pe \left[g_i(\B)\right] + \left(1- \max_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type}(1-\sens_{\type}))\right) \sum_{\type \in T} \sum_{i \in N_{\type}} \pe \left[p_i(\B)\right] \nonumber \\     
    & = \sum_{\type \in T} \sum_{i \in  N_{\type}} \delta_{\type}  \pe \left[g_i(\B)\right] 
    + 
    \left(1- \max_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type}(1-\sens_{\type}))\right) \pe \left[\sum_{i \in N} p_i(\B)\right]. \label{eq:SWdecomp} 
\end{align}
Here, the first inequality is obtained by using the ROI constraint \eqref{eq:ROI} for each agent $i \in N$. 
The last equality holds because $\set{N_{\type}}_{\type \in T}$ is a partition of $N$. 

We continue with lower bounding the first part of \eqref{eq:SWdecomp}. Using Lemma~\ref{lem:comp}, we obtain that: 
\begin{align}
    \sum_{\type \in T} \sum_{i \in  N_{\type}} \delta_{\type} \pe \left[g_i(\B)\right] 
    & 
    \ge 
    \sum_{\type \in T} 
    \sum_{\substack{j \in M: \\ \rw(j) \in N_{\type}}} \delta_{\type}\lambda_{\type} v^*_{\rw(j)j} - \delta_{\type} \mu_{\type} \pe\left[p_{\aw(j)j}(\vec{B})\right]
    \nonumber \\    
    & 
    \ge 
    \sum_{\type \in T} 
    \sum_{\substack{j \in M: \\ \rw(j) \in N_{\type}}} \min_{\type \in T} (    \delta_{\type}\lambda_{\type} )  v^*_{\rw(j)j} 
    - \max_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type} \mu_{\type} ) \pe\left[p_{\aw(j)j}(\vec{B})\right]
    \nonumber \\          
    & =     
    \min_{\type \in T} (    \delta_{\type}\lambda_{\type} ) 
    \sw(\vec{x}^*) 
    - 
    \max_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type} \mu_{\type} )
    \pe\left[\sum_{\ i \in N} p_i(\B)\right].
    \label{eq:smoothness-total}
\end{align}
were the last equality follows from property XOS1. 
Using \eqref{eq:smoothness-total}, we expand \eqref{eq:SWdecomp} and finally obtain that:
\begin{align*}
    \pe[\sw(\vec{B})] 
    & 
    \geq 
    \min_{\type \in T} (    \delta_{\type}\lambda_{\type} ) 
    \opt( \vec{v}) \nonumber \\
    &+ 
    \left(1 - \max_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type} \mu_{\type} ) - \max_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type}(1- \sens_{\type}))  \right)
    \pe \left[\sum_{\ i \in N} p_i(\B)\right] \\
    & 
    \ge 
    \min_{\type \in T} (    \delta_{\type}\lambda_{\type} ) 
    \opt(\vec{v}), 
\end{align*}
where the second inequality holds because $\vec{\delta} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$ (see Definition~\ref{def:Xi}). 
By rearranging terms and selecting a calibration vector $\vec{\delta} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$ that maximizes $\min_{\type \in T} (\delta_{\type}\lambda_{\type})$, the proof follows.  
\end{proof}

Note that in the proof above, the whole purpose of our calibration vector was to eventually lower bound the total payments in the final expression by $0$. This also explains the specific definition of the feasibility constraint of $\mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$ in \eqref{equation:Xi-def}. 


\section{A POA-Revealing Mathematical Program} \label{sec:POA-reveal}

We present our approach to determine the price of anarchy through the Extension Theorem. 
A crucial building block for deriving our POA-revealing mathematical program is the characterization of optimal calibration vectors given below.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:optimal-xi}
Let $T$ be a set of different agent types with sensitivities $\vec{\sigma} = (\sigma_{\type})_{\type \in T} \in [0,1]^{|T|}$. Let $\vec{\mu} = (\mu_{\type})_{\type \in T}$ and $\vec{\lambda} = (\lambda_{\type})_{\type \in T}$ be such that $\mu_{\type} >0$ and $\lambda_{\type} >0$ for each $\type \in T$. 
Then, $\max_{\vec{\delta} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})}\min_{\vec{\type} \in T}\lambda_{\type}\delta_{\type}=O$, where:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:optimal-xi-lambda-mu}
    O = \min\left\{\min_{\type \in T} \lambda_{\type}, \left(\max_{\type \in T}\left(\frac{\mu_{\type}}{\lambda_{\type}}\right) 
    + \max_{\type \in T}\left(\frac{1-\sens_{\type}}{\lambda_{\type}}\right) \right)^{-1}\right\}.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}{Proof}
Define $\vec{\delta}'$ such that $\delta'_{\type} = {O}/{\lambda_{\type}}$ for each $\type \in T$. We first show that $\vec{\delta}' \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$.
First, note that $\vec{\delta}' \in (0,1]^{|T|}$, since for each $\type \in T$ it holds that:
\begin{equation*}
    \delta'_{\type} 
    = \frac{O}{\lambda_{\type}} 
    \leq \frac{\min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}}{\lambda_{\type}} \leq 1,
\end{equation*}
and $\delta'_{\type} >0$ as $O >0$.
Furthermore, $\vec{\delta}'$ satisfies:
\begin{align*}
    \max_{ \type \in T} \left(\delta'_{\type} \mu_{\type} \right) + \max_{\type \in T} \left(\delta'_{\type} \left(1 - \sens_{\type} \right) \right) 
    &=\max_{ \type \in T} \left(\frac{O}{\lambda_{\type}} \mu_{\type} \right) 
    + \max_{\type \in T} \left(\frac{O}{\lambda_{\type}} \left(1 - \sens_{\type} \right) \right) \\
    &=O \cdot \left(  \max_{ \type \in T} \left(\frac{\mu_{\type}}{\lambda_{\type}} \right) + \max_{\type \in T} \left(\frac{1 - \sens_{\type} }{\lambda_{\type}}  \right) \right)
    \le 1,
\end{align*}
where the last inequality follows from \eqref{eq:optimal-xi-lambda-mu}.
Hence, $\vec{\delta}' \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$ and therefore $\min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}\delta'_{\type}=O$.

To complete the proof, we need to show that $\max_{\vec{\delta} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu},\vec{\sigma})}\min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}\delta_{\type}=O$. Towards a contradiction, assume that there exists a $\bar{\vec{\delta}} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$ with:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:contradiction-opt}
    \min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}\bar{\delta}_{\type}>O.
\end{equation}
We distinguish two cases for the value of $O$.

\noindent \underline{Case 1:} $O=\min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}$. In this case we get the following contradiction: 
\[
\min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type} = O < \min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}\bar{\delta}_{\type} \leq \min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type},
\]
where the equality and first inequality hold by assumption. The second inequality follows as $\bar{\vec{\delta}} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$, so $\bar{\delta}_{\type} \le 1$ for all $\type \in T$.

\noindent \underline{Case 2:} $O=\left(\max_{\type \in T}\left(\frac{\mu_{\type}}{\lambda_{\type}}\right) 
    + \max_{\type \in T}\left(\frac{1-\sens_{\type}}{\lambda_{\type}}\right) \right)^{-1}$. Let  $\hat{\type}=\argmax _{\type \in T} \mu_{\type} / \lambda_{\type}$ and $\tilde{\type}=\argmax_{\type \in T} (1-\sens_{\type}) / \lambda_{\type}$.  
    In this case we get the following contradiction:
    \begin{align*}
        1< \left(\frac{\mu_{\hat{\type}}}{\lambda_{\hat{\type}}}\ 
    + \frac{1-\sens_{\tilde{\type}}}{\lambda_{\tilde{\type}}}\right)\cdot \min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}\bar{\delta}_{\type}
    &= \frac{\mu_{\hat{\type}}}{\lambda_{\hat{\type}}}\cdot \min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}\bar{\delta}_{\type} 
    + \frac{1-\sens_{\tilde{\type}}}{\lambda_{\tilde{\type}}} \cdot \min_{\type \in T}\lambda_{\type}\bar{\delta}_{\type}\\
    &\leq\mu_{\hat{\type}}\bar{\delta}_{\hat{\type}} +\left(1-\sens_{\tilde{\type}} \right)\bar{\delta}_{\tilde{\type}}\\
    &\leq \max_{\type \in T}\left( \mu_{\type}\bar{\delta}_{\type} \right) + \max_{\type \in T}\left( \left(1-\sens_{\type}\right)\bar{\delta}_{\type} \right)\\
    &\leq 1.
    \end{align*}
Here, the first inequality holds by assumption and the last inequality holds since $\vec{\bar{\delta}} \in \mathcal{C}(\vec{\mu}, \vec{\sigma})$. This concludes the proof.
\end{proof}

We use the above lemma together with our smoothness lemmas to derive a mathematical program that captures a bound on the POA. We refer to this program as the \emph{POA-revealing mathematical program (\poarmp)}. 
Let $T$ be the set of different agent types with sensitivities $\vec{\sigma} \in [0,1]^{|T|}$. 
Lemmas \ref{lem:fpa-smoothness-key-2} and \ref{lem:fpa-smoothness-key-1} then imply the following smoothness parameters, depending on the respective agent type $\type \in T$ and assuming that $\eta = \min_{j \in M} \eta_j \in [0,1)$. 
\begin{alignat}{3}
    & \textstyle
    \lambda_{\type} = \mu_{\type}\left(1 - \frac{1 - {\eta}}{e^{1/{\mu_{\type}}}}\right)
    \quad 
    && \textstyle
    \mu_{\type} > 0
    \quad
    && \text{$\forall \type \in T$: $\sens_{\type} = 1$}    
    \label{eq:key-lemma-0-constraint}   
    \\[-.5ex]    
    & \textstyle
    \lambda_{\type} = \frac{\mu_{\type}}{\sens_{\type}}\left(1 - \frac{1 - {\sens_{\type}\eta}}{e^{\sens_{\type}/{\mu_{\type}}}}\right)
    \quad 
    && \textstyle
    \mu_{\type} \ge 
    \sens_{\type}
    \left(    
    \ln\left(\frac{1-{\sens_{\type}\eta}}{1- \sens_{\type}} \right)\right)^{-1}   
    \quad
    && \textstyle
    \text{$\forall \type \in T$: $\sens_{\type} \in (0,1)$} 
    \label{eq:key-lemma-1-constraint}
    \\[.5ex]
    & \textstyle
    \lambda_{\type} = \mu_{\type}
    \quad 
    && \textstyle
%    \text{with}\quad
    \mu_{\type} \in \left (0, {\frac{1}{1-\eta}} \right]
    \quad
    && \textstyle
    \text{$\forall \type \in T$: $\sens_{\type} = 0$} \label{eq:key-lemma-2-constraint} 
\end{alignat}

To obtain a bound on the POA, we determine a vector $\vec{\mu} = (\mu_{\type})_{\type \in T}$ that maximizes the expression in \eqref{eq:optimal-xi-lambda-mu} subject to the constraints above. 
Thus, our $\poarmp$ is:
\begin{align*}
\poarmp \ &= \ \max
\ 
\min \left\{ 
        \min_{\type \in T} \lambda_{\type}, 
        \left( 
            \max_{\substack{\type \in T}} \left (\frac{\mu_{\type} }{\lambda_{\type}} \right)
            + \max_{\type \in T} \left (\frac{1 - \sens_{\type} }{\lambda_{\type}} 
        \right) \right )^{-1} 
    \right\}  \\
    &\text{subject to}\  
    \text{\eqref{eq:key-lemma-0-constraint}--\eqref{eq:key-lemma-2-constraint}}.
\notag
\end{align*}

\subsection{Solving \poarmp\ by Partitioning Agent Types}\label{subsec:solving-poarmp}

We are left to determine a lower bound on the optimal value of $\poarmp$.
The main technical challenge is to find a solution analytically. 
Below, we provide a policy to define a solution $\vec{\mu}$ of $\poarmp$. 
We first focus on the case without reserve prices, i.e., $\eta = 0$ in \eqref{eq:key-lemma-0-constraint}--\eqref{eq:key-lemma-2-constraint}.

The main idea is to partition the agent types $T$ of the rightful winners into two classes $H_{\omega}$ (high) and $L_{\omega}$ (low), where $\omega$ is a separation parameter.
We then define $\mu_{\type}$ depending on the class $\type$ belongs to. 
Intuitively, $H_{\omega}$ contains agent types that are structurally close to utility maximizers, while $L_{\omega}$ contains agent types that are structurally close to value maximizers (see also Figure \ref{fig:partition}).

