\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Reinsurance markets play a critical role in the global risk transfer ecosystem by enabling insurance carriers to offload portions of their exposure to third-party capital providers. This process facilitates portfolio diversification, enhances capital efficiency, and stabilizes insurer solvency in the face of large-scale and low-frequency events such as natural disasters or systemic shocks \cite{dias2003insurance, cummins2001reinsurance}. Despite its importance, reinsurance treaty placement remains characterized by persistent inefficiencies: high transaction costs, limited price transparency, and strategic frictions stemming from broker intermediation \cite{cummings2021economics, grace2004ereinsure}.

The prevailing placement model relies on bilateral negotiations facilitated by brokers who control the flow of information between insurers and reinsurers. Three institutional features reinforce inefficiencies in this arrangement. First, \textit{broker intermediation} restricts reinsurers' access to underlying underwriting data, impeding independent pricing and risk assessment. Second, \textit{incumbent advantage} privileges reinsurers with prior participation in the treaty, often at the expense of competitive newcomers. Third, \textit{last-look privilege} permits selected reinsurers to revise their bids after viewing competitors’ quotes. These market frictions distort efficient price discovery, inhibit dynamic learning, and raise barriers to entry.

The need for improved placement mechanisms is becoming increasingly urgent due to heightened systemic risk from climate volatility, pandemic-related losses, and macroeconomic uncertainty \cite{mills2005insurance, hartwig2020covid, knight2022reinsurance}. While workflow digitization platforms such as \textit{eReinsure} have streamlined administrative aspects of treaty placement, they do not address the deeper problem of strategic inefficiency nor do they incorporate adaptive decision-making capabilities \cite{boehm2004web}.

In parallel domains such as financial trading and supply chain contracting, the application of reinforcement learning and autonomous agents has led to significant gains in efficiency and responsiveness under uncertainty \cite{silver2016mastering, buehler2019deep}. Motivated by these advances, this paper investigates whether multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) can serve as a viable market design mechanism in reinsurance---specifically, whether it can improve the allocation of treaty capital relative to traditional broker-mediated processes.

\textbf{We introduce a novel MARL-based framework for reinsurance treaty bidding}, in which reinsurers are modeled as autonomous agents that learn to optimize dynamic bidding strategies through repeated interactions in a simulated placement environment. Each agent jointly maximizes underwriting profit, tail risk performance (as measured by Conditional Value-at-Risk, CVaR \cite{artzner1999coherent}), and competitive positioning. The simulation incorporates key institutional features of the market, including sequential bid solicitations by insurers, asymmetric access to historical loss data, and insurer-specific utility functions that evaluate bids across multiple attributes such as pricing, capital relief, and counterparty diversification \cite{wang2018optimal, embrechts1997modelling}.

This framework enables a comparative evaluation of MARL-based bidding against conventional benchmarks, including static actuarial pricing and rule-based heuristics. More broadly, it contributes to the design of algorithmically-mediated markets by examining how learning agents can navigate complex environments characterized by information frictions, competition, and risk constraints.

\subsection*{Summary of Contributions}

\begin{itemize}
    \item We formulate reinsurance treaty placement as a sequential, strategic interaction among learning agents, grounded in a multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) framework that reflects real-world market frictions and institutional constraints.

    \item We develop a high-fidelity simulation engine that replicates treaty solicitation, asymmetric information access, and insurer multi-attribute utility scoring. This environment allows for rigorous experimentation across a range of market conditions.

    \item We construct a domain-specific reward function tailored to reinsurance objectives---balancing profit, tail risk (CVaR), and bid competitiveness---and demonstrate how agents learn optimal strategies under these tradeoffs.

    \item We show that MARL-based bidding significantly outperforms actuarial and heuristic baselines in profitability, Sharpe ratio, CVaR reduction, and bid success rate. Robustness is further confirmed through hyperparameter sweeps, ablation studies, and stress tests simulating catastrophe shocks and capital constraints.

    \item We identify managerial and policy implications, including the potential for MARL to improve market transparency, reduce placement costs, and enable more responsive and resilient risk transfer mechanisms. We also discuss adjacent applications in catastrophe bond markets, sovereign risk auctions, and infrastructure procurement.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of the paper is structured as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related_work} situates this study within the broader literature on reinsurance market structure, auction theory, and algorithmic market design. Section~\ref{sec:problem_formulation} formalizes the treaty bidding environment and the agents’ optimization objectives. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} describes the simulation architecture, learning algorithms, and evaluation procedures. Section~\ref{sec:results_analysis} reports empirical results, including benchmark comparisons, sensitivity analyses, and stress test scenarios. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} examines the managerial and regulatory implications of the findings. Section~\ref{sec:limitations_future_work} discusses limitations and outlines directions for future research. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} summarizes the paper’s key contributions and concludes.




