\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

Despite substantial research in reinsurance modeling and reinforcement learning across financial domains, no prior study has applied dynamic, multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) to the treaty bidding problem. This section reviews foundational approaches from insurance economics, auction theory, and AI-driven market design---highlighting how our proposed framework addresses persistent limitations in adaptivity, institutional realism, and strategic competition.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Modeling Reinsurance Markets}

Classical models of reinsurance treaty placement fall into two broad categories: game-theoretic formulations and portfolio optimization methods. Equilibrium models such as Nash or Bayesian-Nash games describe reinsurer behavior under idealized assumptions of complete information and rationality \cite{dias2003insurance, cummins2001reinsurance}. While analytically tractable, these models abstract away from critical institutional frictions such as broker intermediation, last-look bidding rights, and asymmetric access to treaty-specific information.

A separate literature frames reinsurance structuring as a single-period portfolio optimization problem. These models optimize risk-based objectives (e.g., VaR or CVaR) under regulatory and capital constraints from the insurer’s perspective \cite{cao2010optimal, wang2018optimal, cai2007optimal}. However, they typically ignore the strategic behavior of reinsurers, and cannot capture iterative, competitive dynamics or adaptive learning over repeated treaty auctions.

Although digital platforms like \textit{eReinsure} have improved document workflows and quote standardization, empirical studies report that reinsurance markets continue to lag behind other sectors in algorithmic adoption and AI integration \cite{cummings2021economics, ng2023insurtech}. Negotiation and pricing still largely depend on human brokers, making automation of strategic behavior a critical next step.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Multi-Agent Learning in Strategic Markets}

Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) provides a powerful modeling paradigm for competitive decision-making in decentralized and partially observable environments. Recent studies have applied MARL to domains such as electronic trading \cite{yang2020agent}, electricity markets \cite{morstyn2020multi}, ad auctions \cite{jin2020learning}, and negotiation games \cite{li2023strategic}. Notable algorithmic innovations include centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE) \cite{foerster2016learning}, opponent modeling \cite{he2016opponent}, and population-based policy evolution \cite{openai2020emergent}.

In financial contexts, MARL has been used to study price formation, liquidity provision, and competitive bidding. For example, \cite{capobianco2023learning} develop a MARL system for decentralized exchange market making, and \cite{ye2024cooperative} propose cooperative bidding under budget constraints in ad auctions. These contributions show that MARL can model complex market dynamics. Yet to date, no MARL framework has been applied to treaty reinsurance---despite its importance as a repeated, high-stakes, and friction-laden bidding process.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Reinforcement Learning in Finance and Insurtech}

Beyond multi-agent contexts, reinforcement learning (RL) has found broad applications in finance, particularly in portfolio allocation \cite{jiang2017deep}, trading signal generation \cite{nevmyvaka2006reinforcement}, market making \cite{spooner2018market}, and derivatives pricing \cite{xu2020pricing}. In the insurance sector, RL has supported underwriting, claims handling, and fraud detection \cite{albrecher2017machine, buehler2019deep, bauer2021machine}---though these remain largely prediction-driven, single-agent use cases.

The growing Insurtech literature incorporates machine learning for pricing, reserving, and risk scoring, but few contributions adopt reinforcement learning, and none model the strategic interaction among reinsurers \cite{berns2022ai, ng2023insurtech}. Thus, treaty placement remains underserved by both academic research and practical decision support systems.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{Synthesis and Research Gap}

To summarize:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Prior models rely on static or equilibrium frameworks that do not accommodate real-world frictions such as broker influence, asymmetric information, and last-look advantages.
    \item Most approaches treat insurers as optimizing agents but neglect strategic interaction among reinsurers bidding for treaty share.
    \item No existing studies use MARL to simulate dynamic competition under institutional constraints in treaty reinsurance.
\end{itemize}

This paper addresses these gaps by introducing a MARL-based framework for treaty bidding. Reinsurance agents learn bidding policies through repeated interaction in a simulated environment that reflects key institutional realities---including incumbent favoritism, limited observability, and broker-mediated quoting. Our formulation enables a shift from static optimization to dynamic market design.

To clarify this distinction, Figure~\ref{fig:taxonomy} illustrates the conceptual contrast between traditional modeling pipelines and our proposed approach. On the left, classical economic models proceed from equilibrium theory to portfolio-level decisions, culminating in manual negotiation facilitated by brokers. On the right, our MARL-based framework enables adaptive policy learning through agent interaction, simulation of market frictions, and autonomous pricing decisions based on profitability, risk-adjusted return, and market share.

