\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:problem_formulation}

We model reinsurance treaty placement as a high-stakes, sequential decision-making process involving multiple autonomous reinsurers. To capture its decentralized, strategic, and partially observed nature, we formulate the problem as a \textit{Partially Observable Markov Game} (POMG) \cite{oliehoek2016concise, busoniu2008comprehensive}, a generalization of Markov decision processes to multi-agent settings.

Each agent represents a reinsurer learning a policy through repeated bidding episodes. The environment models an insurer issuing treaties, reinsurers submitting bids, and a centralized mechanism selecting winners. Unlike classical equilibrium models, this formulation supports adaptive learning, bounded rationality, and endogenous market feedback under realistic institutional frictions.

We provide an overview of this process in Figure~\ref{fig:environment_architecture}, which illustrates the bid generation, selection, and feedback cycle that drives learning across agents and time.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% section 3.1
\subsection{Market Environment}
\label{subsec:environment}

Let $\mathcal{I} = \{1, \ldots, N\}$ denote the set of reinsurance agents. At each episode $t$, a new treaty is issued and agents simultaneously submit bids.

\paragraph{Global State $\mathcal{S}$:} The environment’s true state $s_t \in \mathcal{S}$ encodes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Treaty features:} loss distribution, line of business, attachment/limit, exposure, and retention.
    \item \textbf{Market context:} macroeconomic signals, incumbent status, prior placements, and reinsurer count.
\end{itemize}

\noindent \textit{Economic interpretation:} These variables represent market signals analogous to private-value and common-value components in auction theory, informing strategic bidding under uncertainty.

\paragraph{Local Observations $\mathcal{O}_i$:} Each agent $i$ receives a private observation $o_i(t)$, including treaty-specific metadata and the agent’s own portfolio performance metrics, but not competitor bids or the full global state $s_t$. This reflects realistic information constraints faced by reinsurers in actual treaty markets.

\paragraph{Action Space $\mathcal{A}_i$:} Agent actions $a_i(t) \in \mathcal{A}_i$ parameterize bids according to standard market conventions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Excess-of-loss:} Premium rate, attachment point, and coverage limit.
    \item \textbf{Quota-share:} Quota percentage and ceding commission rate.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Insurer Utility Function:} Submitted bids $b_i$ are evaluated by the insurer based on a utility function:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{U}(b_i) = \text{ExpectedNetBenefit}(b_i) - \lambda_{\text{insurer}} \cdot \text{RiskExposure}(b_i),
\label{eq:insurer_utility}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{\text{insurer}}$ controls the insurer’s risk aversion. The term \textit{ExpectedNetBenefit} represents the net premium inflow minus expected claims, while \textit{RiskExposure} captures tail volatility using metrics such as Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR). When bid scores fall within an $\epsilon$ margin of the maximum, ties are broken in favor of incumbent reinsurers---reflecting the industry’s preference for continuity and stable underwriting relationships.\footnote{In traditional reinsurance markets, brokers coordinate treaty placement by soliciting quotes from reinsurers and facilitating negotiations. Our MARL-based framework abstracts this process by modeling direct interactions between insurers and agents, with centralized selection and explicit institutional features such as incumbent preference and asymmetric observability. While simplified, this design preserves core economic incentives and competitive dynamics, enabling scalable, realistic agent-based learning.}


