% section 3.3
\subsection{Learning Setup}
\label{subsec:learning_framework}

To train reinsurance agents in a competitive, partially observable environment, we adopt the \textit{Centralized Training with Decentralized Execution} (CTDE) framework~\cite{foerster2016learning}. This approach enables agents to access global state information during training---facilitating coordinated credit assignment and stable policy updates---while enforcing decentralized decision-making at execution time based solely on local observations. This design mirrors the informational asymmetries faced by real-world reinsurers.

\subsubsection{Algorithms Compared.}
We evaluate three state-of-the-art actor-critic algorithms for multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL), all tailored to continuous action spaces and strategic bidding dynamics:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Multi-Agent Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (MADDPG)}~\cite{lowe2017multi}: An extension of DDPG with decentralized actors and a centralized critic conditioned on joint actions. While effective in low-noise control tasks, MADDPG is often unstable in highly non-stationary environments such as competitive bidding.

    \item \textbf{Multi-Agent Twin Delayed DDPG (MATD3)}~\cite{zhang2021multi}: Builds upon MADDPG with twin critics and policy smoothing to reduce Q-value overestimation. MATD3 improves robustness and sample efficiency, especially under stochastic rewards.

    \item \textbf{Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization (MAPPO)}~\cite{yu2021surprising}: Combines PPO’s clipped surrogate objective with centralized critics. MAPPO provides greater training stability, especially under adversarial interactions and partial observability. It has demonstrated strong empirical performance across standard MARL benchmarks.
\end{itemize}

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curve_comparison}, MAPPO consistently achieves faster convergence, more stable learning curves, and better generalization across random seeds compared to MADDPG and MATD3. Its clipped policy updates and advantage normalization mitigate instability from opponent-induced non-stationarity. Based on these results, we adopt MAPPO as the default algorithm for all subsequent experiments.\footnote{Experiments were implemented in PyMARL with Stable Baselines and conducted locally on a MacBook Pro (Apple M2 chip), demonstrating the feasibility of training MARL agents on consumer hardware.}


\subsubsection{Environment Characteristics.}
Our reinsurance bidding environment introduces domain-specific challenges that motivate algorithmic care:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{High-dimensional input and action spaces:} Each agent encodes treaty metadata and internal portfolio context to generate continuous-valued bids, including premium rate, attachment, and limit.

    \item \textbf{Episodic interaction with structured resets:} Each episode simulates a full treaty placement cycle---starting from issuance and ending in reward allocation---consistent with real-world quarterly or annual reinsurance operations (see Figure~\ref{fig:episode_timeline}).

    \item \textbf{Strategic coupling:} Agent outcomes are interdependent due to overlapping exposures and competitive pricing. This creates a highly non-stationary learning environment where naïve policies quickly become obsolete.
\end{itemize}

These characteristics make reinsurance treaty placement a natural testbed for evaluating stability and coordination in competitive MARL systems.

