\section{System Design and Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

This section presents the architectural and methodological foundation of our multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) framework for modeling competitive treaty reinsurance bidding. We describe the simulation environment that captures key structural and stochastic features of real-world reinsurance markets. We then detail the learning setup, including algorithmic choices, training protocols, and environment dynamics. Baseline strategies---both actuarial and heuristic---are introduced to contextualize performance benchmarks. Finally, we outline a suite of stress-testing scenarios designed to assess robustness under economic shocks and adversarial conditions.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% section 4.1
\subsection{Simulation Environment}
\label{subsec:simulation_environment}

We construct a simulation environment to capture the structural complexity and stochastic variability of real-world reinsurance markets. Each episode begins with the issuance of a new treaty, characterized by a feature vector drawn from empirically calibrated distributions. These features include: exposure size, historical loss distribution, line of business, attachment point, coverage limit, and retention level. Table~\ref{tab:treaty_generation} summarizes the main components of treaty generation.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Summary of treaty generation features in the simulated reinsurance environment.}
\label{tab:treaty_generation}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Description} \\
\midrule
Exposure Size & Total insured value subject to reinsurance \\
Loss Distribution & Empirical distribution calibrated from historical claims \\
Line of Business & Categorical indicator (e.g., property, casualty) \\
Attachment Point & Threshold at which reinsurance coverage begins \\
Coverage Limit & Maximum payout by the reinsurer \\
Retention Level & Portion of risk retained by the primary insurer \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Loss outcomes are generated using a two-part probabilistic model. Attritional losses---representing frequent, low-severity claims---are sampled from a compound Poisson distribution based on calibrated frequency and severity parameters. Catastrophic losses---rare but high-impact events---are drawn from a heavy-tailed distribution (e.g., Pareto or Lognormal)~\cite{embrechts1997modelling}. This layered structure captures both volatility in expected losses and extreme tail exposure typical of commercial treaty portfolios.


The bid evaluation mechanism is governed by a stochastic utility function used by the insurer to rank submitted bids. Each bid is evaluated based on a joint consideration of price and coverage generosity. The insurer's utility for bid $i$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
U_i = -\text{Premium}_i + \theta \cdot \text{CoverageQuality}_i + \epsilon_i,
\end{equation}
where $\theta$ is a weighting parameter reflecting risk-adjusted preferences and $\epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ introduces selection noise. This formulation reflects real-world frictions such as broker discretion, asymmetric information, and placement uncertainty~\cite{dias2003insurance}.

The presence of stochastic utility rankings introduces strategic uncertainty for bidding agents. Agents must learn robust policies that balance exploration and exploitation in the face of variable market conditions and partial observability, aligning with the decision-making complexity faced by real reinsurers.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% section 4.2
\subsection{Agent Architecture}
\label{subsec:agent_architecture}

We model each reinsurer as an autonomous learning agent embedded in a decentralized, partially observable multi-agent environment. Each agent maintains two neural components:

\begin{itemize}
    \item A \textbf{policy network} (actor) that processes local treaty features and market observations to output a continuous bid vector---comprising premium rates, quota shares, and ceding commissions. This actor governs the agent’s decisions at deployment time.

    \item A \textbf{value network} (critic) trained centrally with access to global state information to estimate expected returns. This critic is used exclusively during training to provide stable gradient feedback for actor updates.
\end{itemize}

We implement the \textbf{Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization (MAPPO)} algorithm~\cite{yu2021surprising} as the core learning method. MAPPO extends PPO with centralized critics, clipped surrogate objectives, and advantage normalization---offering robust training dynamics in environments with adversarial coupling and partial observability. Empirical evaluation shows MAPPO outperforms alternative MARL algorithms such as MADDPG and MATD3 in convergence speed and reward stability (see Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curve_comparison}).

Training follows a \textit{Centralized Training with Decentralized Execution} (CTDE) paradigm~\cite{foerster2016learning}. During training, agents share a prioritized experience replay buffer~\cite{mnih2015human} and update policies using global state trajectories. At execution, agents operate independently based on local observations---mirroring real-world informational asymmetries in treaty markets.

Agent rewards incorporate both profitability and downside risk. In particular, a Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR) penalty~\cite{artzner1999coherent, tamar2015optimizing} discourages exposure to catastrophic tail losses, promoting risk-aware bidding strategies that satisfy solvency and regulatory constraints.

As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:environment_architecture}, agents iteratively receive treaty opportunities, submit bids, receive placement feedback, and update their policies through repeated simulation. This loop enables the emergence of adaptive, economically rational bidding behaviors in a dynamic and competitive reinsurance market.


