% section 4.3
\subsection{Modeling Market Frictions}
\label{subsec:market_dynamics}

To better reflect institutional constraints and informational asymmetries characteristic of reinsurance markets, we explicitly incorporate three salient market frictions that shape bidding dynamics and outcomes. These frictions introduce deviations from idealized auction behavior and are grounded in both empirical market design and auction-theoretic foundations~\cite{milgrom1982theory, klemperer1999auction}.

\paragraph{Formal Setup.} Let $\mathcal{A} = \{1, \dots, N\}$ denote the set of reinsurer agents bidding on a treaty $T$. Each agent $i \in \mathcal{A}$ submits a bid $b_i = (r_i, c_i)$, where $r_i$ is a quoted premium rate and $c_i$ comprises additional coverage terms such as quota share, coverage limit, and attachment level. The insurer evaluates each bid using a stochastic utility function
\[
U_i = u(b_i; \theta_T) + \epsilon_i,
\]
where $u(\cdot)$ is a deterministic function that maps the bid and treaty attributes $\theta_T$ into utility space, and $\epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ captures idiosyncratic heterogeneity in preferences~\cite{dias2003insurance}.

Under frictionless conditions, the treaty is awarded to the agent offering the highest utility:
\[
i^* = \arg\max_{i \in \mathcal{A}} U_i.
\]
However, if agent $j \in \mathcal{A}$ is the incumbent reinsurer, they are awarded the treaty even when slightly outbid, provided their utility is within a threshold buffer $\delta > 0$ of the top bid:
\begin{equation}
i^* =
\begin{cases}
j, & \text{if } j \text{ is incumbent and } U_j \geq (1 - \delta) \cdot \max_{i \in \mathcal{A}} U_i, \\
\arg\max_{i \in \mathcal{A}} U_i, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:incumbent_selection}
\end{equation}
This mechanism formalizes \emph{incumbent advantage}---a structural bias favoring previously selected reinsurers, justified by continuity of relationship, reduced onboarding cost, and trust in claim settlement.

\paragraph{Last-Look Mechanism.} If the incumbent is not initially selected via Equation~\eqref{eq:incumbent_selection}, a \emph{last-look} opportunity is provided. The incumbent is allowed to revise their bid, $b_j \rightarrow b_j'$, after observing partial market outcomes. The updated bid is accepted if it exceeds the prior best offer in utility terms:
\begin{equation}
U_j' = u(b_j'; \theta_T) + \epsilon_j \geq \max_{i \in \mathcal{A} \setminus \{j\}} U_i.
\label{eq:last_look}
\end{equation}
This introduces a sequential, dynamic auction structure with asymmetric timing, in line with the literature on efficient dynamic auctions~\cite{ausubel2006efficient, milgrom1982theory}.

\paragraph{Broker Visibility and Asymmetry.} Brokers facilitate the market by collecting bids from all agents and recommending placements to the insurer. Brokers have full visibility into all submitted bids $\{b_i\}_{i=1}^N$. Reinsurers, in contrast, observe only their own bid and final outcome ($y_i \in \{0,1\}$). This creates a \emph{partially observable game with asymmetric information}, departing from classical Nash equilibrium settings. Strategic reasoning under such opacity leads to emergent behavior shaped by Bayesian updating and belief modeling across repeated episodes.

