\subsection{Baseline Models for Comparison}
\label{subsec:baseline_models}

To assess the value and robustness of our proposed multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) framework, we benchmark its performance against three widely recognized baseline strategies. These baselines reflect common practices in actuarial pricing, naive heuristics, and single-agent learning, allowing us to disentangle the contributions of strategic adaptation, coordination, and environmental awareness.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Static Actuarial Pricing}: Each agent computes a bid based on the expected loss $\mathbb{E}[L]$ plus a fixed risk loading $\lambda$, i.e., $b_i = \mathbb{E}[L_i] \cdot (1 + \lambda)$, where $\lambda$ is calibrated from historical reinsurance margins. This method approximates standard actuarial pricing rules used in industry, grounded in underwriting discipline and loss ratio targeting~\cite{boonen2019machine, xu2020pricing}.

    \item \textbf{Random Bidding}: Bid vectors are drawn uniformly from the admissible action space $\mathcal{B}$ without reference to treaty features or opponent actions. This naive strategy serves as a lower-bound baseline and exposes the performance degradation under uncoordinated, non-strategic behavior.

    \item \textbf{Single-Agent Q-Learning}: Each agent treats the bidding environment as stationary and optimizes its policy independently via tabular or deep Q-learning. While this method enables reactive adaptation, it fails to account for competitor responses or market-induced non-stationarity~\cite{yang2020agent}.
\end{itemize}

Figure~\ref{fig:baseline_comparison_plot} illustrates the comparative performance of each approach in terms of average cumulative reward over 10,000 episodes. The MARL framework significantly outperforms all baselines, demonstrating superior generalization, learning stability, and tail-risk control.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.55\linewidth]{baseline_comparison_reward_plot.png}
\caption{Performance comparison across learning approaches. MARL agents achieve higher cumulative reward with lower volatility relative to actuarial, random, and single-agent learning baselines. Shaded areas denote 95\% confidence intervals across 5 random seeds.}
\label{fig:baseline_comparison_plot}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Statistical Testing.}
To verify the significance of performance differences, we apply non-parametric two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov (K–S) tests and Welch's t-tests on cumulative reward distributions. In all cases, the null hypothesis of equal performance between MARL and baselines is rejected at the 1\% level ($p < 0.01$). Robustness holds under varying random seeds, treaty mixes, and market frictions. These tests confirm that MARL’s improvements are statistically meaningful and not driven by chance or noise.




