\subsection{Stress Testing the System}
\label{subsec:stress_tests}

To assess the resilience, adaptability, and generalization capacity of the learned bidding strategies, we evaluate MARL agents under two adverse market conditions designed to mimic real-world systemic shocks. Stress testing is a standard practice in financial risk management, particularly in insurance and reinsurance, to ensure robustness under tail events and capital strain~\cite{albert2021deep, jobst2014stress}.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Catastrophic Loss Scenario}: We triple the frequency and severity of catastrophe claims relative to baseline settings, emulating years of elevated natural disaster incidence (e.g., post-hurricane seasons or multi-event periods). This scenario tests whether agents can maintain profitability while managing tail risk exposure in highly volatile underwriting environments~\cite{embrechts1997modelling}.

    \item \textbf{Capacity Constraint Scenario}: A 30\% reduction in total reinsurer capital is enforced, simulating a market-wide contraction in available risk-bearing capacity due to capital withdrawal, retrocession failures, or regulatory tightening. Agents must adapt their bidding to operate efficiently under constrained supply and increased competitive pressure~\cite{cummins2002capacity}.
\end{itemize}

Figure~\ref{fig:stress_test_policy_robustness} summarizes the impact of these stressors on key performance metrics. Compared to the baseline, both stress regimes induce a degradation in average cumulative rewards, heightened bid volatility, and significantly worsened CVaR profiles. These effects highlight the dual challenge of maintaining competitiveness while preserving solvency in adverse market regimes.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{stress_test_policy_robustness.png}
\caption{Performance of MARL agents under stress test scenarios. Compared to baseline, both catastrophic loss and capital constraint regimes reduce average rewards, increase bid volatility, and degrade CVaR metrics, highlighting the strategic and financial challenges faced in stressed reinsurance markets.}
\label{fig:stress_test_policy_robustness}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Statistical Robustness Checks.}
To formally validate the significance of observed performance differences, we conduct paired two-tailed t-tests comparing baseline and stress test regimes across 20 simulation seeds. For each seed, we compute the following metrics: mean episode reward, standard deviation of bid magnitudes, and CVaR at the 95\% level.

\begin{itemize}
    \item The catastrophic loss regime exhibits a statistically significant decline in mean reward ($t = 5.83$, $p < 0.001$) and CVaR performance ($t = 4.45$, $p < 0.01$), with a corresponding increase in bid volatility ($t = -3.92$, $p < 0.01$).

    \item The capacity constraint regime shows similarly significant effects: reward degradation ($t = 4.12$, $p < 0.01$), increased bid variance ($t = -3.57$, $p < 0.01$), and lower CVaR ($t = 3.88$, $p < 0.01$).
\end{itemize}

To supplement parametric testing, we construct 95\% percentile bootstrap confidence intervals (1{,}000 resamples) for reward differences. The intervals do not contain zero under either stress condition:
\[
\text{Catastrophe: } \Delta R \in [-22.5, -11.4], \quad \text{Capacity: } \Delta R \in [-18.7, -9.6].
\]

These findings affirm that both stressors significantly disrupt agent performance, suggesting that MARL bidding policies---while adaptive---remain sensitive to macro-level regime shifts. This sensitivity underscores the importance of scenario-based retraining and dynamic risk management in real-world reinsurance systems.


