\subsection{Training the Agents}
\label{subsec:training_process}

Reinsurer agents are trained through repeated interaction with the simulated market environment. In each episode, agents receive treaty features and market signals, submit competitive bids, and observe the resulting outcomes. Feedback from these interactions---captured as rewards, updated states, and allocation outcomes---is used to update agent policies using gradient-based reinforcement learning~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement}. The training process employs the centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE) paradigm~\cite{lowe2017multi}, enabling agents to leverage joint state-action information during learning while preserving decentralized decision-making at deployment.

The overall training loop is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:marl_training}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Treaty Bidding}
\label{alg:marl_training}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Initialize:} Policy networks $\pi_{\theta_i}$ and critic networks $Q_{\phi_i}$ for each agent $i$
\State \textbf{Initialize:} Experience replay buffers $\mathcal{D}_i$ for all $i \in \{1, \dots, N\}$
\For{episode $e = 1$ to $E$}
    \State Sample a synthetic treaty and market context
    \For{each agent $i$}
        \State Observe local state $o_i$ and submit bid $a_i \sim \pi_{\theta_i}(o_i)$
    \EndFor
    \State Insurer evaluates bids and allocates treaty based on market rules
    \State Simulate treaty outcomes; compute agent-specific rewards $R_i$
    \State Store transitions $(o_i, a_i, R_i, o_i')$ in $\mathcal{D}_i$
    \If{episode $e \bmod$ update interval $= 0$}
        \For{each agent $i$}
            \State Sample minibatch from $\mathcal{D}_i$
            \State Update $Q_{\phi_i}$ using Bellman loss~\cite{mnih2015human}
            \State Update $\pi_{\theta_i}$ via clipped policy gradient (MAPPO)~\cite{yu2021surprising}
        \EndFor
    \EndIf
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\vspace{0.5em}
\noindent
Training is conducted using a shared replay buffer with prioritized experience sampling~\cite{schaul2015prioritized} to accelerate learning and stabilize convergence. Each agent independently learns a policy tailored to its perspective and market role, enabling strategic differentiation and competition.

Full hyperparameter specifications are provided in Appendix~\ref{appendix:hyperparameters}. Appendix~\ref{appendix:learning_diagnostics} includes diagnostics on learning stability, convergence behavior, and policy variance across random seeds.

