% section 5.5
\subsection{Stress Testing Under Market Shocks}
\label{subsec:stress_testing}

To evaluate the resilience of the MARL system under real-world disruptions, we perform two stress tests: a catastrophic loss shock and a capital constraint shock.

\subsubsection*{Catastrophe Shock}
We simulate a sharp increase in both frequency and severity of catastrophic claims between episodes 600 and 700. MARL agents demonstrate strong resilience: cumulative rewards decline briefly by only 0.44\% and recover at a slope of +0.224 reward units per episode post-shock. A two-sample $t$-test comparing pre-shock and post-recovery rewards confirms statistically significant improvement ($t = 54.71$, $p < 10^{-190}$).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.55\textwidth]{catastrophe_stress_test.png}
\caption{Catastrophe shock response. MARL agents adapt quickly and resume upward reward trajectory.}
\label{fig:catastrophe_stress}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection*{Capital Constraint Shock}
In a second scenario, we reduce total available reinsurance capital by 30\%, mimicking market-wide tightening due to retrocession failures or regulatory shifts. MARL agents adaptively revise their bidding strategies, prioritizing high-efficiency treaty placements. Compared to baseline conditions, average profit declines modestly by 7.2\%, and CVaR$_{95\%}$ increases from 0.17 to 0.21, indicating slightly elevated tail risk. Bid success rates remain relatively stable at 38.6\% (versus 41.5\% under normal capital conditions). All differences are statistically significant at the 1\% level ($p < 0.01$, paired $t$-tests across five seeds).

\vspace{0.75em}
\noindent
\textbf{Strategic insight}: MARL agents exhibit robust adaptive coordination under systemic shocks. In catastrophe scenarios, agents rapidly re-optimize their policies; in constrained markets, they strategically reduce exposure and bid more selectively. This mirrors observed industry responses to events like Hurricane Katrina (2005), COVID-19 (2020), and Hurricane Ian (2022), where reinsurers de-risked portfolios and adjusted capital allocation \cite{swissre_katrina, lloyds_covid, ian_guycarp, cummins2002capacity}.


