% section 8
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This study presents a novel framework for dynamic reinsurance treaty placement using multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL), addressing long-standing inefficiencies in broker-mediated, static bidding processes \cite{cummins2001reinsurance, cummings2021economics}. By modeling treaty bidding as a competitive multi-agent environment and incorporating risk-sensitive objectives, we enable reinsurers to learn adaptive strategies that dynamically balance profit and risk in a realistic market simulation.

Our core contributions are threefold. First, we introduce the first MARL-based architecture specifically tailored for the reinsurance domain, combining centralized training with decentralized execution. Second, we demonstrate empirically that MARL agents significantly outperform conventional approaches---including random bidding and actuarial pricing baselines---across multiple financial metrics such as profit, CVaR, and Sharpe ratio \cite{cao2010optimal, wang2018optimal}. Third, we provide a detailed evaluation of system robustness, stress resilience, and strategic behavior, revealing meaningful implications for both market participants and regulators \cite{embrechts1997modelling, yang2020agent}.

Beyond technical gains, the framework opens new opportunities for operational transparency, strategic decision support, and more resilient capital allocation in volatile markets. By reducing dependency on manual intermediation and enabling algorithmic bidding under uncertainty, MARL offers a powerful tool for modernizing risk transfer mechanisms.

Ultimately, this study offers a viable path toward a future where reinsurance markets are more transparent, adaptive, and resilient---powered by advanced multi-agent learning systems.


