\section{Training Hyperparameters}
\label{appendix:hyperparameters}

The multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) framework employed centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE), a widely adopted paradigm shown to improve coordination and stability in competitive environments~\cite{lowe2017multi, foerster2018counterfactual}. Table~\ref{tab:hyperparameters} summarizes the key hyperparameters used throughout training.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Training Hyperparameters for MARL Agents}
\label{tab:hyperparameters}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Hyperparameter} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Actor Learning Rate & $1 \times 10^{-4}$ \\
Critic Learning Rate & $1 \times 10^{-3}$ \\
Discount Factor ($\gamma$) & 0.99 \\
Replay Buffer Size & $10^6$ transitions \\
Mini-batch Size & 1,024 \\
Target Network Update Rate ($\tau$) & 0.005 \\
Exploration Strategy & Ornstein–Uhlenbeck Process ($\theta = 0.15$, $\sigma = 0.2$) \\
Optimizer & Adam~\cite{kingma2014adam} \\
Policy Update Frequency & Every 100 steps \\
Evaluation Frequency & Every 500 episodes \\
Risk Aversion Coefficients ($\lambda$) & \{0.1, 1.0, 10.0\} \\
CVaR Confidence Level ($\alpha$) & 95\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We adopt a high discount factor ($\gamma = 0.99$) to encourage long-horizon optimization, consistent with sequential decision-making under delayed feedback. Soft updates to target networks using a small interpolation factor ($\tau = 0.005$) improve critic stability by smoothing parameter transitions. For exploration in continuous action spaces, we apply the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process, which introduces temporally correlated noise to facilitate more consistent exploration during training. All neural network updates use the Adam optimizer~\cite{kingma2014adam}, which adapts learning rates per parameter and has demonstrated strong empirical performance in deep reinforcement learning contexts.


