\section{{Introduction}}

\noindent Mechanism design deals with the design of incentive-compatible mechanisms to achieve desirable outcomes in the presence of self-interested participants. This line of research has led to landmark papers in auction theory and related fields ($e.g.$, \citet{hurwicz1973design}, \citet{myerson1983mechanism}). Traditionally, it is assumed that the designer cannot observe the participants' valuations ex-ante or ex-post. However, in some applications, the designer might actually be able to observe the valuations ex-ante or ex-post, often referred to as mechanism design with inspection ($e.g.$, \citet{ben2014optimal}).

For instance, a franchising firm (the principal) may receive an offer for its copyrights from a potential franchisee (the agent) who has better insights into the investment's potential than the franchising firm. The franchising firm can require the franchisee to integrate their IT systems with the main firm, allowing the firm to monitor their performance at no cost. The franchising firm can hold the agent's bid as a security deposit and evaluate their claims after a set period. If the bid is accurate, the agent earns rewards. The franchising firm benefits from the good performance of the franchisee and wants to maximize the profit of the franchisee minus the reward. 

Similarly, consider a municipal government that considers selling a piece of land to a private firm aiming to set up a production facility. The municipality benefits from the production site via taxes if it generates profits. The firm can predict future profits, but this is difficult for the municipality.   
The private firm must share its operational data with the municipality after the production facility was built and submit the projected profit value as a bid, upon which the municipality decides. The performance and profit can be assessed at no cost, and if the bid is truthful, the firm receives rewards. 
Yet another example is the allocation of scarce storage that a retail and logistics platform assigns to a seller based on his sales predictions for the next period \citep{alaei2024optimal}. 
In all of these cases, the principal decides to award to an agent based on his bid and wants the bid to be truthful. The principal aims to maximize profit minus the rewards to the bidder, ensuring the mechanism encourages the bidder to provide a genuine profit forecast rather than an inflated figure. 


\citet{alaei2024optimal} design a revenue-maximizing mechanism under a standard common prior assumption. 
In their model, a seller (she) wants to sell an indivisible object to a buyer (he). The buyer will generate a positive value in the event that they receive the object.
The authors assume that the buyers privately know these values with certainty.
The seller has the means to observe the value of the agents after the allocation. After acquiring this additional information, the seller may reward the buyer with respect to their true valuations. 
Hence, the seller may use costless inspection and rewards to increase her payoff from selling the object. 
The optimal mechanism derived by \citet{alaei2024optimal} is truthful so that the buyers or agents report their true valuations and receive non-negative rewards only if their report matches their true valuation. 
The mechanism allocates the good to the agents with respect to an allocation rule, where the probability of an agent receiving the good is concave and increasing in his report. 
In the model, each agent's true valuation is revealed regardless of their probability of receiving the good. 
After an agent is reimbursed with rewards, the agents with low valuation receive zero payoff, even if they receive the good with positive probability, whereas an agent with high valuation has a higher probability of receiving the good and gaining a positive payoff (information rent).

The authors show that the optimal mechanism increases the principal's payoff by around 50\% compared to the classical optimal auction that does not use the ex-post information from an inspection. 
Although these results are compelling, the assumption of having a common prior is strong. The Wilson doctrine stipulates that game theory should not rely on such assumptions (see \citet{Wilson_1987}). This critique gave rise to robust mechanism design (see \citet{bergemann2005robust}),  where the goal is to find mechanisms that perform well in the face of ambiguity. In particular, we assume that the distribution of the agent's types is unknown but falls within a commonly known ambiguity set, which may include infinitely many continuous or discrete distributions. We focus on the single agent case (as in \citet{carrasco2018optimal}) and study the problem for two different objectives of the ambiguity-averse mechanism designer, $i.e.$, worst-case expected payoff maximization and worst-case expected regret minimization across all distributions in the ambiguity set. 
Utilizing techniques from distributionally robust optimization ($e.g.$, \citet{delage2010distributionally}), we identify robustly optimal mechanisms for two well-known classes of ambiguity sets: (i) support-only ambiguity sets, which include all distributions supported on a rectangle and (ii) Markov ambiguity sets, which consist of distributions in a support-only ambiguity set with mean values within another smaller rectangle. These ambiguity sets are especially relevant when there is limited prior distributional information, as one only needs to identify the agent's minimum, maximum, and most likely (expected) types from the available data. These ambiguity sets are also considered for other settings when the data is scarce ($e.g.$, \citet{ben2009robust}) and can be found in many robust mechanism design studies ($e.g.$, \citet{carrasco2018optimal}, \citet{wang2024minimax}, \citet{koccyiugit2020distributionally}, \citet{bayrak2022distributionally}). 
{ In the context of the franchisee selection example, the valuation distribution would depend on various elements related to the entrepreneur and the location, either of which can be unfamiliar to the franchisor. In such cases, it can either be infeasible or very costly to estimate the true valuation distribution of the agent, but limited information (such as minimal, maximal, and expected value) can be extracted from scarce data.}


We characterize a mechanism with a concave allocation and a linear payment rule that is distributionally robust irrespective of the ambiguity-averse principal's attitude towards ambiguity, $i.e.$, whether she wants to maximize her worst-case payoff or minimize her worst-case regret.
If the ambiguity set is only constrained by the support information (Support-only case) and the principal wants to maximize her worst-case payoff, then the worst-case distribution is always the Dirac distribution that concentrates all probability to the lowest possible valuation (type) of the agent. In this case, if the lowest possible valuation is strictly positive, then the Posted price mechanism with the price equivalent to the lowest possible type is the unique robustly optimal mechanism. Hence, the presence of ambiguity in this extreme case and the seller's attitude towards ambiguity cancel the advantage introduced by the deferred inspection and rewards. Since this case's results are straightforward, we do not include them in our paper. The support-only ambiguity set becomes interesting and relevant when the principal wants to minimize her worst-case regret. Then, the worst-case distribution is a two-point distribution, under which we show that it is sufficient to focus on mechanisms with linear payment rules to achieve robust optimality. As in \citet{alaei2024optimal}, this mechanism has a concave allocation rule but might be more favorable in terms of practicality. We show that the same approach can also be used to find robustly optimal mechanisms under Markov ambiguity sets. We also present a robustly optimal mechanism that deploys the same allocation rule and extracts the maximal payment from each type of the agent, allowing it to derive a strictly higher expected payoff under non-worst-case distributions compared to the mechanism with linear payment rule.

We also present a numerical study on the robustly optimal mechanism with inspection and rewards when the principal faces two agents. We show that restricting the feasible set to linear payment rules, which enables us to find the robust optimal solution in the one-agent setting, leads to suboptimality but still performs reasonably well. The analytical results of the current paper do not allow for a simple extension to the multiple-agent setting (even for the purpose of finding an approximation mechanism). While our analysis provides a foundation, multi-agent mechanisms become significantly more complex. In numerical experiments, we show that monotonous mechanisms might not exist. We leave this mechanism design problem for future research.



\subsection{Contributions}

Our main contributions can be summarized as follows. 
We characterize two different robustly optimal mechanisms for the mechanism design problem when the buyers privately know their values, and the principal can verify these values after allocation without any cost.
Except for the trivial cases, we show that robust optimality cannot be achieved by posted price mechanisms, but a linear payment rule would be sufficient. However, the linearity of the payment rule leads to negative payments for some small types, and the allocation rule still has to be concave, as in \citet{alaei2024optimal}. 
We explicitly describe two robustly optimal mechanisms, one with the linear payment rule and the other with the maximum possible payment from each type. 
Note that these two mechanisms are robustly optimal both when the principal maximizes her worst-case payoff and when she minimizes her worst-case regret. Unlike \citet{alaei2024optimal}, we cannot use variational calculus in our setting since we relax their assumptions on the common prior that allows them to focus on mechanisms with strictly concave allocation rule. Instead, we utilize linear programming to identify the worst-case distribution as a discrete one and argue that a linear payment is sufficient for robust optimality. Then, we are able to construct a robustly optimal mechanism. 
{ Lastly, the upper bound on the expected value in Markov ambiguity set do not play any role and can be omitted if the seller is maximizing her worst-case payoff. However, this is not generally the case if she is minimizing her worst-case regret.}

\subsection{Illustration} 

To motivate distributional robust mechanisms further, we report a numerical experiment comparing the optimal mechanism from \citet{alaei2024optimal} tailored to a specific distribution and our robustly optimal mechanism that maximizes the worst-case payoff across a set of distributions. For a detailed discussion, see the end of Section \ref{sec: twoOptimalMechanisms}. In this experiment, we assume that the true distribution of the types is a convex combination of the uniform distribution and a worst-case distribution that we identify. Figure \ref{fig: experimentIntro} reports the performances of two mechanisms where a performance of one means that the mechanism achieves the same expected payoff as the optimal mechanism tailored to the true distribution. At one extreme point, when the true distribution is exactly uniform ($\varepsilon=0$), the mechanism characterized by \citet{alaei2024optimal} is optimal, whereas our robustly optimal mechanism (maximal payment) becomes optimal when the misspecification is at its most severe ($\varepsilon=1$). As the figure shows, the robustly optimal mechanism with maximal payments consistently performs close to the true optimal solution and outperforms the mechanism from \citet{alaei2024optimal} when $\varepsilon \geq 0.324$. 

