We next present the results of \citet{alaei2024optimal} that remain valid in the face of ambiguity, as they do not utilize the assumption of commonly known prior.

\begin{proposition}[\citet{alaei2024optimal}]\label{prop: resultsAlaei}
    Given a Markov ambiguity set $\mathcal{P}$, there exists a robustly optimal deferred-inspection auction $(x,p)$ that satisfies
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(i)] $p(\nu)=\inf_{\hat \nu \leq \nu} \{ \nu(x(\nu)-x(\hat \nu)) +\hat \nu \}$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$,
        \item[(ii)] the optimal allocation rule $x$ is weakly increasing with $x(0)=0$ and $x(1)=1$, and it is concave.
    \end{itemize}
\end{proposition}

The above result appears as a necessary condition for optimality in \citet{alaei2024optimal}, whereas robust optimality may be achieved without it. This is because \citet{alaei2024optimal} assume their prior distribution to be everywhere strictly positive, whereas, in our setting, the ambiguity set also includes discrete or mixed distributions. Nevertheless, the proof of \citet{alaei2024optimal} can be deployed in our setting to show the existence of a robustly optimal mechanism satisfying the same conditions.

We now use Proposition \ref{prop: resultsAlaei} to show that the same robustly optimal mechanism has a weakly increasing payment rule. This result will be useful in the next section. 
%Its proof is relegated to the appendix.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: weaklyIncreasingPayment}
    Given a Markov ambiguity set $\mathcal{P}$, there exists a robustly optimal deferred-inspection auction $(x,p)$ with a weakly increasing payment rule $p$.
\end{lemma}

Given any allocation rule $x(\nu)$ weakly increasing and concave, the optimal value of $p(\nu)$, $i.e.$ the maximum feasible payment that can be extracted from $\nu$, can be found using the formula in Proposition \ref{prop: resultsAlaei}. This requires finding the type $\hat \nu$ that satisfies the first order condition for the function $-\nu x(\hat \nu) +\hat \nu$.
Hence, the optimal mechanism could be rather complex for application as the agents should be able to calculate first-order conditions to check its incentive compatibility. 

As evident from Figure \ref{fig: comparisonToPosted}, deferred inspection with rewards enlarges the set of feasible solutions for the auction design problem, which renders simple mechanisms such as Posted price obsolete if one can embrace the complexity that comes with it. However, the optimal mechanism from \citet{alaei2024optimal} and the optimal Posted price mechanism are in stark difference, which may suggest the existence of other mechanisms that can balance performance and simplicity. Introducing ambiguity to the model is one way to reduce the optimal mechanism's dependence on the environment's assumptions.


% Head 2
\subsection{Mechanisms with Linear Payment rules}
We will now argue that the seller interested in maximizing her worst-case expected revenue under Markov ambiguity sets can focus on mechanisms that admit linear payment rules. 
This would simplify our search for a robustly optimal mechanism and may also return an easy-to-use mechanism. In this section, we also give upper bounds to our robust optimization problem and argue on the feasible mechanisms that can achieve these upper bounds.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: linearPayment}
    Under a Markov ambiguity set $\mathcal{P}$, the robust mechanism design problem is equivalent to solving the following linear program:
    \begin{align}
    {z^*} = \sup_{{x,p,\alpha,\beta}} \; & \alpha \underline \mu + \beta \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \; & \alpha \nu + \beta \leq p(\nu) &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber \\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq \nu x(\hat{\nu}) - \hat{\nu} &\forall \nu, \hat \nu \in [0,1]: \hat \nu< \nu, \tag{\ref{IC}}\\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \tag{\ref{IR}}\\
    &x(\nu) \leq 1 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber\\
    &x(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber\\
    &\alpha \geq 0. \nonumber
\end{align}
\end{lemma}


%The proof of Lemma \ref{lem: linearPayment} can be found in the Appendix. 
In what follows, we discuss the intuition behind Lemma \ref{lem: linearPayment}. Consider an arbitrary robustly optimal mechanism $(x^*,p^*)$, that yields a worst-case revenue of $z^*>0$. Then, as the Dirac distribution at $\underline{\mu}$ is contained by the Markov ambiguity set, we must have $p^*(\underline{\mu}) \geq z^*$. Now consider the straight lines that pass through the points $(\underline{\mu}, z^*)$ and $(\nu,p^*(\nu))$ for all $\nu \in [0,1] \setminus \{\underline \mu\}$. Two lines are important to us, and we will define them using the following parameters: 
\begin{align*}
    &\underline \alpha =\sup_{\nu \in [0, \underline \mu)} \frac{p^*(\nu)-z^*}{\nu-\underline \mu}, &\underline \beta =z^* - \underline \alpha \underline \mu,\\
    &\overline{\alpha} =\inf_{\nu \in (\underline \mu,1]} \frac{p^*(\nu)-z^*}{\nu-\underline \mu}, &\overline{\beta} =z^*- \overline{a} \underline \mu.
\end{align*}
We illustrate these two lines for the optimal payment rule of \citet{alaei2024optimal} and an arbitrary $z$ value in Figure \ref{fig: LinearPayment}. 

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figures/linearP.pdf}
\caption{The optimal payment rule of \citet{alaei2024optimal} and an arbitrary worst-case payoff of $z$.} 
\label{fig: LinearPayment}
\end{figure}

We observe that for any robustly optimal $(x^*,p^*)$ with a worst-case revenue of $z^*$, one must have $\underline \alpha \leq \overline{\alpha}$. In this case, for any $\alpha \in [\underline \alpha,\overline{\alpha}]$ and $\beta = z^* - \alpha \underline \mu$, we have $p^*(\nu) \geq \alpha\nu+\beta$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$ due to the definition of $\alpha$, and $\alpha \underline \mu+\beta \geq z^*$ due to the definition of $\beta$.

Assuming the contrary, $\underline \alpha > \overline{\alpha}$, there should exist some $\nu' \in [0,\underline \mu)$ with $\frac{p^*(\nu')-z^*}{\nu'-\underline \mu} >\overline{\alpha}$. This leads to the following:
$$
p^*(\nu') - \overline{\alpha} (\nu'-\underline \mu)< z^*.
$$
For the sake of argument, assume that there exists some $\nu'' \in (\underline \mu,1]$ with $\frac{p^*(\nu')-z^*}{\nu'-\underline \mu} =\overline{\alpha}$. Then, we can find a convex combination of the points $(\nu',p^*(\nu'))$ and $(\nu'',p^*(\nu''))$ with the value $(\underline \mu,\lambda p^*(\nu')+ (1-\lambda)p^*(\nu''))$. This convex combination of the points $\nu'$ and $\nu''$ is an admissible distribution in $\mathcal{P}$, and it leads to an expected payoff of
$$
\lambda[ p^*(\nu') -\overline{\alpha} (\nu'-\underline \mu)]+ (1-\lambda)[p^*(\nu'')-\overline{\alpha} (\nu''-\underline \mu)] = \lambda p^*(\nu') + (1-\lambda)p^*(\nu'')< z^*.
$$
This contradicts the robust optimality of $(x^*,p^*)$.

This observation tells us that it is enough to consider mechanisms with a linear payment rule to achieve robust optimality under Markov ambiguity sets. A linear payment rule is the next best thing for a seller interested in practicality but not content with a Posted price mechanism. In what follows, we focus on worst-case distributions to get upper bounds on the maximum $z^*$ value. We will then show that these upper bounds can be achieved by a mechanism that has a linear payment rule. 


The following result provides two upper bounds for the seller's maximum worst-case revenue. 
{ Notice that the upper bounds depend only on the lowest possible expected value, $\underline{\mu}$, whereas $\overline{\mu}$ do not play a role. In fact, when the seller is a worst-case payoff maximizer, it is suboptimal for the adversary to choose a distribution that has an expected value $\mu \in (\underline{\mu},\overline{\mu}]$. Therefore, $\overline{\mu}$ information do not give any leverage to the seller and can be disregarded. In section \ref{sec: worst-case regret minimization}, we show that this is not the case for the seller who minimizes her worst-case regret.}
%Its proof is relegated to the appendix.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:upperBounds}
    The seller's optimal worst-case revenue $z^*$ is bounded above by $\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}$ for any $\underline \mu \in (0,1)$ and by $\frac{\underline \mu}{3}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2$ for any $\underline \mu \in (0,(15-3\sqrt{5})/10]$, where $\delta= \frac{\underline \mu}{3-\underline \mu}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figures/UpperBoundsFrom2and3PointDist.pdf}
\caption{The upper-bounds in Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}.}
    \label{fig: upperBounds}
\end{figure}

The upper bounds in Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds} are illustrated in Figure \ref{fig: upperBounds}. The first bound $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$ is derived using a two-point distribution with the points $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$ and 1. This distribution leaves the principal indifferent between two mechanisms and any convex combination of these two mechanisms. The first mechanism 
allocates the good to both types with probability $1$ and asks payment of $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$ from either type. The second one allocates the good to type 1 with probability 1, whereas the probability for the lower type is only $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$. This latter mechanism asks for the payment values that leave both types with zero information rent. The expected payoffs under these two mechanisms coincide under the two-point distribution considered in the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}. That is, the expected gains from allocating the good to the lowest type and the expected loss from the information rent given to the highest type breaks even. The second bound is derived via a three-point distribution, which is admissible only for a subset of $\underline \mu$ values. Here, the principal is indifferent between allocating the good to the medium type with a small probability or with a probability of $1$, whereas the lowest type always gets a fractional allocation probability. In any case, the payments from the low type and the medium type are proportional to their allocation, leaving them with zero information rent. The highest type always receives a positive information rent.


\textbf{Example:} Given any $\underline \mu \in (0,1)$, consider the admissible distribution $\mathbb P$ that assigns a probability of $\underline \mu/2$ to type $1$, and $(2-\underline \mu)/2$ to type $\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}$. The following mechanism is optimal under this two-point distribution with an expected payoff of $\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}$.
\begin{align*}
    &x(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        1 &\text{if } \nu \in \{\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}, 1\},\\
        0 &\text{otherwise,}
    \end{cases}
    &p(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        \frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu} &\text{if } \nu \in \{\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}, 1\},\\
        0 &\text{otherwise.}
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
However, this mechanism is not robustly optimal since the mechanism does not receive any payment from type $\underline \mu > \frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}$, $i.e.$, the Dirac distribution at $\underline \mu$ is admissible and will result in an expected payoff of zero. The following mechanism is robustly optimal with a worst-case expected payoff of $\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}$ if the support of the admissible distributions is restricted to the type space $[\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu},1]$. 
\begin{align*}
    &\hat x(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        1 &\text{if } \nu \in [\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}, 1],\\
        0 &\text{otherwise,}
    \end{cases} 
    &\hat p(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        \frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu} &\text{if } \nu \in [\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}, 1],\\
        0 &\text{otherwise.}
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
This is a Posted price mechanism, and it is practical, but it fails to be robustly optimal under our ambiguity set with the full support $[0,1]$. To see this, consider the admissible distribution that assigns a probability of $\underline \mu$ to type $1$ and a probability of $1-\underline \mu$ to type $0$. The expected payoff of $(\hat x, \hat p)$ under this distribution is $\underline \mu^2/(2-\underline \mu)$ which is strictly less than $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$ for any $\underline \mu \in (0,1)$. Hence, to attain the upper bound $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$ and robust optimality, we need a mechanism that is more complicated than the Posted price mechanism. 

Let us fix a linear payment rule $p$ that attains the upper bound $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$. Can we find a feasible allocation rule $x$ that implements $p$? We can first ignore \eqref{IC} constraints and construct an allocation rule from \eqref{IR} constraints; $x(\nu) = p(\nu)/\nu$. Then, starting from the lowest types, we could alleviate any violations of \eqref{IC} constraints by increasing their allocation probability. Note that increasing the allocation probability for some $\nu$ does not affect \eqref{IC} constraints of $\hat \nu < \nu$. Hence, this approach would work if we end up with a feasible allocation rule (always less than $1$).


\subsection{Two Different Robustly Optimal Mechanisms}\label{sec: twoOptimalMechanisms}

Our next result presents a robustly optimal mechanism with a linear payment rule that attains the upper bound $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$ given in Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds} for a subset of $\underline \mu$ values. We will illustrate this mechanism for different $\underline \mu$ values and argue that the payment rule can be improved at the cost of complexity. Our second result in this section presents the payment rule that extracts the maximum feasible payment from each type under the same allocation rule.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism}
    If $\underline \mu \in [0.315,1)$, then the following mechanism is robustly optimal with a worst-case expected payoff of $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$,
    \begin{equation*}\label{eq:linearPaymentMechanism}
    \begin{aligned}
    &x^*(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu &\text{if } \nu \in [0, \underline \nu],\\
        \alpha+ \frac{\beta}{\nu} &\text{if } \nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ],\\
        \nu+\alpha-2 \sqrt{-\beta} &\text{if } \nu \in [\nu^\circ, \overline{\nu}],\\
        1 &\text{if } \nu \in [\overline{\nu},1],
    \end{cases}
    &p^*(\nu) = \alpha \nu+ \beta \quad \forall \nu \in [0,1],
\end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
where $\alpha = 2/(2-\underline \mu)^2$, $\beta =-(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)^2$, $\underline \nu = -2\beta/\alpha$, $\nu^\circ = \sqrt{-\beta}$, and $\overline{\nu}= 1-\alpha+2\sqrt{-\beta}$.
\end{proposition}


Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism} describes a robustly optimal mechanism for the case $\underline \mu \in [0.315,1)$. The exact lower bound on $\underline \mu$ under which the mechanism $(x^*,p^*)$ becomes infeasible is very close to $0.315$, and finding it requires solving a high-order polynomial. One way to do this is by finding the roots of the second-degree polynomial in \eqref{eq: IC-nonnegativity} for fixed $\nu$, identifying the root that is between zero and one, and maximizing it over appropriate $\nu \in [0,1]$ values (with non-negative discriminant). This procedure would give the minimum $\underline \mu$ value for which the mechanism $(x^*,p^*)$ still satisfies \eqref{IC} constraints for all types. We avoid doing these tedious computations and only hint that this minimum $\underline \mu$ value is less than and very close to $0.315$. 

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figures/twoPointMechanism.pdf}
\caption{Mechanism $(x^*,p^*)$ from Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism} for different $\underline \mu$ values.}
    \label{fig: optimalTwoPointMechanisms}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig: optimalTwoPointMechanisms} illustrates the mechanism $(x^*,p^*)$ for different values of $\underline \mu \in [0.315,1)$. The first thing to be noticed in Figure \ref{fig: optimalTwoPointMechanisms} is that some types make negative net payments, $i.e.$ receive money from the principal. One can omit these negative payments and use a piecewise linear payment rule instead, which is equal to $p^*$ whenever $p^*$ is non-negative and equal to zero when $p^*$ is negative. It is easy to check that this mechanism is feasible, and the principal's worst-case expected payoff remains the same. Moreover, this would improve the expected payoff under some non-worst-case distributions in $\mathcal{P}$. Our next result presents the payment rule that extracts the maximum payment from each type under $x^*$. 

\begin{proposition}\label{prop: twoPointMaximalPayment}
    If $\underline \mu \in [0.315,1)$, then the following payment rule, $p_m^*$ extracts the maximum feasible payment from each type under $x^*$, and $(x^*,p_m^*)$ is robustly optimal:
        \begin{align*}
        &p_m^*(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu^2 &\text{if } \nu \in [0, \underline \nu],\\
        \alpha \nu+ \beta &\text{if } \nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ],\\
        \nu(\nu +\alpha -2\sqrt{-\beta}) &\text{if } \nu \in [\nu^\circ, \nu^\star],\\
        \nu^2-2\nu \sqrt{-\beta}+2 \sqrt{-\beta \nu} &\text{if } \nu \in [\nu^\star, \overline{\nu}],\\
        \nu(1-\alpha)+2\sqrt{-\beta \nu} &\text{if } \nu \in [\overline{\nu},1],
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
where $\nu^\star = -4\beta/\alpha^2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figures/twoPointMechanismMaximal.pdf}
\caption{Mechanism $(x^*,p_m^*)$ from Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointMaximalPayment} for different $\underline \mu$ values.}
    \label{fig: maximalTwoPointMechanisms}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig: maximalTwoPointMechanisms} illustrates the mechanism $(x^*,p_m^*)$ for different $\underline \mu$ values. This payment rule is mostly non-linear but improves the expected payoff of the principal under non-worst-case distributions. Extracting the maximum payment leaves all types $\nu \leq \nu^*$ with zero information rent, so they might as well not participate in the mechanism. On the other hand, all types $\nu >\nu^*$ receive a positive information rent, and their payments leave them indifferent between truthfully reporting $\nu$ and posing themselves as $\sqrt{-\beta \nu}$.


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figures/approximationGuaranteesMid.pdf}
\caption{The relative, $\rho$, and absolute, $c$, performance guarantees of the mechanism $(x^*,p^*)$ defined for $0.315$.}
    \label{fig: approximationMid}
\end{figure}
Next, we comment on the optimal worst-case payoff when $\underline \mu \in (0.25,0.315)$, which can be approximately attained by $(x^*,p^*)$. In this case, nature can use a convex combination of the two-point and three-point distributions from the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds} to get an upper bound that is less than those illustrated in Figure \ref{fig: upperBounds}. From our numerical experiments, we see that this is equivalent to finding the maximal convex function that is less than $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$ and $\underline \mu(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2/3$. To identify the robustly optimal mechanism, we first need to pin down this convex function, which requires solving a high-degree polynomial of $\underline \mu$, and use the linear programming approach from the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds} to argue that this is indeed an upper bound. Instead of doing all these tedious calculations, we argue that the mechanism $(x^*,p^*)$ defined for $\underline \mu^*=0.315$ demonstrates near-optimal performances when $\underline \mu \in (0.25,0.315)$. Figure \ref{fig: approximationMid} illustrates the performance metrics of $(x^*,p^*)$ relative to the upper bound $b = \min\{\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu),\underline \mu(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2/3\}$. Namely, $\rho = z/b$ and $c=b-z$, where $z$ is the worst-case expected payoff under $(x^*,p^*)$.
We know from Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism} that this mechanism is optimal when $\underline \mu=0.315$, and Figure \ref{fig: approximationMid} shows that it is nearly optimal when $\underline \mu=0.25$. In general, its relative performance, $\rho$, is always above $0.996$ when $\underline \mu \in (0.25,0.315)$.


\textbf{Example.} When $\nu$ is uniformly distributed over $[0,1]$, the optimal mechanism from \citet{alaei2024optimal} (see Figure \ref{fig: comparisonToPosted}) generates an expected revenue of $0.3808$, and the optimal posted price mechanism generates $0.25$. Assume that the prior is the same, but we use the robustly optimal mechanism with $\underline \mu = 0.5$. Then, the mechanism with the linear payment rule (Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism}) generates an expected revenue of $\int_0^1 \alpha \nu + \beta \text{d} \nu = \int_0^1 8 \nu/9 -1/9 \text{d} \nu \approx 0.3333$, whereas the mechanism with maximal payments (Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointMaximalPayment}) generates $0.3716$. Hence, the linear payment rule brings simplicity at the cost of performance but still outperforms the optimal Posted price mechanism. On the other hand, if complexity is not an issue, then using the robustly optimal mechanism with the maximal payment rule instead of the optimal mechanism tailored to a specific distribution ensures near-optimal performance guarantees and requires only the support and the expected value information, ensuring robustness against possible errors in data.


 \textbf{Numerical experiment.} We also report a numerical experiment to compare the performances of three mechanisms, namely the optimal mechanism from \citet{alaei2024optimal}, our two robustly optimal mechanisms with linear and maximal payment rules, when the type distribution is contaminated with the worst-case distribution that we identified in Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}. For this experiment, we consider a discrete type set to be able to use linear programming. The type set is $100$ equidistant points from $[0,1]$. We let $\mathbb P^N$ be the nominal distribution that puts $1/100$ probability to each point in the type set, and we let $\mathbb P^W$ be the worst-case distribution with the same expected value $1/2$. We will calculate the true type distribution as $\mathbb P^\varepsilon = (1-\varepsilon)\mathbb P^N + \varepsilon \mathbb P^W$ where $\varepsilon \in [0,1]$ is the contamination level,
 and report the performances of the three mechanisms across different contamination levels. Here, the performance of a mechanism refers to its expected payoff divided by the maximum achievable payoff under the unique prior $\mathbb P^\varepsilon$ without ambiguity.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figures/experimentPerformances.pdf}
\caption{Expected payoffs of the nominal mechanism (optimal under the unique prior $\mathbb P^N$), the robustly optimal mechanism with the linear payment rule, and the robustly optimal mechanism with maximal payments.}
    \label{fig: experiment}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig: experiment} visualizes the relative performances of the three mechanisms. As the figure shows, the robustly optimal mechanism with maximal payments consistently performs close to the true optimal solution and outperforms the nominal mechanism when the contamination level exceeds $0.324$. For contamination levels beyond $0.716$, the nominal mechanism performs worse than both of our robustly optimal mechanisms.  
Therefore, in addition to achieving the worst-case optimal payoffs, our robustly optimal mechanisms also perform favorably compared to optimal mechanisms designed for a misspecified distribution.

We also find the optimal mechanism when $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25]$ and propose an approximation mechanism for the remaining case. We relegate this part to the Appendix as the proofs for the optimal mechanism closely follow what we presented in this section, and the approximation result is only relevant for extremely small values of $\underline \mu$.




\section{Regret Minimization} \label{sec: worst-case regret minimization}

\subsection{The Model Under Support-Only Ambiguity Set}

In this section, we consider the same problem only with the difference that the seller wants to minimize her worst-case regret under the support-only ambiguity set $ \mathcal{P}_0([0,1])$. In this case, two and three-point distributions that we considered in the previous section are still feasible, as well as any other distribution with the support $[0,1]$. The seller needs to solve the following problem to minimize her worst-case regret
\begin{align}
    z^* = \inf_{x,p} \;&\sup_{\mathbb{P} \in \mathcal{P}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}[\nu - p(\nu)] \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \;&\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq \nu x(\hat{\nu}) - \hat{\nu} &\forall \nu,\hat \nu \in [0,1]: \hat \nu< \nu, \tag{\ref{IC}}\\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \tag{\ref{IR}}\\
    &x(\nu) \leq 1 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber\\
    &x(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in [0,1]. \nonumber
\end{align}

Using the duality argument from the proof of Lemma \ref{lem: linearPayment}, we can solve the following equivalent linear program instead
    \begin{align}
    z^* = \inf_{{x,p,\beta}} \; & \beta \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \; &\beta \geq \nu -  p(\nu)  &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber \\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq \nu x(\hat{\nu}) - \hat{\nu} &\forall \nu,\hat \nu \in [0,1]: \hat \nu< \nu, \tag{\ref{IC}}\\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \tag{\ref{IR}}\\
    &x(\nu) \leq 1 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber\\
    &x(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in [0,1]. \nonumber
\end{align}
This observation immediately tells us that it is again possible to solve this problem using a mechanism with a linear payment rule of $\nu - \beta$. We could use the feasible mechanism from the previous section that has a linear payment with a slope of $1$, but is it robustly optimal in this setting? We next show that the answer is affirmative.

We will first find lower bounds on the optimal worst-case regret value $z^*$ by considering plausible worst-case distributions from the support-only ambiguity set. We can deduce the following result as the worst-case distributions we identified in the previous section are contained in the support-only ambiguity set.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor: lower bounds}
    The seller's optimal worst-case regret $z^*$ is bounded below by $ \mu - \frac{ \mu}{2- \mu}$ for any $ \mu \in (0,1)$ and by $ \mu-\frac{ \mu}{3}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2$ for any $ \mu \in (0,(15-3\sqrt{5})/10]$, where $\delta= \frac{ \mu}{3- \mu}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figures/RegretFrom2and3PointDist.pdf}
\caption{The lower bounds in Corollary \ref{cor: lower bounds}.}
    \label{fig: regretUpperBounds}
\end{figure}


The proof of Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds} is also valid when we consider regret minimization because it fixes a two or three-point distribution with the expected value $\mu$ and finds the maximum payoff the seller can achieve. The difference between the expected value and the maximum payoff will be the seller's regret under the fixed distribution. These distributions are in the support-only ambiguity set, giving us a lower bound on the optimal worst-case regret. The lower bounds in Corollary \ref{cor: lower bounds} are illustrated in Figure \ref{fig: regretUpperBounds}. As the adversary wants to maximize the seller's regret, it would choose the distribution that gives the maximum lower bound. For instance, if restricted to these two and three-point distributions, the adversary can maximize the lower bound on regret via the two-point distribution with expectation $ \mu^* =2-\sqrt{2}$. This lower bound amounts to $ \mu^* - \frac{ \mu^*}{2- \mu^*}=3-2\sqrt{2}$. Our following result shows that the seller can use a mechanism from Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism}, which guarantees a worst-case regret of $3-2\sqrt{2}$. This mechanism should be optimal as it attains the maximum lower bound presented in Corollary \ref{cor: lower bounds}.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop: optimal mech for support only}
    Under the support-only ambiguity set, the following mechanism is robustly optimal with a worst-case regret of $3-2\sqrt{2}$,
    \begin{align*}
    &x^*(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu &\text{if } \nu \in [0, \underline \nu],\\
        1+ \frac{\beta}{\nu} &\text{if } \nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ],\\
        \nu+1-2 \sqrt{-\beta} &\text{if } \nu \in [\nu^\circ, \overline{\nu}],\\
        1 &\text{if } \nu \in [\overline{\nu},1],
    \end{cases}    &p^*(\nu) = \nu + \beta \quad \forall \nu \in [0,1],
\end{align*}
where $\beta =-(\sqrt{2}-1)^2$, $\underline \nu = -2\beta$, $\nu^\circ = \sqrt{-\beta}$, and $\overline{\nu}= 2\sqrt{-\beta}$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{The Model Under Markov Ambiguity Sets}

In this section, we consider regret minimization under Markov ambiguity sets of the form $\mathcal{P} = \{\mathbb{P} \in \mathcal{P}_0([0,1]): \mathbb{E}_{\mathbb{P}}[\nu] \in [\underline{\mu},\overline{\mu}]\}$. 
We again take the dual of the adversary's problem to find an equivalent problem for the seller
\begin{align}
    z^* = \inf_{{x,p,\beta, \overline{\alpha},\underline \alpha}} \;  & \overline{\alpha} \overline{\mu}-\underline \alpha \underline\mu +\beta  \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \; &(\overline{\alpha}-\underline \alpha) \nu+\beta \geq \nu -  p(\nu)  &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber \\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq \nu x(\hat{\nu}) - \hat{\nu} &\forall \nu,\hat \nu \in [0,1]: \hat \nu<\nu, \tag{\ref{IC}}\\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \tag{\ref{IR}}\\
    &x(\nu) \leq 1 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber\\
    &x(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber\\
    &\overline{\alpha},\underline \alpha \geq 0. \nonumber
\end{align}
The seller's problem stays the same except for the variables $\overline{\alpha}$ and $\underline \alpha$. Then, we can again restrict attention to mechanisms with a linear payment rule $\nu(1-\overline{\alpha}+\underline \alpha)-\beta$, but the slope may have to change compared to the support-only case.

Recall from the support-only case that the worst-case distribution has an expected value of $\mu^* = 2-\sqrt{2}$. Hence, for any Markov ambiguity set that contains $\mu^*$, the adversary would pick the corresponding two-point distribution that maximizes the worst-case regret of the seller as shown in Corollary \ref{cor: lower bounds}, and the seller would counter by choosing the robustly optimal mechanism from Proposition \ref{prop: optimal mech for support only}. For the case $[\underline \mu, \overline \mu] \not \ni \mu^*$, the worst-case distributions from Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds} and the mechanism from Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism} turn out to be still relevant in this setup.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop: optimal mech for markov}
    If $[\underline \mu, \overline \mu] \cap [0.315,1) \neq \emptyset$, then the mechanism in \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism} defined for $\mu$ is robustly optimal and gives a worst-case regret of $z^*=\mu-\mu/(2- \mu)$, where
    \begin{align*}
    \mu &= \begin{cases}
        \overline{\mu} &\text{if }\overline \mu < \mu^*,\\
        \mu^* &\text{if } \underline \mu \leq \mu^* \leq \overline{\mu},\\
        \underline \mu &\text{if } \mu^* < \underline \mu,
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
and $\mu^* = 2-\sqrt{2}$.
\end{proposition}


Following the same reasoning in Proposition \ref{prop: optimal mech for markov}, if $\overline{\mu} \in [0.107,0.25]$, then the mechanism in Proposition \ref{prop: threePointMechanism} defined for $\overline \mu$ is robustly optimal (see Section \ref{sec: smallMu}). This is because the adversary archives the maximum regret under a three-point distribution with the expected value $\overline{\mu}$, and the expected regret will be minimized under the mechanism defined in Proposition \ref{prop: threePointMechanism}. Similarly, if $\overline{\mu} \in (0.25,0.315)$, then one should use the mechanism in Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism} to approximate the optimal mechanism, whereas for $\overline{\mu} \in (0,0.107)$, the mechanism in Proposition \ref{prop: threePointMechanismMaximal} approximates the optimal solution.


\section{Multiple Agents} \label{sec: multipleAgents}

In this section, we report our numerical results on the multiple-agent case and discuss the properties of a robustly optimal mechanism. Due to the intricate nature of this problem, we limit the discussion to numerics and leave the closed-form robustly optimal mechanism as a future research direction.

Up until this section, we tackled the problem with a single agent and argued that a linear payment rule suffices for robust optimality. This observation allowed us to derive the allocation rule by leveraging individual rationality and incentive compatibility constraints. However, in scenarios involving multiple agents, the requirement that the sum of all agents' payments must be linear does not provide sufficient information to determine individual allocation rules under the dominant incentive compatibility constraints.

To illustrate the complexities, we present a case with two agents whose type sets are composed of 50 equidistant points within the interval \([0,1]\), where the lowest type is 0 and the highest type is 1. We focus on a scenario where both agents have a minimum expected value of $0.5$. The robust optimal mechanism is derived under the constraints that Agent 1's allocation rule should be increasing in his type and decreasing in Agent 2's type, and the mechanism should maintain symmetry between agents. Figure \ref{fig: multipleAgentSolution} illustrates a robustly optimal mechanism under this setting. Note that we do not impose monotonicity on the payment rule, as enforcing such constraints leads to infeasibility in this case. That is, a robustly optimal mechanism under these conditions does not exist. 

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=1]{figures/2agentMechanism.pdf}
\caption{A robustly optimal mechanism when there are two agents with 50 equidistant points within the interval \([0,1]\) and $\underline \mu=0.5$ for all $i=1,2$.}
    \label{fig: multipleAgentSolution}
\end{figure}
The first notable aspect in the figure is that the allocation rule for Agent 1, when Agent 2's type is zero, closely resembles the robustly optimal mechanism for the single-agent case (see Figure \ref{fig: optimalTwoPointMechanisms}). Additionally, in this scenario, Agent 1's payment is almost linear. This similarity arises because the linear payment, which ensures robust optimality, should be received from Agent 1, as no payment can be extracted from Agent 2 with a type of zero. Hence, our approach from the one agent case can identify the optimal allocation rule $x_1(\nu_1,0)$.
However, as $\nu_2$ increases, this resemblance quickly fades. The allocation rule for a fixed $\nu_2>0$ may even be discontinuous, making its closed form difficult to predict. 
Lastly, notice that the payment rule for agent 1 in Figure \ref{fig: multipleAgentSolution} is not monotone in Agent 2's type $\nu_2$. This non-monotonicity is a direct consequence of the complexity introduced by multiple agents and the necessity to maintain robustness. Although the allocation to agent 1 is decreasing in agent 2's type, the payment from agent 1 does not necessarily decrease with $\nu_2$ but can increase up until $\nu_2=\nu_1$.


\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[scale=1]{figures/2agentMechanismLinear.pdf}
\caption{A robustly optimal mechanism when the payment rule of agent 1 with type $\nu_1$ must be linear for all $\nu_2 \in [0,1]$.}
 \label{fig: multipleAgentSolutionLinear}
\end{figure}
One might ask at this point if we can find a mechanism with a linear payment rule with a good worst-case performance. To answer this question, we restrict the feasible set to symmetric mechanisms with linear payment rules that satisfy $p_1(\nu_1,\nu_2)= \alpha(\nu_2) \nu_1 + \beta(\nu_2)$ for all $\nu_1,\nu_2 \in [0,1]$ and repeat our numerical experiment. Figure \ref{fig: multipleAgentSolutionLinear} illustrates the mechanism that maximizes the worst-case expected payoff. This mechanism has a worst-case expected payoff of $0.4798$, whereas the robustly optimal mechanism from Figure \ref{fig: multipleAgentSolution} has an expected payoff of $0.4948$ at the worst-case. Hence, restricting the feasible set to linear payment rules can approximate the robustly optimal solution with a relative performance guarantee of $0.969$. Therefore, an approximation mechanism with linear payment rules can perform close to optimality while also being easier to pin down and implement. Nevertheless, one first needs to find the optimal values of $\alpha(\nu_2)$ and $\beta(\nu_2)$ functions, which can be challenging.


These observations highlight the intricate balance required in robust mechanism design when scaling from single to multiple agents. Developing closed-form solutions for the multi-agent case describes a significant challenge and one that we leave for future research.

\section{Concluding Remarks} \label{sec: conclusion}

Our paper focuses on the mechanism design problem when the designer can observe valuations ex-post, also known as {auction} design with costless inspection. Motivated by real-world applications, we build upon prior research by \citet{alaei2024optimal} by relaxing the common prior assumption, which is rarely known in practice. 
We focus on the single-agent case and assume ambiguity aversion on the part of the principal, {and consider both the worst-case payoff maximization and worst-case regret minimization}. Our findings suggest that in scenarios where information on the common prior is limited to its support and expectation, robust optimality cannot be achieved by a posted price mechanism alone. Instead, we propose linear payment rules as sufficient mechanisms, which can be easy to use but feature negative payments for some small types. We explicitly describe two robustly optimal mechanisms (with linear and {maximal} payment rules), which perform reasonably well compared to the optimal mechanism tailored to a specific type distribution even when the distribution is fully known. We argue through a numerical experiment that our robustly optimal mechanisms perform favorably if the prior distribution is unknown.
Our study provides valuable insights into the design of robust mechanisms in scenarios where the common prior assumption does not hold, offering practical solutions to mechanism design problems in real-world settings.

In the study of \citet{alaei2024optimal} and ours, the buyers know the value (e.g., future profit or turnover) with certainty. This value might be based on forecasts. In practice, a principal might allow for some deviations in the reported values. However, the uncertainty of buyer valuations also constitutes a future research direction beyond this paper.

