\section{Optimal mechanisms for small $\underline \mu$}\label{sec: smallMu}


This section proposes another mechanism tailored for the three-point worst-case distribution from the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}. It is optimal when $\underline \mu \in [0.107, 0.25]$ and approximates the optimal solution when $\underline \mu \in (0,0.107)$. In the latter case, when $\underline \mu \in (0,0.107)$, the adversary can use a four-point discrete distribution that gives an upper for the worst-case expected payoff lower than those provided in Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}. Finding the optimal mechanism in this case necessitates repeating every step in this paper for worst-case distributions with four points, which would lengthen the paper without adding more insight. Hence, we do not pursue optimality for this extremely small $\underline \mu$ case and propose an approximation mechanism that performs close to optimality.


\begin{proposition}\label{prop: threePointMechanism}
If $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25]$, then the following mechanism is robustly optimal with a worst-case expected payoff of $\frac{\underline \mu}{3}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2$,
\begin{equation*}\label{eq:linearPaymentMechanismSmallmu}
    \begin{aligned}
    &x^s(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu &\text{if } \nu \in [0, \underline \nu],\\
        \alpha+ \frac{\beta}{\nu} &\text{if } \nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ],\\
        -\frac{\beta}{ {\nu^\circ}^2}\nu + \alpha + 2\frac{\beta}{\nu^\circ} &\text{if } \nu \in [\nu^\circ,\nu^\star],\\
        \nu-\overline{\nu} +1 &\text{if } \nu \in [\nu^\star, \overline{\nu}],\\
        1 &\text{if } \nu \in [\overline{\nu},1],
\end{cases}
&p^s(\nu) = \alpha \nu+ \beta \quad \forall \nu \in [0,1],
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
where $\delta= \frac{\underline \mu}{3-\underline \mu}$, $\beta =-\delta\sqrt{\delta}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)$, $\alpha = (\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2/3-\beta/\underline \mu$, 
$\underline \nu = -2\beta/\alpha$, 
$\nu^\circ = (-\beta - \sqrt{\beta^2+\tau \beta \nu^\star})/\tau$, $\nu^\star = 2-\alpha -2\sqrt{1-\overline{\nu}}$, $\tau = \alpha -\nu^\star +\overline{\nu}-1$ and $\overline{\nu}= \alpha + \beta$.
\end{proposition}

\noindent{\bf Proof of Proposition \ref{prop: threePointMechanism}}. 
    To prove that $(x^s,p^s)$ is robustly optimal, we will show that it is feasible and generates an expected payoff of $\frac{\underline \mu}{3}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2$ for $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25]$, attaining the upper bound given in Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}. That is, $(x^s,p^s)$ is feasible in the formulation given in Lemma \ref{lem: linearPayment}, and it satisfies $p^s(\underline \mu) = \alpha \underline \mu +\beta =\frac{\underline \mu}{3}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2$. The latter follows trivially from the definitions of $\alpha$ and $\beta$.

    To show that $(x^s,p^s)$ is a feasible mechanism, we will prove that it is well-defined, satisfies \eqref{IC}, \eqref{IR} constraints, and its allocation is always between $0$ and $1$. To prove that it is well-defined, we must show that $\nu^\circ$ and $\nu^\star$ are well-defined real numbers. In particular, we need $1-\overline{\nu} \geq 0$ for a real-valued $\nu^\star$, and $\beta^2+\tau \beta \nu^\star \geq 0$ and $\tau \neq 0$ for a well-defined and real valued $\nu^\circ$.
    Since these are quite complex functions of $\underline \mu$, we refer to their plots given in Figures \ref{fig: discriminantForNuCirc} and \ref{fig: typesForThreePointMechanism}, which ensure that both requirements hold for $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25]$. From Figure \ref{fig: typesForThreePointMechanism}, it is also apparent that $0 \leq \underline \nu \leq \nu^\circ \leq \nu^\star \leq \overline{\nu} \leq 1$ holds. Note that $\tau$ is strictly positive for the interval $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25]$ and equals zero when $\underline \mu \approx 0.1067$. 

