    Next, we prove some properties of $x^s$ that will help us in proving $x^s(\nu) \in [0,1]$ for any $\nu \in [0,1]$. First, we show that $x^s$ is continuous for $\nu \in [0,1]$, for which we only need to show that $x^s$ takes the same values at the corresponding endpoints of the intervals. The proof for $-\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2} \underline \nu = \alpha + \frac{\beta}{\underline \nu}$ is presented in the proof of Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism}. The equalities for $\nu^\circ$ and $\overline{\nu}$ follow directly from their definition and the definition of $x^s$. Hence, we only need to prove the continuity at the end point $\nu^\star$. We subtract the two allocation functions for the point $\nu^\star$ and show that it is equal to zero:
    \begin{align*}
        -\frac{\beta}{{\nu^\circ}^2} \nu^\star + \alpha +2\frac{\beta}{\nu^\circ} - (\nu^\star -\overline{\nu}+1)
        = \big[ (\alpha -\nu^\star +\overline{\nu}-1) {\nu^\circ}^2 +2\beta\nu^\circ -\beta \nu^\star \big]/{\nu^\circ}^2.
    \end{align*}
    As $\nu^\circ$ is strictly positive for the interval $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25)$ (see Figure \ref{fig: typesForThreePointMechanism}), the above function is well-defined and equals zero if the numerator is zero. Notice that the numerator is a quadratic function of $\nu^\circ$ that can be easily solved for $\nu^\circ$ values that make it equal to zero. Since its discriminant is $\beta^2 + \tau \beta \nu^\star$, which is non-negative as depicted in Figure \ref{fig: discriminantForNuCirc}, it has real roots. One can check that one of the roots is equal to $\nu^\circ$ by definition, which means that $x^s$ is also continuous $\nu^\star$.

    Another useful property of $x^s$ is that it is weakly increasing over $\nu$, $i.e.$, its first derivative with respect to $\nu$ is non-negative. The first derivatives are $-\beta/\underline \nu^2$ for interval $[0,\underline \nu]$, $-\beta/\nu^2$ for $[\underline \nu,\nu^\circ]$, $-\beta/ {\nu^\circ}^2$ for $[\nu^\circ,\nu^\star]$, $1$ for $[\nu^\star,\overline{\nu}]$, and $0$ for $[\overline{\nu},1]$, which are all non-negative as $\beta \leq 0$. Hence, as $x^s$ is weakly increasing and continuous for $\nu \in [0,1]$, and $x^s(0)=0$, and $x^s(1)=1$ by definition, we must have $x^s(\nu) \in [0,1]$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$. 

    Here, we also note two other properties of $x^s$ that will be helpful in proving \eqref{IC} constraints. First, the first derivative $x^s$ is non-increasing in $\nu$, because $-\beta /\underline \nu^2 \geq - \beta/{\nu^\circ}^2 \geq 1$ where the first inequality follows from $\beta \leq 0$ and $\underline \nu \leq \nu^\circ \leq 1$, whereas the second inequality follows from $-{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta \leq 1$ as shown in Figure \ref{fig: typesForThreePointMechanism}. Second, the second derivative of $x^s$ is always non-positive, which makes $x^s$ a concave function. 


    To prove that $(x^s,p^s)$ is individually rational, $i.e.$, it satisfies \eqref{IR}, we need to show that $x^s(\nu) \geq \alpha  +\beta/\nu$ holds holds for all $\nu \in [0,1]$. It holds with equality for $\nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ]$ by definition. Notice that $\alpha  +\beta/\nu$ is strictly concave as $\beta <0$. Then, the following inequalities are satisfied for any $\nu$:
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu} + \frac{-\beta}{ \nu^2}(\nu'- \nu) &\geq \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu'} &\forall \nu' \in[0,1].
    \end{align*}
   When $\nu = \underline \nu$, the left-hand side of the above inequality is equal to $x^s(\nu')$ for all $\nu' \in [0, \underline \nu]$ (see proof of Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism}).
    Next, we prove that, when $\nu = \nu^\circ$, the left-hand side is equal to $x^s(\nu')$ for $\nu' \in [\nu^\circ, {\nu}^\star]$:
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu^\circ} + \frac{-\beta}{{\nu^\circ}^2}(\nu'- \nu^\circ) &= -\frac{\beta}{{\nu^\circ}^2}\nu' + \alpha +2\frac{\beta}{\nu^\circ} \geq \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu'}.
    \end{align*}
    Hence, for $\nu \in [0,\nu^\star]$, satisfaction of \eqref{IR} follows from $\alpha+\beta/\nu$ and its concavity.
    For $\nu \in [\nu^\star, \overline{\nu}]$, the \eqref{IR} constraint is satisfied if the following is non-negative:
    \begin{align*}
        \nu - \overline{\nu} +1 -(\alpha\nu +\beta) &= \nu(1-\alpha) - \alpha -2\beta +1 =(1-\alpha)(\nu +1) -2 \beta.
    \end{align*}
    As $\beta \leq 0$, this term is non-negative if $\alpha \leq 1$. We next show that this is true for $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25)$:
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha &= \frac{(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2}{3} - \frac{\beta}{\underline \mu} = (\sqrt{\delta}+1) \big( \frac{\sqrt{\delta} +1}{3} + \frac{\sqrt{\delta}}{3-\underline \mu} \big) = (\sqrt{\delta}+1) \big( \frac{1}{3} + \sqrt{\delta}(\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3-\underline \mu}) \big) \\
        &= \frac{\sqrt{\delta}+1}{3} + (\frac{\underline \mu}{3-\underline \mu}+\sqrt{\delta})(\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3-\underline \mu}) \leq \frac{1/2+1}{3} + (\frac{1/4}{3-\underline \mu}+\frac{1}{2})(\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3-\underline \mu})\\
        &\leq \frac{1}{2} + (\frac{1/4}{11/4}+\frac{1}{2})(\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{11/4}) = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{13}{22} \cdot \frac{23}{33} \leq 1,
    \end{align*}    
    where the inequalities follow from $\delta \leq \underline \mu \leq 0.25$. Lastly, when $\nu \in [\overline{\nu},1]$, we can write \eqref{IR} as $\nu x^*(\nu) -(\alpha \nu +\beta) =\nu(1-\alpha)-\beta \geq 0$, which follows again from $\alpha \leq 1$. Hence, $(x^s,p^s)$ satisfies the \eqref{IR} constraints.

    To prove that $(x^s,p^s)$ is incentive compatible, $i.e.$, satisfies \eqref{IC}, we need to show the following for all $\nu \in[0,1]$:
    \begin{align*}
    \nu(x^s(\nu)-\alpha) -\beta + \min_{\hat \nu \in [0,\nu]} -\nu x^s(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu \geq 0.    
    \end{align*}
    By construction, for any fixed $\nu$, the function $-\nu x^s(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu$ is convex as $x^s$ is concave, $i.e.$, $\text{d}x^s(\nu)/\text{d}{\nu}$ is decreasing over $\nu$ by definition. Hence, we can identify the minimizer by inspecting the derivative of $-\nu x^s(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu$ with respect to $\hat \nu$:
    \begin{align}\label{eq: best deviation}
    - \nu \frac{\text{d}x^s(\hat \nu)}{\text{d}\hat{\nu}} +1 &= \begin{cases}
        \nu\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2} +1 &\text{if } \hat \nu \in [0,\underline \nu],\\
        \nu\frac{\beta}{\hat \nu^2} +1 &\text{if } \hat \nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ],\\
        \nu\frac{\beta}{{\nu^\circ}^2} +1 &\text{if } \hat \nu \in [\nu^\circ, \nu^\star],\\
        -\nu +1 &\text{if } \hat \nu \in [\nu^\star, \overline \nu],\\
        1 &\text{if } \hat \nu \in [\overline \nu,1].
    \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    If \eqref{eq: best deviation} is non-negative for all $\hat \nu \in [0, \nu]$, then the minimum is attained at $\hat \nu=0$, meaning that \eqref{IC} reduces to \eqref{IR}, which is already proven above. As we already showed the first derivative of $x^s$ is non-increasing in $\hat \nu$, for any $\nu \leq -\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta}$,
    \eqref{eq: best deviation} is non-negative for all $\hat \nu \in [0,1]$, so that \eqref{IC} is satisfied via \eqref{IR}. For $\nu > -\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta}$, the minimum will be attained at some $\hat \nu \in [0, \nu]$, so that proving non-negativity at this point is enough to show that \eqref{IC} holds. Since $x^s$ is concave, the minimum will be attained when \eqref{eq: best deviation} equals zero or when it changes signs at an endpoint, which can happen in two different instances. First, when $\hat \nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ]$, we may have:
    \begin{align*}
    - \nu \frac{\text{d}x^s(\hat \nu)}{\text{d}\hat{\nu}}\Big\rvert_{\hat \nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ]} +1 = -\nu\frac{-\beta}{\hat \nu^2} +1 = 0 \quad  
    \Rightarrow \quad \hat \nu = \sqrt{-\beta \nu},
    \end{align*}
    which can be satisfied only for $\nu \in [-\underline \nu^2/\beta, -{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta]$. 
    Second, \eqref{eq: best deviation} can change signs when $\hat \nu = \nu^\star$ and $\nu \beta / {\nu^\circ}^2 +1 \leq 0$, which can happen only if $\nu \geq -{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta$.
    Hence, for $\nu \in [-\underline \nu^2/\beta, 1]$,
    the minimum value of $-\nu x^s(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu$ is given by 
    \begin{align*}
     &= \begin{cases}
       -\nu \alpha + 2\sqrt{-\beta \nu} &\text{if } \nu \in [-\underline \nu^2/\beta,-{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta],\\
        -\nu (\nu^\star-\overline{\nu} +1) + \nu^\star &\text{if } \nu \in [-{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta,1].
    \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    First, we show that \eqref{IC} is satisfied for all $\nu \in [-{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta,1]$, which reduces to the following inequality:
    \begin{align}
    \nu(x^s(\nu)-\alpha) -\beta + \min_{\hat \nu \in [0,\nu]} -\nu x^s(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu = \nu(x^s(\nu)-\alpha) -\beta -\nu(\nu^\star-\overline{\nu}+1)+\nu^\star \geq 0.
    \label{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll2}
    \end{align}
    Since $-{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta \in [\nu^\star,\overline{\nu}]$ (see Figure \ref{fig: typesForThreePointMechanism}), the left-hand side of \eqref{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll2} equals to the following two functions that follow from the definition of $x^s(\nu)$:
    \begin{align*}
    \begin{cases}
     \nu(\nu-\nu^\star-\alpha) -\beta +\nu^\star &\text{if } \nu \in [-\frac{{\nu^\circ}^2}{\beta}, \overline{\nu}],\\
     \nu(\overline{\nu}-\nu^\star-\alpha) -\beta+\nu^\star &\text{if } \nu \in [\overline{\nu},1].
    \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    The first function above is a quadratic convex function of $\nu$, whereas the second function is linear. To ensure that a linear function is non-negative for some interval, we can check its values at some endpoints beyond this interval. For $\nu =0$, it becomes $-\beta +\nu^\star$, which is non-negative since $\beta \leq 0$ by construction. For $\nu=1$, it equals zero since $\overline{\nu} = \alpha +\beta$ by construction. To ensure that a quadratic convex function is non-negative, we can check its minimum value.
    Its minimum is attained when $\nu = (\nu^\star+\alpha)/2$ and equals:
    \begin{align*}
        -\big(\frac{\nu^\star+\alpha}{2}\big)^2-\beta+\nu^\star &= -(1-\sqrt{1-\overline{\nu}})^2-\beta+\nu^\star= -2 +\overline{\nu}+2\sqrt{1-\overline{\nu}} -\beta+\nu^\star\\
        &= \alpha-2+2\sqrt{1-\overline{\nu}} +\nu^\star = 0,
    \end{align*}
    where the equalities follow from the definitions of $\nu^\star$ and $\overline{\nu}$.
    Lastly, we need to show that the following holds for all $\nu \in [-\underline \nu^2/\beta, -{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta]$:
    \begin{align}
    \nu(x^s(\nu)-\alpha) -\beta + \min_{\hat \nu \in [0,\nu]} -\nu x^s(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu = \nu(x^s(\nu)-2\alpha) -\beta +2\sqrt{-\beta \nu} \geq 0.
    \label{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll}
    \end{align}
    As it can be seen in Figure \ref{fig: typesForThreePointMechanism}, for any $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25]$, we have $\nu^\star \leq -\underline \nu^2/\beta  \leq -{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta \leq \overline{\nu}$.
    Hence, we only need to consider the case $x^s(\nu) = \nu-\overline{\nu}+1$ for any $\nu \in [-\underline \nu^2/\beta, -{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta]$. Then, \eqref{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll} reduces to the following:
    \begin{align}\label{eq: ICnonnegativityForAllReduced}
     \nu(\nu-\overline{\nu}+1-2\alpha)-\beta+2\sqrt{-\beta \nu} \geq 0.
    \end{align}
