Figure \ref{fig: threePointMechanism} illustrates the robustly optimal mechanism $(x^s,p^s)$, which has a concave allocation and linear payment rules.
It is only guaranteed to be feasible when $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25]$. We identified the bounds of this interval through numerical experiments as the mechanism is defined through high-order polynomials of $\underline \mu$.

Next, we present the mechanism that extracts the maximal payment under the same allocation rule $x^s$. We will use this mechanism to approximate the optimal solution of the case $\underline \mu \in (0,0.107)$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop: threePointMechanismMaximal}
    If $\underline \mu \in [0.107,0.25]$, then the following payment rule, $p_m^s$ extracts the maximum feasible payment from each type under $x^s$, and $(x^s,p_m^s)$ is robustly optimal:
        \begin{align*}
        p_m^s(\nu) = 
        &\begin{cases}
        \nu x^s(\nu) &\text{if } \nu \in [0,\nu'],\\
        \nu(\nu -\overline{\nu}+1 -\alpha)+2\sqrt{-\beta \nu} &\text{if } \nu \in [\nu',\nu''],\\
        \nu(\nu-\nu^\star)+\nu^\star &\text{if } \nu \in [\nu'', \overline{\nu}],\\
        \nu(\overline{\nu}-\nu^\star)+\nu^\star &\text{if } \nu \in [\overline{\nu},1],
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
where $\nu' = -\underline \nu^2/\beta$ and $\nu'' = -{\nu^\circ}^2/\beta$.
\end{proposition}

