We close this section by proposing an approximation mechanism for the case of $\underline \mu \in (0,0.107)$.
\begin{proposition} \label{prop: approximation}
    If $\underline \mu \in (0, 0.107)$, let $z^*$ be the optimal worst-case expected payoff, and let $z_{\underline \mu^*}$ be the worst-case expected payoff under the mechanism $(x^s,p_m^s)$ defined for $\underline \mu^* = 0.107$. The relative and absolute performance guarantees of $(x^s,p_m^s)$ are $z_{\underline \mu^*} \geq \rho z^*$ and $z_{\underline \mu^*} \geq z^*-c$ respectively, where $\rho = z_{\underline \mu^*}/b$, $c=b-z_{\underline \mu^*}$,
\begin{align*}
    z_{\underline \mu^*} =\begin{cases}
        \underline \mu x^s(\underline \mu) &\text{if } \underline \mu \in [0,\underline \nu_{\underline \mu^*}],\\
        \underline \mu \alpha + \beta, &\text{if } \underline \mu \in [\underline \nu_{\underline \mu^*}, \underline \mu^*],
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
$b = \min\{\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu),\underline \mu(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2/3\}$, and $\underline \nu$, $\alpha$, and $\beta$ are the parameters of $(x^s,p_m^s)$ as defined in Proposition \ref{prop: threePointMechanism}.
\end{proposition}

\noindent{\bf Proof of Proposition \ref{prop: approximation}}. 
    Given any $\underline \mu \in (0, 0.107)$, the relative and absolute performance guarantees follow by their definition when $z_{\underline \mu^*}$ is the worst-case expected payoff under $(x^s,p_m^s)$, and $b$ is an upper bound for $z^*$. By definition, $b$ is an upper bound of $z^*$, which is defined in Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}. To complete the proof, we must show that $z_{\underline \mu^*}$ is the worst-case expected payoff under $(x^s,p_m^s)$. By Lemma \ref{lem: linearPayment}, we can equivalently show that there exists a linear line that is below $p_m^s(\nu)$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$, which is equal to $z_{\underline \mu^*}$ when $\nu =\underline \mu$.    

    Consider the payment made by type $\nu \in (0, 0.107)$ under the mechanism $(x^s,p_m^s)$ defined for the fixed $\underline \mu^* = 0.107$. It satisfies the following:
    \begin{align*}
            p_m^s(\nu) \geq \begin{cases}
            -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2} \nu^2 &\text{if } \nu \in [0,\underline \nu],\\
            \alpha \nu +\beta &\text{if } \nu \in [\underline \nu,1],
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    where the inequality follows from the definition of $p_m^s$ and from the fact that $p_m^s(\nu) \geq \alpha \nu +\beta$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$. 
    Then, by definition, there exists a linear line, $\alpha \nu +\beta$, that is below $p_m^s(\nu)$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$ and equals to $z_{\underline \mu^*}$ when $\nu =\underline \mu \in [\underline \nu,1]$. Next, notice that $-\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu^2$ is a convex function of $\nu$ as $\beta \leq 0$, so that, for any $\underline \mu \in (0,\underline \nu]$, it satisfies:
    \begin{align}\label{eq: approximationLowerBound}
    -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu^2 \geq -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\underline \mu^2+ (\nu-\underline \mu)(-2\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\underline \mu) \quad \forall \nu \in [0,1],
    \end{align}
    and the above right-hand side is a linear function of $\nu$. It is easy to see that this linear function reduces to $z_{\underline \mu^*}$ when $\nu =\underline \mu$, and the left-hand side of \eqref{eq: approximationLowerBound} equals $p_m^s(\nu)$ by definition when $\nu =\underline \mu \in (0,\underline \nu]$. We conclude the proof by showing that this linear function is also below $p_m^s(\nu)$ for $\nu \in [\underline \nu,1]$. This follows from the following:
    \begin{align*}
        -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\underline \mu^2+ (\nu-\underline \mu)(-2\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\underline \mu) &= -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\underline \mu^2+ (\nu-\underline \nu+\underline \nu-\underline \mu)(-2\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2} \underline \mu) \leq p_m^s(\underline \nu)+ (\nu-\underline \nu)(-2\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\underline \mu) \\
        &\leq p_m^s(\underline \nu)+ (\nu-\underline \nu)(-2\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\underline \nu) \leq \alpha \nu +\beta \leq p_m^s(\nu),
    \end{align*}
    where the first inequality follows from \eqref{eq: approximationLowerBound} and the definition of $p_m^s$, the second inequality from $\nu \in [\underline \nu,1]$ and $-\beta/\underline \nu^2 \geq 0$, the penultimate inequality from the definitions of $p_m^s(\underline \nu)$ and $\underline \nu$, and the last inequality from the fact that $p_m^s(\nu) \geq \alpha \nu +\beta$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$. This completes the proof.
\hfill \qed  \\


