Figure \ref{fig: approximation} shows the performance indicators of $(x^*,p_m^*)$. We see that it is near-optimal if $\underline \mu$ is close to $0.107$ and performs fairly well when it is not too small. As $\underline \mu$ approaches zero, the relative and absolute performance guarantees both tend to zero. This means that for the small values of $\underline \mu$, $(x^*,p_m^*)$ fails to perform well relative to the optimal worst-case expected payoff ($\rho \leq 0.5$ when $\underline \mu \leq 0.02$), whereas the distance to optimality will also be small ($c \leq 10^{-3}$ for all $\underline \mu \in [0,0.107]$). This is because the worst-case expected payoff guarantee is extremely small for small values of $\underline \mu$, even under the robustly optimal mechanism that we approximate.

\section{Relegated Proofs}

\noindent{\bf Proof of Lemma \ref{lem: weaklyIncreasingPayment}}.
    Consider a robustly optimal mechanism $(x,p)$ that satisfies the conditions in Proposition \ref{prop: resultsAlaei}. Due to feasibility, it has to satisfy \eqref{IC} constraints for all $\nu \in [0,1]$:
    \begin{align*}
        p(\nu) &\leq \nu(x(\nu)- x(\hat \nu)) + \hat \nu &\forall \hat \nu \in [0,1].
    \end{align*}
    Then for any $\nu^\circ \geq \nu$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        p(\nu) &\leq \nu(x(\nu^\circ)- x(\hat \nu)) + \hat \nu &\forall \hat \nu \in [0,1],
    \end{align*}
    as, from Proposition \ref{prop: resultsAlaei}, we know that $x$ is weakly increasing. We can use a subset of these inequalities to deduce that
    \begin{align*}
        p(\nu) &\leq \nu^\circ(x(\nu^\circ)- x(\hat \nu)) + \hat \nu &\forall \hat \nu \in [0,\nu^\circ],
    \end{align*}
    as $x(\nu^\circ)\geq  x(\hat \nu)$ for all $\nu^\circ \geq \hat \nu$. Using the definition of $p$ given in Proposition \ref{prop: resultsAlaei}, this leads to
    \begin{align*}
        p(\nu) &\leq p(\nu^\circ) = \inf_{\hat \nu \leq \nu^\circ} \{ \nu^\circ(x(\nu^\circ)- x(\hat \nu)) + \hat \nu \}.
    \end{align*}
    This completes our proof.
\hfill \qed  \\

\noindent{\bf Proof of Lemma \ref{lem: linearPayment}}. 
    Consider a robustly optimal mechanism $(x,p)$ that satisfies the conditions in Proposition \ref{prop: resultsAlaei}. 
    %\HB{I will add results from \cite{alaei2024optimal} which apply to our setting and show that the optimal robust mechanism is non-decreasing on $\nu$.} 
    To minimize the seller's payoff, nature needs to solve the following problem:
    \begin{align*}
    z^*=\inf_{\mathbb{P}} \; & \int_0^1 p(\nu) \mathbb{P}(\nu) \; d \nu\\
    \text{s.t.} \; &\int_0^1 \nu \mathbb{P}(\nu)  \; d \nu \leq \overline \mu, \\&\int_0^1 \nu \mathbb{P}(\nu)  \; d \nu \geq \underline \mu, \\
    &\int_0^1 \mathbb{P}(\nu) \; d \nu =1.
    \end{align*}
    Here, the upper bound constraint on the expectation is redundant because nature wants to minimize the worst-case payoff, and the payment rule $p$ is weakly increasing due to Lemma \ref{lem: weaklyIncreasingPayment}. The dual of nature's problem is given as follows:
    \begin{align}
    z^*=\sup_{\alpha, \beta} \; & \alpha \underline \mu + \beta \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \; &\alpha \nu + \beta \leq p(\nu) &\forall \nu \in [0,1], \nonumber \\
    &\alpha \geq 0. \nonumber
    \end{align}
    In other words, nature's problem is equivalent to finding the linear function that is always below $p(\nu)$ and maximizing its value at $\underline \mu$. Hence, to find a robustly optimal mechanism, the principal can solve for a feasible mechanism $(x,p)$ and $\alpha \in \mathbb R_+$, $\beta \in \mathbb R$ that maximize $\alpha \underline \mu + \beta$ while satisfying the constraint $\alpha \nu + \beta \leq p(\nu)$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$.
\hfill \qed  \\

\noindent{\bf Proof of Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}}. 
    Limiting the adversary to pick a specific distribution from the ambiguity set would result in a higher expected revenue for the seller. Hence, to derive our first upper bound, we consider the two-point distribution that draws type $1$ with probability $\alpha= \underline \mu /2$ and type $\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}$ with probability $1-\alpha$. This distribution indeed belongs to our ambiguity set as $\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu} < \underline \mu < 1$ for any $\underline \mu \in (0,1)$ and
    $$
    \alpha(1)+ (1-\alpha)\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}= \frac{\underline \mu}{2}+ (1-\frac{\underline \mu}{2})\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu} = \frac{\underline \mu}{2} + \frac{\underline \mu}{2} =\underline \mu.
    $$
    From now on, we let $\nu^*$ denote the type $\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}$. 

    When the adversary is limited to this two-point distribution, our objective reduces to the expected payment received from types $1$ and $\nu^*$. Hence, all other types, $\nu \in [0,1) \setminus \{\nu^*\}$, do not contribute to the seller's revenue. To avoid violating our constraints, we let
    \begin{align*}
    &x(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        0 &\text{if } \nu < \nu^*,\\
        x(\nu^*) &\text{if } \nu \in (\nu^*,1),
    \end{cases}
    &p(\nu) = \begin{cases}
        0 &\text{if } \nu < \nu^*,\\
        p(\nu^*) &\text{if } \nu \in (\nu^*,1),
    \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    for all $\nu \in [0,1) \setminus \{\nu^*\}$. Through this reduction, we maintain feasibility and safely omit all variables and constraints for the types other than $1$ and $\nu^*$. This leads to the following problem for the principal:
    \begin{align*}
    z^*\leq \max_{x,p} \;& \alpha p(1)+ (1- \alpha)p(\nu^*) \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \;&x(1)- p(1) \geq x({\nu}^*) - {\nu}^*\\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in \{\nu^*,1\},\\
    &x(\nu) \leq 1 &\forall \nu \in \{\nu^*,1\},\\
    &x(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in \{\nu^*,1\}.
    \end{align*}
    In this problem, the only upper bound for the payment received from $\nu^*$ comes from its \eqref{IR} constraint: $p(\nu^*) \leq \nu^* x(\nu^*)$. On the other hand, $p(1)$ is upper bounded by $\min\{x(1),x(1)-x(\nu^*) + \nu^*\}$, which follows from the \eqref{IC} and \eqref{IR} constraints of type $1$. As we maximize the expected payment, setting all $p(\nu)$ to their upper bounds is optimal. This leads to:
    \begin{align*}
    z^*\leq \max_{x,p} \phantom{i}&\alpha p(1)+ (1- \alpha)\nu^*x(\nu^*) \\
    \text{s.t.} \phantom{i}&p(1) = \min\{x(1), x(1)-x(\nu^*)+\nu^*\}\\
    &x(1),x(\nu^*) \in [0,1].
    \end{align*}
    It is obvious that we should have $x(1)=1$ and $x(\nu^*)\geq \nu^*$ at optimality. First, $x(1)$ does not have any upper bounds other than $1$, and $p(1)$ is non-decreasing in $x(1)$. Second, it is not optimal to have $x(\nu^*)<\nu^*$ as one can increase $x(\nu^*)$ by some small enough $\varepsilon>0$ and $p(\nu^*)$ by $\nu^*\varepsilon$ to strictly increase the expected revenue without violating any constraints. Hence, we have $p(1)=1-x(\nu^*)+\nu^*$ at optimality, and the problem reduces to:
    \begin{align*}
    \max_{x^+} \;& \alpha (1-x^+(\nu^*))+ (1- \alpha)\nu^*x^+(\nu^*) + (1- \alpha){\nu^*}^2 \\
    \text{s.t.} \;& x^+(\nu^*) \in [0,1-\nu^*],
    \end{align*}
    where $x^+(\nu^*) = x(\nu^*)-\nu^*$. Here, the role of type $\nu^*$ becomes apparent. The principal should find an allocation probability for type $\nu^*$ that optimally trades off the gains from type $\nu^*$ and type $1$. It turns out that the objective has the same value for any feasible $x^+(\nu^*)$ because the objective coefficient of $x^+(\nu^*)$ is equal to zero:
    $$
    (1- \alpha)\nu^*-\alpha = (1-\frac{\underline \mu}{2})\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu} -\frac{\underline \mu}{2} = 0. 
    $$
    Hence, under this two-point distribution, the principal can gain at most:
    $$
    \alpha + (1- \alpha){\nu^*}^2 = \frac{\underline \mu}{2} + (1-\frac{\underline \mu}{2})\big(\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}\big)^2 = \frac{\underline \mu}{2} \big( 1+ \frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu} \big) =  \frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}.
    $$

    Next, we derive our second upper bound by considering the three-point distribution that draws type $1$ with probability $\underline \mu /3$, type $\overline{\nu}=\sqrt{\delta}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)$ with probability $ \underline \mu /3 \overline{\nu}$, and type $\underline{\nu}=\delta(\sqrt{\delta}+1)$ with probability $\underline \mu /3 \underline{\nu}$ where $\delta = \frac{\underline \mu}{3- \underline \mu}$. This distribution belongs to our ambiguity set if the following conditions hold:
    \begin{align*}
        (i) &\; \frac{\underline \mu}{3},\; \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \overline{\nu}},\; \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \underline{\nu}} \in [0,1],\\
        (ii) &\; \underline \nu,\; \overline{\nu} \in [0,1],\\
        (iii) &\; \frac{\underline \mu}{3} +\frac{\underline \mu}{3 \overline{\nu}}  + \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \underline{\nu}} = 1, \; \text{ and } \; \frac{\underline \mu}{3}(1) + \frac{\underline \mu}{3\overline{\nu}}  (\overline{\nu})+  \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \underline{\nu}}(\underline \nu) = \underline \mu.
    \end{align*}
    It is easy to see that the types and the probabilities are non-negative as $\delta \geq 0$. These probabilities are also less than $1$ as they sum up to $1$:
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{\underline \mu}{3} + \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \overline{\nu}}  + \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \underline{\nu}} &= \frac{\underline \mu}{3}\bigg( 1 + \frac{1}{\sqrt{\delta}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)} + \frac{1}{{\delta}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)}\bigg)  = \frac{\underline \mu}{3}\bigg( 1 + \frac{\sqrt{\delta}+1}{{\delta}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)} \bigg) = \frac{\underline \mu}{3}\bigg( 1 + \frac{3- \underline
        \mu}{{\underline \mu}}\bigg) = 1.
    \end{align*}
    As the expectation of this distribution equals $\underline \mu$ by construction, the only remaining condition to check is $\underline \nu, \overline{\nu} \leq 1$. This is easy to see for type $\underline \nu = {\delta}(\sqrt{\delta}+1) = \underline \mu (\sqrt{\delta}+1)/ (3-\underline \mu) \leq \underline \mu (2) / (3-\underline \mu) < \underline \mu$ for any $\underline \mu \in (0,1)$. For type $\overline{\nu}$, the following inequality needs to hold:
    \begin{align*}
        \sqrt{\delta}(\sqrt{\delta}+1) &\leq 1,\\
    \sqrt{\delta} &\leq 1-\delta,\\
    \sqrt{\frac{\underline \mu}{3-\underline \mu}} &\leq \frac{3-2\underline \mu}{3-\underline \mu},\\ 
    \underline \mu(3-\underline \mu) &\leq (3-2\underline \mu)^2,\\
    0 &\leq 5 \underline \mu^2 -15 \underline \mu + 9.
    \end{align*}
    This condition holds only if $\underline \mu \leq (15-3\sqrt{5})/10$. Hence, for any $\underline \mu \in (0, (15-3\sqrt{5})/10]$, the conditions $(i)$, $(ii)$ and $(iii)$ are satisfied, and we have an admissible distribution.

    Now, we solve the principal's problem under this three-point distribution. Similar to the previous part, all types $\nu \in [0,1) \setminus \{\underline \nu, \overline{\nu}\}$ are irrelevant, and one can disregard them in a feasible manner. The principal needs to solve the following model:
    \begin{align*}
    \max_{x,p} \;& \frac{\underline \mu}{3} p(1)+ \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \overline \nu}p(\overline \nu)+ \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \underline \nu}p(\underline \nu)  \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \;&x(1)- p(1) \geq x({\nu}) - {\nu} &\forall \nu \in \{\overline \nu, \underline \nu\},\\
    &\overline \nu x(\overline \nu)- p(\overline \nu) \geq x(\underline{\nu}) - \underline{\nu},\\
    &\nu x(\nu)- p(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in \{1, \overline{\nu}, \underline \nu\},\\
    &x(\nu) \leq 1 &\forall \nu \in \{1, \overline{\nu}, \underline \nu\},\\
    &x(\nu) \geq 0 &\forall \nu \in \{1, \overline{\nu}, \underline \nu\}.
    \end{align*}
    In the optimal solution, all payment rules should equal their lowest upper bound. That is, $p(1)=\min\{x(1), x(1)-x(\overline{\nu}) + \overline{\nu}, x(1)-x(\underline{\nu}) + \underline{\nu}\}$, $p(\overline{\nu}) = \min \{\overline \nu x(\overline \nu), \overline \nu (x(\overline \nu)- x(\underline{\nu})) + \underline{\nu}\}$, and $p(\underline \nu) =x(\underline \nu)$.
    Repeating the same argument from the previous part, in the optimal solution, we should have $x(1)=1$, $x(\overline{\nu}) \geq \overline{\nu}$, and $x(\underline \nu) \geq \underline \nu$. This reduces the principal problem to the following model:
    \begin{align*}
    \max_{x,p} \;& \frac{\underline \mu}{3} \min\{1-x(\overline{\nu}) + \overline{\nu}, 1-x(\underline{\nu}) + \underline{\nu}\} + \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \overline \nu}\min \{\overline \nu x(\overline \nu), \overline \nu (x(\overline \nu)- x(\underline{\nu})) + \underline{\nu}\} + \frac{\underline \mu}{3}x(\underline \nu)  \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \;&x(\nu) \in [\nu,1] \hspace{3.4cm}\forall \nu \in \{\overline{\nu}, \underline \nu\}.
    \end{align*}
    Here, we first argue that there is an optimal solution where $x(\underline \nu) +(\overline{\nu}-\underline \nu) \leq 1$. To show this, we consider a solution with $x(\underline \nu) > 1-(\overline{\nu}-\underline \nu)$. In this case, only one of the \eqref{IC} constraints of type $1$ binds, while the other is strict:
    $$
    p(1)=1-x(\underline \nu) + \underline \nu < 1-\big(1-(\overline{\nu}-\underline \nu)\big) + \underline \nu = \overline{\nu} \leq 1-x(\overline{\nu}) +\overline{\nu}.
    $$
    Hence, one can decrease $x(\underline{\nu})$ by some small $\varepsilon >0$ and increase $p(1)$ by $\varepsilon$ without violating any constraints. This change of variables is feasible for any $\varepsilon$ less than $x(\underline \nu) - \big(1-(\overline{\nu}-\underline \nu)\big)$ and does not change the value of the objective. Next, we show that one has $1-x(\overline{\nu})+\overline{\nu} \leq 1-x(\underline \nu) + \underline \nu$ at optimality. Assume to the contrary that $x(\underline \nu) +\overline{\nu} - \underline \nu> x(\overline{\nu})$. Then, as $x(\underline \nu) +(\overline{\nu}-\underline \nu) \leq 1$, one can increase $x(\overline{\nu})$ by some small $\varepsilon>0$ and $p(\overline{\nu})$ by $\overline{\nu}\varepsilon$ to strictly increase the expected revenue without violating any constraints. Hence, we have $p(1)=1-x(\overline{\nu})+\overline{\nu}$ and $x(\overline{\nu}) \geq \overline{\nu} +x(\underline \nu) - \underline \nu$ at optimality. The problem becomes:
    \begin{align*}
    \max_{x,p} \;& \frac{\underline \mu}{3} (1-x^+(\overline{\nu})-x^+(\underline{\nu})) + \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \overline \nu}\min \{\overline \nu (x^+(\overline \nu)+x^+(\underline{\nu})+\overline{\nu}) \overline \nu (x^+(\overline \nu)+\overline{\nu}) +\underline \nu(1-\overline{\nu})\} + \frac{\underline \mu}{3}x^+(\underline \nu)+ \frac{\underline \mu}{3}\underline \nu  \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \;&x^+(\overline \nu) \in [0,1-\overline{\nu}-x^+(\underline \nu)],\\
    &x^+(\underline \nu) \in [0,1-\underline{\nu}],
    \end{align*}
    where $x^+(\underline \nu) = x(\underline \nu)-\underline \nu$, and $x^+(\overline{\nu}) = x(\overline{\nu})-\big( \overline{\nu} +x^+(\underline \nu) \big)$.

    To solve this problem, first consider any solution with $x^+(\underline \nu) > \underline \nu(1-\overline{\nu})/\overline{\nu}$. In this case, the variable $p(\overline{\nu})$ does not depend on $x^+(\underline \nu)$, so the objective coefficient of $x^+(\underline \nu)$ becomes zero. As $\underline \nu(1-\overline{\nu})/\overline{\nu} \geq 0$, we can decrease $x^+(\underline \nu)$ up to this value without changing the value of the objective. Hence, there exists an optimal solution with $x^+(\underline \nu) \leq \underline \nu(1-\overline{\nu})/\overline{\nu}$, which should also satisfy $p(\overline{\nu})=\overline \nu (x^+(\overline \nu)+x^+(\underline{\nu})+\overline{\nu})$. Then, we need to maximize the following objective over two variables:
    \begin{align*}
    \max_{x} \;& \frac{\underline \mu}{3} (1-x^+(\overline{\nu})-x^+(\underline{\nu})) + \frac{\underline \mu}{3 \overline \nu}(\overline \nu (x^+(\overline \nu)+x^+(\underline{\nu})+\overline{\nu})) + \frac{\underline \mu}{3}x^+(\underline \nu)+ \frac{\underline \mu}{3}\underline \nu  \nonumber\\
    \text{s.t.} \;&x^+(\overline \nu) \in [0,1-\overline{\nu}-x^+(\underline \nu)],\\
    &x^+(\underline \nu) \in [0,\frac{\underline \nu}{\overline{\nu}}(1-\overline{\nu})],
    \end{align*}
    where the objective coefficients of $x^+(\overline{\nu})$ and $x^+(\underline{\nu})$ are $0$ and $\underline \mu /3$. Hence, for optimality, we should set $x^+(\underline \nu)$ equal to its upper-bound $\underline \nu(1-\overline{\nu})/ \overline{\nu}$, whereas $x^+(\overline \nu)$ can take any value in $[0,1-\overline{\nu}-x^*(\underline \nu)]$. Hence, under this three-point distribution, the principal can gain at most:
    \begin{align*}
    \frac{\underline \mu}{3}(1+\overline{\nu}) + \frac{\underline \mu}{3}\frac{\underline \nu}{\overline{\nu}}(1-\overline{\nu})+ \frac{\underline \mu}{3}\underline \nu 
    &= \frac{\underline \mu}{3} \big[ 1+\overline{\nu}+ \frac{\delta(\sqrt{\delta}+1)}{\sqrt{\delta}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)}(1-\overline{\nu})+ \underline \nu \big]
     = \frac{\underline \mu}{3} \big[ 1 +\sqrt{\delta} +\overline{\nu}(1- \sqrt{\delta}) +\underline \nu\big] \\
     &= \frac{\underline \mu}{3} \big[ 1 +\sqrt{\delta} +\sqrt{\delta}(1- {\delta}) +\delta(\sqrt{\delta}+1)\big]
    = \frac{\underline \mu}{3}(\sqrt{\delta}+1)^2.
    \end{align*}
    This completes the proof.
    \hfill \qed	 \\


\noindent{\bf Proof of Proposition \ref{prop: twoPointOptimalMechanism}}.
    To prove that $(x^*,p^*)$ is robustly optimal, we will show that it is feasible and generates an expected payoff of $\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$, attaining the upper bound given in Lemma \ref{lem:upperBounds}. That is, $(x^*,p^*)$ is feasible in the formulation given in Lemma \ref{lem: linearPayment}, and it satisfies $p^*(\underline \mu) = \alpha \underline \mu +\beta =\underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu)$. We first show the latter:
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha \underline \mu +\beta &= \frac{2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2} \underline \mu -(\sqrt{2\frac{2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2}}-1)^2=\frac{2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2} \underline \mu - (\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu})^2 =\frac{\underline \mu}{(2-\underline \mu)^2}(2-\underline \mu) =\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu}.
    \end{align*}

    Next we show that $(x^*,p^*)$ is a feasible mechanism, $i.e.$, it satisfies \eqref{IC}, \eqref{IR} constraints, and the allocation variables are always between $0$ and $1$. To this end, we begin with some properties of $x^*$, $e.g.$ it is weakly increasing and continuous for $\nu \in [0,1]$. We first show that $0 \leq \underline \nu \leq \nu^\circ \leq \overline{\nu} \leq 1$ for any $\underline \mu \in (0,1)$. Firstly, the non-negativity of $\underline \nu$ follows from the definitions of $\alpha$ and $\beta$. The second inequality follows because of the following facts:
    \begin{align*}
        \underline \nu &= -\frac{2\beta}{\alpha} = \frac{2(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)^2}{\alpha} = \frac{2(\frac{2}{2-\underline \mu}-1)^2}{2/(2-\underline \mu)^2} = \frac{(\frac{\underline \mu}{2- \underline \mu})^2}{1/(2-\underline \mu)^2}= \underline \mu^2,\\
        \nu^\circ &= \sqrt{-\beta} = \sqrt{2\alpha}-1 = \frac{2}{2-\underline \mu} -1 = \frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu} \geq \underline \mu \geq \underline \mu^2 = \underline \nu.
    \end{align*}
    The last two inequalities are proven below.
    \begin{align*}
        \overline{\nu} &= 1-\alpha +2\sqrt{-\beta} = 1 -\alpha+2(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1) = 1 - \frac{2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2}+\frac{2\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu} \\
        &=\big[(2-\underline \mu)^2 -2 +2\underline \mu(2-\underline \mu) \big]/(2-\underline \mu)^2 = \frac{2-\underline \mu^2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2} = \nu^\circ \frac{2-\underline \mu^2}{\underline \mu(2-\underline \mu)} \geq \nu^\circ,\\
        1-\overline{\nu} &= 1- \frac{2-\underline \mu^2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2} = \frac{4-4\underline \mu +\underline \mu^2 -(2-\underline \mu^2)}{(2-\underline \mu)^2} = \frac{2-4\underline \mu +2\underline \mu^2}{(2-\underline 
        \mu)^2} =\frac{2(1-\underline \mu)^2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2} \geq 0.
    \end{align*}
    For continuity, we will show that $x^*$ takes the same values at the corresponding end points of the intervals:
    \begin{align*}
        -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\underline \nu &= -\frac{\beta}{-2\beta/ \alpha} = \frac{\alpha}{2} = \alpha +\frac{\beta}{-2\beta/\alpha} = \alpha + \frac{\beta}{\underline \nu},\\
        \alpha + \frac{\beta}{\nu^\circ} &= \alpha + \frac{\beta}{\sqrt{-\beta}}= \alpha -\sqrt{-\beta} = \nu^\circ + \alpha -2\sqrt{-\beta}.
    \end{align*}
    Also, $\overline{\nu} +\alpha -2\sqrt{-\beta} =1$ follows from the definition of $\overline{\nu}$.
    To prove that $x^*$ is weakly increasing, it is enough to show that its first derivative is non-negative in each interval. The first derivatives are $-\beta/\underline \nu^2$ for interval $[0, \underline \nu]$, $-\beta/ \nu^2$ for $[\underline \nu, \nu^\circ]$, $1$ for $[\nu^\circ, \overline{\nu}]$, and $0$ for $[\overline{\nu},1]$, which are all non-negative as $\beta \leq 0$. Hence, as $x^*$ is weakly increasing and continuous for $\nu \in [0,1]$, and $x^*(0)=0$, and $x^*(1)=1$ by definition, we must have $x^*(\nu) \in [0,1]$ for all $\nu \in [0,1]$.

    To prove that $(x^*,p^*)$ is individually rational, $i.e.$, it satisfies \eqref{IR}, we need to show $\nu x^*(\nu) \geq \alpha \nu +\beta$. 
    Equivalently, $x^*(\nu) \geq \alpha  +\beta/\nu$, which holds with equality for $\nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ]$. Notice that $\alpha  +\beta/\nu$ is strictly concave as $\beta <0$. Then, the following inequalities are satisfied for any $\nu$:
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu} + \frac{-\beta}{ \nu^2}(\nu'- \nu) &\geq \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu'} &\forall \nu' \in[0,1].
    \end{align*}
   {Now we show that the left-hand side of the above inequality is equal to $x^*(\nu')$ for all $\nu' \in [0, \underline \nu] \cup [\nu^\circ, \overline{\nu}]$. First consider any $\nu' \in[0,\underline \nu]$ and recall that $x^*(\nu')=-\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu'$ for all $\nu' \in [0, \underline \nu]$. In this case, \eqref{IR} follows from:}
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu} + \frac{-\beta}{ \underline \nu^2}(\nu'- \underline \nu) &= -\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu' +\alpha + 2\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu} =-\frac{\beta}{\underline \nu^2}\nu' \geq \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu'}.
    \end{align*}
    {Then, consider any $\nu' \in [\nu^\circ, \overline{\nu}]$ and recall that $x^*(\nu')=\nu' + \alpha + -2\sqrt{-\beta}$. \eqref{IR} follows from:}
    \begin{align*}
        \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu^\circ} + \frac{-\beta}{{\nu^\circ}^2}(\nu'- \nu^\circ) &= \nu' + \alpha + 2\frac{\beta}{\nu^\circ} =\nu' + \alpha + -2\sqrt{-\beta} \geq \alpha  +\frac{\beta}{\nu'}.
    \end{align*}
    {Finally, for any $\nu \in [\overline{\nu},1]$, \eqref{IR} can be written as $\nu x^*(\nu) -(\alpha \nu +\beta) =\nu(1-\alpha)+(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)^2$, which happens to be non-negative irrespective of the value of $\alpha$. If $(1-\alpha) \geq 0$, this is immediate. If $(1-\alpha) < 0$, on the other hand, we have the following as $\nu \in [0,1]$:}
    \begin{align*}
        \nu(1-\alpha)+(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)^2 &\geq 1-\alpha+(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)^2 \geq (\sqrt{\alpha}-\sqrt{2})^2 \geq 0.        
    \end{align*}

    To prove that $(x^*,p^*)$ is incentive compatible, $i.e.$, satisfies \eqref{IC}, we need to show that $\nu(x^*(\nu)-x^*(\hat \nu)) +\hat \nu \geq \alpha \nu + \beta$ for any $\hat \nu \in [0,\nu]$ and $\nu \in[0,1]$. Equivalently, for all $\nu \in[0,1]$, we need to show:
    \begin{align*}
    \nu(x^*(\nu)-\alpha) -\beta + \min_{\hat \nu \in [0,\nu]} -\nu x^*(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu \geq 0.    
    \end{align*}
    Notice that, for any fixed $\nu$, the function $-\nu x^*(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu$ is convex as $x^*$ is concave, $i.e.$, $\text{d}x^*(\nu)/\text{d}{\nu}$ is decreasing over $\nu$ by definition. Let us inspect the derivative of $-\nu x^*(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu$ with respect to $\hat \nu$.
    If this derivative is non-negative for all $\hat \nu \in [0, \nu]$, then the minimum is attained at $\hat \nu=0$, meaning that \eqref{IC} reduces to \eqref{IR}, which is already proven above. As $\text{d}x^*(\hat \nu)/\text{d}{\hat \nu}$ is decreasing over $\hat \nu$ by definition, the derivative would be non-negative for all $\hat \nu \in [0, \nu]$, if it is non-negative at $\hat \nu =0$:
    \begin{align*}
    - \nu \frac{\text{d}x^*(\hat \nu)}{\text{d}\hat{\nu}}\Big\rvert_{\hat \nu =0} +1 = -\nu\frac{-\beta}{\underline \nu^2} +1 &\geq 0, \quad \Rightarrow \quad
    -\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta} \geq \nu.
    \end{align*}
    Hence, \eqref{IC} is satisfied for all $\nu \leq -\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta}$. For $\nu > -\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta}$, the minimum will be attained at some $\hat \nu \in [0, \nu]$, so that proving non-negativity at this minimum is enough to show that \eqref{IC} holds. {Since $x^*$ is concave, the minimum will be attained when the derivative is equal to zero:}
    \begin{align*}
    - \nu \frac{\text{d}x^*(\hat \nu)}{\text{d}\hat{\nu}}\Big\rvert_{\hat \nu \in [\underline \nu, \nu^\circ]} +1 = -\nu\frac{-\beta}{\hat \nu^2} +1 &= 0, \quad
    \Rightarrow \quad \hat \nu = \sqrt{-\beta \nu}.
    \end{align*}
    Hence, the minimum value of $-\nu x^*(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu$ is given by $-\nu (\alpha+\beta/\sqrt{-\beta \nu}) + \sqrt{-\beta \nu} = -\nu \alpha +2\sqrt{-\beta \nu}$. Then, we need to show that the following holds for all $\nu \in (-\underline \nu^2/\beta,1]$:
    \begin{align}
    \nu(x^*(\nu)-\alpha) -\beta + \min_{\hat \nu \in [0,\nu]} -\nu x^*(\hat \nu) + \hat \nu &= \nu(x^*(\nu)-\alpha) -\beta -\nu\alpha+2\sqrt{-\beta \nu} \nonumber \\
    &=\nu(x^*(\nu)- 2\alpha) -\beta +2\sqrt{-\beta \nu}\geq 0. 
    \tag{\ref{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll}}
    \end{align}
    We first show \eqref{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll} for $\nu \in [\overline{\nu},1]$, for which $x^*(\nu)=1$. This function is concave over $\nu$ so that it is enough to verify non-negativity at the endpoints. When $\nu=0$, this function is non-negative as $\beta \leq 0$. When $\nu=1$,
    \begin{align*}
   (1-2\alpha)-\beta+2\sqrt{-\beta} &= (1-2\alpha) +(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)^2 +2(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)\\
   &=(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)\big(-(1+\sqrt{2\alpha})+\sqrt{2\alpha}-1+2\big) = 0.
    \end{align*}
    Now, we will prove \eqref{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll} for $\nu \in [-\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta}, \overline{\nu}]$ and conclude the proof. First, we will show that $-\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta} \geq \nu^\circ$ for all $\mu \in [0.315,1]$ so that we can focus on the allocation function for the interval $[\nu^\circ,\overline{\nu}]$. This follows if the following is non-negative:
    \begin{align*}
   -\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta} - \nu^\circ &= -\frac{4\beta}{\alpha^2} -\sqrt{-\beta} = \frac{4(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)^2}{\alpha^2}-(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1) = (\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)\frac{4(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)-\alpha^2}{\alpha^2}.
    \end{align*}
    {As $(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)= \underline \mu/(2-\underline \mu) \geq 0$, we can equivalently} prove the non-negativity of $4(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)-\alpha^2$. Substituting the value of $\alpha$ gives:
    $$
    4(\frac{2}{2-\underline \mu}-1)-\frac{4}{(2-\underline \mu)^4}= \frac{4}{(2-\underline \mu)^4}\big[\underline \mu(2-\underline \mu)^3 -1\big],
    $$
    which is non-negative for any $\underline \mu \in [0.16071,1]$. Hence, we have $[-\frac{\underline \nu^2}{\beta}, \overline{\nu}] \subseteq [\nu^\circ, \overline{\nu}]$ for any $\underline \mu \in [0.315,1)$. Then, we just need to prove \eqref{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll} for $\nu \in [\nu^\circ, \overline{\nu}]$, which satisfies $x^*(\nu)=\nu+\alpha-2\sqrt{-\beta}$. Then, \eqref{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll} becomes:
    \begin{align*}
    \nu(x^*(\nu)- 2\alpha) -\beta +2\sqrt{-\beta \nu} = \nu(\nu-\alpha-2\sqrt{-\beta} -1) +(\sqrt{\nu} +\sqrt{-\beta})^2 \geq 0.
    \end{align*}
    Now we substitute the values of $\alpha$ and $\beta$, and rearrange:
    \begin{align*}
    \nu\big(\nu- \alpha-2\sqrt{2\alpha}+1\big)+ \big(\sqrt{\nu}+(\sqrt{2\alpha}-1)\big)^2 &=\nu\big(\nu- \frac{2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2}-\frac{4}{2-\underline \mu}+1\big)+ (\sqrt{\nu}+\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline\mu})^2\\
    &=\nu\big(\nu +\frac{\underline \mu^2-6}{(2-\underline \mu)^2}\big)+(\sqrt{\nu}+\frac{\underline \mu}{2-\underline \mu})^2\\
    &= \nu\frac{\nu(2-\underline \mu)^2 +\underline \mu^2-6}{(2-\underline \mu)^2}+\frac{(\sqrt{\nu}(2-\underline \mu) +\underline \mu)^2}{(2-\underline \mu)^2}.
    \end{align*}
    This function is non-negative if the following is non-negative:
    $$
    \nu\big(\nu(2-\underline \mu)^2+\underline \mu^2-6+(2-\underline \mu)^2\big)+2\sqrt{\nu}(2-\underline\mu)\underline \mu+\underline \mu^2.
    $$
    For fixed $\nu$, this function is a second degree polynomial over $\underline \mu$ and can be rearranged as follows:
    \begin{align}\label{eq: IC-nonnegativity}
    \underline \mu^2 \big(\nu(2+\nu)+1-2\sqrt{\nu}\big)
    -\underline \mu\big(4\nu(1+\nu)-4\sqrt{\nu}\big)+\nu(4\nu-2).
    \end{align}
    Here, the coefficient of $\underline \mu^2$ is always non-negative as it can be written as $\nu(1+\nu)+(1-\sqrt{\nu})^2$. Hence, \eqref{eq: IC-nonnegativity} is a convex function of $\underline \mu$. 
    %Now we fix any $\nu \in [0,1]$ and minimize this function over $\underline \mu$. 
    Then, for fixed $\nu$, it is easy to find the $\underline \mu$ value that minimizes \eqref{eq: IC-nonnegativity}. We will find this minimum and evaluate it for all $\nu \in [0,1]$. If the minimizer is strictly less than $0.315$, $i.e.$, outside of our feasible set for $\underline \mu$, then the minimum value would be attained at $\underline \mu=0.315$. Figure \ref{fig: IC-nonnegativity} illustrates the function of $\underline \mu$ that minimizes $\eqref{eq: IC-nonnegativity}$, the minimum value of \eqref{eq: IC-nonnegativity}, and the value of \eqref{eq: IC-nonnegativity} evaluated at $\underline \mu = 0.315$. We see that \eqref{eq: IC-nonnegativity} is always non-negative for $\underline \mu=0.315$. Moreover, its minimum is non-negative if the minimizer $\underline \mu$ is higher than $0.315$. Hence, \eqref{eq: ICnonnegativityForAll} is also satisfied for $\nu \in [\nu^\circ, \overline{\nu}]$, which means that $(x^*,p^*)$ satisfies \eqref{IC} constraints.
\hfill \qed  \\

