\par According to the data presented in Figure \ref{fig:longterm}, several spikes have emerged during the long-term monitoring, especially in Figure \ref{fig:longstabilitymoniternnvtnnvt} (Two NNVT PMTs, NNVT+NNVT) and Figure \ref{fig:longstabilitymoniternnvthpk} (One NNVT and one HPK PMTs, NNVT+HPK). These facilitate comparative analyses under various configurations. Notable spikes were observed in both 3-inch and 20-inch PMTs during the long-term tests. The anomalies reflected in the plots could be attributed to either baseline influences or malfunctions originating from the PMTs themselves, or potentially caused by flashers.

The count rate is a crucial parameter for distinguishing the cause of sudden jumps. If the jumps originate from the PMTs or electronic sources, only the count rates for PMT random coincidences would significantly increase, without impacting other PMTs. Conversely, under sufficiently high emission intensity, all PMTs respond simultaneously, leading to concurrent increases in count rates across the board. The graph employs various colors to delineate distinct accidental coincidence conditions.

Importantly, a sudden surge in the PMT count rate can occur due to jumps from flashes, exemplified by the representation of four PMTs coinciding in green. In both configurations, instances of all four PMTs simultaneously experiencing dark count jumps within a monitoring duration of less than 300 hours are rare. An analysis of the monitoring results yields a random coincidence rate of 0.4$\times$10$^{-5}$\,Hz for NNVT/NNVT and 3.6 $\times$10$^{-5}$\,Hz for NNVT/HPK, whereas the measured coincidence rates exceed 0.5\,Hz. This disparity primarily arises from the characteristics of the 20-inch PMT itself.


