\par Using a camera as a tool for the detection of weak light sources is a way to investigate DCR spikes. When the camera noise is sufficiently low to detect single photons \cite{Wang_2023-track,Wang_2023}, it becomes a viable tool for discerning weak light, making it suitable for detecting subtle variations caused by flashes. To further investigate the underlying causes of DCR jumps, two additional tests, a voltage addition test and a capacitor addition test, were designed in conjunction with the camera, as shown in Figure ~\ref{fig:setup}. In these experiment, the camera was oriented towards a 20-inch PMT. In the voltage addition test, we systematically increased the voltage in increments of 100\,V from a baseline of 500\,V and monitored the PMT's coincidence rates using a counter. Conversely, in the capacitor addition test, as illustrated in the diagram (Figure \ref{fig:systemsetup2}), we introduced a capacitor and applied voltage. The breakdown of the capacitor generated flashes (artificial sparks), enabling the observation of the PMT's response through this method. Concurrently, in both setups, we employed a Hamamatsu camera to capture features inside the dark box under varying exposure times.

