The numerical radius of $T\in {\mathcal{B}}({%
\mathcal{H}})$ is defined as
%\begin{equation*}
$\omega \left( T\right) :=\sup \left\{ | \left\langle Tx,x\right\rangle |:x\in {%
\mathcal{H}}\text{, }\left\Vert x\right\Vert =1\right\} \text{.}
$%\end{equation*}
There have been many generalizations of the numerical radius. One of these
generalizations is the $A$-numerical radius of an operator $T\in {\mathcal{B}%
}_{A^{1/2}}({\mathcal{H}})$, which is defined as
\begin{equation*}
\omega _{A}\left( T\right) :=\sup \left\{ \left\vert \left\langle
Tx,x\right\rangle _{A}\right\vert :x\in {\mathcal{H}}\text{, }\left\Vert
x\right\Vert _{A}=1\right\} \text{.}
\end{equation*}
Obviously, if $A=I$, then $\omega _{A}\left( T\right) =\omega \left( T\right) $.
One basic fact about the $A$-numerical radius is
%\begin{equation*}
$\omega _{A}\left( T\right) =\omega _{A}\left( T^{\sharp _{A}}\right) \text{
for every }T\in {\mathcal{B}}_{A}({\mathcal{H}})\text{.}
$  
%\end{equation*}
Further, $A$-numerical radius is a seminorm on ${\mathcal{B}}_{A^{1/2}}({\mathcal{
H}})$ and for every $T\in {\mathcal{B}}_{A^{{1}/{2}}}({\mathcal{H}}%
) $, we have
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{2}\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{A}\leq \omega _{A}\left( T\right) \leq
\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{A}\text{.}  \label{00}
\end{equation}
If $T\in {\mathcal{B}}_{A}({\mathcal{H}})$ is $A$-self-adjoint, then 
%\begin{equation}
$\omega _{A}\left( T\right) =\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{A}\text{}$. And if $AT^2=0$, then $\frac{1}{2}\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{A}= \omega _{A}\left( T\right).$
%\end{equation}%
For proofs and more facts about the $A$-numerical radius of operators, we refer to \cite{Bhunia-IJPA, Bhunia-book, BH, BH2, GU2, GU3, GU4, Z}.
A refinement of the second inequality in (\ref{00}) was established by
Zamani \cite{Z}, namely, if $T\in {\mathcal{B}}_{A}({
\mathcal{H}})$, then 
\begin{equation}
\omega _{A}^{2}(T)\leq \frac{1}{2}\left\Vert T^{\sharp _{A}}T+TT^{\sharp
_{A}}\right\Vert _{A}\text{.}  \label{2}
\end{equation}
In \cite{Z}, he also proved that 
\begin{equation}
\omega _{A}^{2}(T)\leq \frac{1}{4}\left\Vert T^{\sharp _{A}}T+TT^{\sharp
_{A}}\right\Vert _{A}+\frac{1}{2}\omega _{A}\left( T^{2}\right) \text{.}  \label{4}
\end{equation}
After that Guesba \cite{GU2} generalized (\ref{2}) as
\begin{equation}
\omega _{A}^{2n}(T)\leq \frac{1}{2} \left\Vert \left( T^{\sharp _{A}}T\right)
^{n}+\left( TT^{\sharp _{A}}\right) ^{n} \right\Vert _{A} \quad \text{ for all }n\in 
