By \cref{as:C.no.randomized} we have $\mathbb{E}[C(X^i)]\geq \mathbb{E}[C(\tilde{\mathbb{E}}[X^i])]$. Thus we may replace $C(X^i)$ by $C(\tilde{\mathbb{E}}[X^i])$ in the above inequality, and then the right hand side is precisely $J(\tilde{\mathbb{E}}[X^i];\tilde{\mathbb{E}}[\boldsymbol{X}^{-i}])$.
