% Secondly, the dynamic nature of financial markets complicates the task of making accurate predictions over time \citep{fama1965investigations, park2011herding, salahuddin2020timevarying}. Financial markets are influenced by many factors, including macroeconomic indicators, geopolitical events, and investor sentiment, which can change rapidly and unpredictably. Traditional statistical/machine-learning models often struggle to capture these rapid changes, especially in the long run, leading to outdated predictions that can adversely affect portfolio performance. Finally, forecasting errors in the predictive step can be amplified during the optimization step, particularly in high-dimensional portfolio optimization settings where the number of assets is large \citep{MI89, ALZ19}.

