\subsubsection{Advantage of Dynamic Embedding}
In this section, we briefly discuss the benefit of dynamic embedding, and provide some insights on the relations between embedded states and factor models.
\subsubsection*{Benefits of Embedding for RL}
Embedding benefits reinforcement learning from the following perspective:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Information summarizing.} Embedding helps to summarize the important features of raw states into a lower-dimensional space, which is more compact and informative than the original high-dimensional space. This lower-dimensional representation of the state captures the salient features of the environment that are relevant to the RL agent's decision-making process. By focusing on these important features, the agent can more effectively learn a good policy. Furthermore, the lower dimensionality of the embedding makes it easier for the agent to process and learn from the state, leading to faster and more efficient learning.
    \item \textbf{Denoising.} State noises can negatively affect the learning of the optimal policy by the RL agent, leading to suboptimal decision-making \citep{lillicrap2015continuous}. Embedding the states into a lower-dimensional space can help to denoise the states, making it easier for the agent to identify the important and relevant information in the state space. By reducing the effects of noise, the agent can learn more accurate and stable policies with smaller variance and sample complexity. This is particularly beneficial in environments where the states are high-dimensional and noisy, as it can improve the efficiency and effectiveness of the RL algorithm.
    \item \textbf{States clustering.} non-stationary market environments can exhibit different states, but under similar underlying patterns, which can make it challenging for the RL agent to learn an effective policy. Embedding can help to cluster states with similar hidden patterns together by projecting them into lower-dimensional embedded states that are close to each other. This reduces the effective state space that the RL agent needs to explore, making it easier to learn and evaluate policies and therefore improve the sample efficiency. In practice, this can lead to significant improvements in performance and sample efficiency, particularly in complex environments with continuous state space \citep{whitney2019dynamics, pflueger2020plan}.
    \item \textbf{Stable learning process.} In non-stationary markets, the transition $\mathbb{P}(\boldsymbol{s}'|\boldsymbol{s},\boldsymbol{a})$ may change over time. This suggests that under different underlying market patterns, similar states could lead to different optimal actions. Consequently, training RL algorithms with non-stationary transitions may fail to converge and reach optimal solutions. Projecting $\boldsymbol{s}$ into a lower-dimensional representation $\boldsymbol{z_s}$ can help mitigate this issue if the obtained embedded $\boldsymbol{z_s}$ captures the underlying market patterns.
\end{itemize}
\end{comment}
