\textit{Information Perspective of Dynamic Embedding.}
Incorporating a dynamic strategy that absorbs new information as the market evolves leads to improved decision-making in portfolio management \citep{gao2019role}. Traditional methods require intensive computation and infrequent retraining, which can result in poor performance when the market shifts. Dynamic embedding allows for fast information updates, significantly reducing computational intensity and capturing market changes effectively. The RL framework learns from past experiences, and with persistent updates to the embedding, new knowledge is continuously incorporated into the strategy, optimizing performance. The resulting latent states can act as market indicators, capturing underlying trends \citep{colby2003indicator}. Additionally, dynamic embedding adapts to new market conditions without human interference, avoiding the obsolescence of features in static models \citep{factorzoo}. By consistently incorporating new data, the embeddings capture changing market dynamics, enhancing portfolio performance.

\textit{Relations with Factor Model.}
The state embedding in our framework shares certain features with traditional factor models \citep{FAMA19933,Fama2004CAMP}. Both factor models and our framework aim to interpret the market using a small number of features. However, factor models focus on finding variables that can capture return or risk differences across different assets, while the embeddings in our framework emphasize capturing variables that contain market transition information. Additionally, traditional factor models usually start with predetermined criteria, such as Return on Equity ratio (ROE) and Gross Profit-to-Asset, whereas our embeddings are constructed based on historical data using data-driven approaches, similar to the approaches of \cite{chen2023deep} and \cite{cong2020alphaportfolio}. This approach offers more flexibility in the structure and size of embeddings.
