In general, to train the generative autoencoder, we minimize the loss defined in \eqref{eq: generative loss}. For VAE and WAE, when encoding state, we sample the embedded variable $\boldsymbol{z_{s}}\sim \Gamma_\phi(\boldsymbol{s})$, and when reconstructing in WAE, we use a deterministic decoder, which means $G_\theta = \delta(\cdot\mid\boldsymbol{z_{s}}, \boldsymbol{a})$ and can be simplified as $G_\theta(\boldsymbol{z_{s}}, \boldsymbol{a})$. Then, \eqref{eq: generative loss} can be approximated through the empirical losses for the two algorithms:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:VAE}
\mathcal{L}_{\beta-\text{VAE}}\left(\phi, \theta\right)=\underset{\left(\boldsymbol{s}, \boldsymbol{a}, \boldsymbol{s^{\prime}}\right)\in\mathcal{D}_\text{buffer} }{\sum}\left[-\log G_\theta\left(\boldsymbol{s^{\prime}} \mid \boldsymbol{z_{s}}, \boldsymbol{a}\right) 
+\beta D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(\Gamma_{\phi}\left(\boldsymbol{s}\right) \| \mathcal{N}(0, \boldsymbol{I})\right)\right], 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:WAE-MMD}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{WAE-MMD}}\left(\phi, \theta\right)=\underset{\left(\boldsymbol{s}, \boldsymbol{a}, \boldsymbol{s^{\prime}}\right)\in\mathcal{D}_\text{buffer} }{\sum}\left[ \mathcal{C} (\boldsymbol{s}^\prime, G_\theta(\boldsymbol{z_{s}}, \boldsymbol{a}))
+\lambda D_{\mathrm{MMD}}\left(\Gamma_{\phi}\left(\boldsymbol{s}\right) \| \mathcal{N}(0, \boldsymbol{I})\right)\right],
\end{equation}
where $\beta$-VAE is a slight modification of the traditional VAE, and WAE-MMD is a variation of WAE that uses the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) loss. The loss function in both models consists of two components: reconstruction loss and discrepancy loss. The reconstruction loss measures the difference between the original input and the reconstructed output, while the discrepancy loss measures the difference between the learned latent space and a prior distribution. $\beta$-VAE has an advantage over VAE as it allows users to control the degree of disentanglement in the latent space via the $\beta$ hyperparameter.

In (\ref{eq:VAE}), $D_{KL}$ represents the Kullback–Leibler divergence, which measures the difference between two probability distributions $P_1$ and $P_2$, i.e., 
\begin{equation}
D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_1 \| P_2)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} p_1(x) \log \left(\frac{p_1(x)}{p_2(x)}\right) d x =\mathbb{E}_{p_1(x)}\left[\log \left(\frac{p_1(x)}{p_2(x)}\right)\right].
\end{equation}
In (\ref{eq:WAE-MMD}), $D_\mathrm{MMD}$ is a measure of the maximum mean discrepancy between two distributions. It generally compares the inferred distribution with a high-dimensional standard normal distribution, and it is defined using a positive-definite reproducing kernel $k: \mathcal{Z} \times \mathcal{Z} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$, computed as:

\begin{equation}
D_{\text{MMD}_{k}}\left(P_1, P_2\right)=\left\|\int_{\mathcal{Z}} k(\boldsymbol{z}, \cdot) d P_1(\boldsymbol{z})-\int_{\mathcal{Z}} k(\boldsymbol{z}, \cdot) d P_2(\boldsymbol{z})\right\|_{\mathcal{H}_{k}},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{H}_{k}$ is the reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) of the real-valued function that maps $\mathcal{Z}$ to $\mathbb{R}$. 
%MMD also has an unbiased U-statistic estimator that can be used in conjunction with stochastic gradient descent (SGD) \citep{tolstikhin2018wasserstein}. 
