\begin{comment}
Our approach of using an autoencoder to capture the sufficient information necessary to understand the transition dynamics of the stock market shares similarities with the factor model. The factor model has been widely used in finance for identifying economic factors that can explain the cross-sectional return differences of stocks \citep{fama1988permanent,Fama2021Riskfactor}. The idea is that instead of trying to predict the return of each individual stock, one can capture the common sources of systematic risk that affect the whole market. By doing so, investors can build diversified portfolios that are less susceptible to idiosyncratic risk.

Similarly, our approach focuses on identifying the minimum sufficient information necessary to capture the implicit shifts in the stock market, which can help investors better understand and navigate the market. The autoencoder is trained to encode the original state $\boldsymbol{s}$ into a low-dimensional and noise-free latent state $\boldsymbol{z}$, which can be seen as a set of economic factors that are driving the market. By using this approach, we can potentially identify the underlying factors that are driving market trends, which can help investors build more effective investment strategies. Additionally, the resulting latent states can also be used as a market indicator to capture latent trends in the market \citep{colby2003indicator}.
