\subsubsection*{Performance Evaluation: Cumulative Returns.} \label{sec: accumulative}
We assess the investment performance our framework (DERL-VAE and DERL-WAE) and ablation algorithm: TD3 without embedding (TD3), TD3 with VAE embedding (TD3-VAE and TD3-WAE). We also backtest some widely-used investment benchmarks: S\&P 500 (passive investment), Mean-Var, Momentum, and Equalweight. We repeat all algorithms 10 times and evaluate the average results in Table \ref{tab: SP500 summarize performances}, and track the cumulative returns of these algorithms in Figure \ref{fig: accumulative SP500}.

\ins{Computation limits.}


The results presented in Table \ref{tab: SP500 summarize performances} reveal that both DERL-WAE and DERL-VAE substantially surpass all other strategies across multiple investment performance metrics. For example, DERL-VAE attains the highest total return (246\%) and information ratio (0.59), with an annualized return of 51.46\% and a Sharpe ratio of 2.17. Conversely, DERL-WAE excels in total return (238\%), annualized return (50\%), and achieves the highest Sharpe ratio (2.22), Sortino ratio (3.34), and the lowest maximum drawdown (-11\%). In comparison, pure RL approach (TD3) outperforms non-RL baselines with annualized return of 26\% and a Sharpe ratio of 1.17, but only slightly exceeds the passive benchmark (S\&P 500) with a total return of 92\%. Incorporating VAE and WAE embeddings enhances TD3's performance, elevating annualized returns to 39\% (TD3-VAE and TD3-WAE), and augmenting Sharpe ratios to 1.83 and 2.08 seperatively.

The DERL framework provides superior returns and risk management, as evidenced by high Sharpe and Sortino ratios and low drawdown and volatility relative to the benchmarks. Passive investment yields a max draw-down of 34\%  and a Sharpe ratio of 0.76, while both DERL-VAE and DERL-WAE display a marked improvement in these metrics. Enhanced opportunity detection and risk management are also reflected in the lower maximum drawdowns and higher information ratio of the DERL strategies. Overall, the DERL strategies prove to be more robust and adaptable than traditional benchmark algorithms.

Figure \ref{fig: accumulative SP500} shows that relying solely on a reinforcement learning (RL) algorithm does not yield consistently high profits in the long run, and its risk management performance is subpar compared to passive learning. The TD3 (purple line) demonstrates a decline in performance after the initial six months, whereas our framework offers more sustained profit generation. Both TD3-VAE and TD3-WAE leverage embeddings to achieve improved risk control compared to pure RL does, particularly during the market downturn around March 2020. However, they become progressively less responsive to market trends after a year, resulting in larger losses during recessions and lower profits in booming markets. Our proposed Deep Embedding Reinforcement Learning (DERL) framework combines the advantages of online meta-learning and embeddings, enabling it to continually adapt to market trends even after three years of online operations.


