Portfolio management has traditionally relied on minimizing pricing errors and estimating risk premia from historical data to achieve investment objectives. However, this approach suffers from large estimation errors and cannot effectively incorporate transaction costs, economic constraints, and alternative objectives. In addition, financial and economic data are often high-dimensional, noisy, and non-stationary, making it challenging for traditional econometric tools to capture path dependence and cross-asset linkages \citep{cong2020alphaportfolio}. Recent studies have therefore adopted machine learning techniques to tackle these challenges \citep{lu2021frontiers, lei2020time}. However, most of these models still follow the conventional two-step approach of prediction followed by optimization, which can suffer from error propagation. 

To overcome these limitations, we aim to directly extract information about historical path dependence and cross-asset linkages to maximize a portfolio objective in an end-to-end way. This involves designing a single model that takes raw financial data as input and outputs portfolio decisions that achieve investment objectives while considering transaction costs, high noise-to-signal ratio, and nonstationarity. We leverage recent advances in machine learning techniques such as reinforcement learning, dynamic embeddings, and deep generative models to tackle these challenges, enabling a more powerful framework that can capture the complexities of financial markets.
\end{comment}

