\subsubsection*{Optimization Framework} \label{sec: optimization approach}
With the objective in Equation (\ref{equation: transportation loss}), we use parameterization and optimization techniques to approximate the optimal distributions.

\textbf{Parameterization} From a computational perspective, we aim to find the parameters $\theta$ and $\phi$ that minimize:
\begin{equation}
\min_{\phi,\theta} \mathbb{E}_{x\sim P_{X}} \mathbb{E}_{\Gamma_\phi(z \mid x)}[c(x, G_\theta(z|x))],
\end{equation}
where $\phi$ denotes the encoder parameters and $\theta$ denotes the decoder parameters.

\textbf{Optimization} As described in \color{red}\S \ref{sec:generative models and auto-encoders},\color{black} we provide a prior distribution $p(z)$ for the latent variables. In the optimization step, we use this prior distribution $p(z)$ to impose regularization on the inferred distribution $G_\theta(z|x)$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:primal optimization}
    \min_{\phi,\theta} \mathbb{E}_{x\sim P_{X}} \mathbb{E}_{\Gamma_\phi(z \mid x)}[c(x, G_\theta(z|x))] \qquad s.t. \quad Dist(G_\theta(z|x),p(z)) \leq \epsilon,
\end{equation}
where $Dist(Q_{\phi}(\mathbf{z} , \mathbf{x}) ,p(\mathbf{z}))$ represents a divergence between $Q_{\phi}$ and $p(\mathbf{z})$, such as KL-divergence or maximum mean discrepancy (MMD). Under the KKT condition, the dual of Equation (\ref{eq:primal optimization}) can be obtained and solved using gradient descent. We have
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}(\theta, \phi, \beta ; \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{z}) \leq \mathcal{L}(\theta, \phi ; \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{z}, \beta)=\mathbb{E}_{Q_{\phi}(\mathbf{z} \mid \mathbf{x})}\left[c(x, G_\theta(z|x))\right]+\beta Dist\left(\Gamma_{\phi}(\mathbf{z} , \mathbf{x}) , p(\mathbf{z})\right),
\end{equation}



Technically, $N$ data points are sampled independently according to an unknown underlying distribution $x\sim p_\theta(x)$, and construct dataset
$$
\mathcal{D}=\left\{\boldsymbol{x}^{(1)}, \boldsymbol{x}^{(2)}, \ldots, \boldsymbol{x}^{(N)}\right\} \equiv\left\{\boldsymbol{x}^{(i)}\right\}_{i=1}^{N} \equiv \boldsymbol{x}^{(1: N)}.
$$
Generative models learns the unconditional distribution $p_\theta(\boldsymbol{x})$ by
$
p_\theta(\boldsymbol{x})=\int p_\theta (\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{z}) d\boldsymbol{z},
$
where $\boldsymbol{z}$ is a vector representing the unobserved latent variables. Thus, for the latent variable $\boldsymbol{z}$ , we can learn $p(x|z)$ and compute the marginal distribution $p_\theta(\boldsymbol{x})=\int p_\theta (\boldsymbol{x}|\boldsymbol{z}) p_\theta(z) d\boldsymbol{z}$ instead of directly calculating the unconditional distribution $p_\theta (\boldsymbol{x})$.
The lower dimensionality and fewer restrictions on the distribution $p_\theta (\boldsymbol{z})$ \citep{doersch2016tutorial} make the latent variable $\mathcal{Z}$ a good candidate for the embedding space.
The learning process of generative models can be understood in terms of optimal transport \cite{bousquet2017optimal}.
