\section{Suboptimality Measurement}
In this section, we assess the performance of our framework by evaluating its suboptimality. Owing to the utilization of neural networks in both the embedding and reinforcement learning (RL) components, deriving a theoretical performance guarantee proves to be highly complex. Consequently, we adopt an empirical approach to estimate the RL performance by employing simulations and information relaxation techniques.
\subsection{Lower and Upper Bounds}  \label{sec: suboptimal meansurement}
%\cite{glasserman2004monte} \cite{lai2010approximate} \cite{el2020lookahead} \cite{brown2011dynamic} \cite{yang2021least}   \cite{salas2018benchmarking}

In this section, we develop both lower and upper bounds to our problem, and numerically test these bounds. These bounds can be used to help us get an basic idea about the best achievable in this problem and test the efficacy of our approach. 

Since our problem is a maximization problem, any feasible policy is a lower bound on the optimal value. We apply information relaxation and duality to construct an upper bound of our method. The method was first developed by \cite{brown2010information}, and a tutorial of this method is provided by \cite{brown2022information}.
To develop the upper bound, we use an information relaxation approach following  \cite{brown2022information} and \cite{el2020lookahead} that allows the policy to observe the realization of future prices. A straightforward alternative is to provide all future information to the policy when making decisions, referred to as \textit{perfect information relaxation}, but this relaxation often leads to loose bounds. In the remainder part of this section, we show how information relaxation can be used to develop a tighter upper bound while imposing a penalty on the reward function for knowing future information.

In developing the upper bound, we consider penalties that are \textit{dual feasible}, which do not penalize \textit{non-anticipativity} policies in expectation. For notation simplicity, if we rewrite the MDP transition as $\boldsymbol{s_{t+1}}=f(\boldsymbol{s_t},\boldsymbol{a_t},\boldsymbol{w_t})$, where the $\boldsymbol{w_t}$ is exogenous noise, the penalty is 
\begin{equation}\label{eq: penalties}
\varrho_{t}^{\pi}\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}, \boldsymbol{w_{t+1}} \mid \varphi\right):=\gamma^{t+1}\left(\varphi\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t+1}}, \pi\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t+1}}\right)\right)-\mathbf{E}\left[\varphi\left(f\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}, \boldsymbol{w}\right), \pi\left(f\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}, \boldsymbol{w}\right)\right)\right)\right]\right),
\end{equation}
where $\varphi$ is an approximation to the optimal value function. This penalty is dual feasible because 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \varrho_{t}^{\pi}\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}, \boldsymbol{w_{t+1}} \mid \varphi\right)\right]=0.
\end{equation}
In the following proposition, we follow \cite{el2020lookahead} to provide a tight upper bound using information relaxation and dual feasibility for the $Q$ function proposed in our problem, which is a variant of the of penalty introduced in \cite{brown2017information} extended to the case of Q functions. 

