By definition of the $Q$ function and the dual feasibility of $\varrho_{t}^{\pi_{\varphi}}$, we have
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
Q^{*}\left(\boldsymbol{s_{0}}, \boldsymbol{a_{0}}\right) &= \mathbf{E} \left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t r\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}\right)\right]\\
&=  \mathbf{E} \left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t r\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}\right) - \varrho_{t}^{\pi_{\varphi}}\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}, \boldsymbol{w_{t+1}} \mid \varphi\right) \right]\\
&\leq \mathbf{E}\left[\max_{ \boldsymbol{a} } \left\{\sum _ { t = 0 } ^ { \infty } \left(\gamma^{t} r\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}\right)-\varrho_{t}^{\pi_{\varphi}}\left(\boldsymbol{s_{t}}, \boldsymbol{a_{t}}, \boldsymbol{w_{t+1}} \mid \varphi\right)\right)\right\}\right]\\
& = Q^{U}\left(\boldsymbol{s_{0}}, \boldsymbol{a_{0}}\right)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\Halmos
\endproof

In contrast to the Bellman optimality equation, we note that the maximization is inside the expectation in \eqref{eq:upper_bound}. Therefore, the upper bound $Q^{U}\left(\boldsymbol{s_{0}}, \boldsymbol{a_{0}}\right)$ can be estimated by performing multiple Monte Carlo simulations in the outer loop and solving the inner loop optimization and averaging the results. 

\subsection{Numerical Results} \label{sec: suboptimal experiements}

We empirically estimate the upper bound of the DERL algorithm by modifying the LBQL upper bound in \cite{el2020lookahead} to fit the TD3 algorithm we introduced in \S \ref{sec: suboptimal meansurement}. We generate 500 sample paths to calculate Equation (\ref{eq: penalties}) and compute the information relaxation bound using Equation (\ref{eq:upper_bound}) with gradient descent. Since we use the discount rate $\gamma=\frac{1}{1+\delta_f}$ to compute the value function, $Q^U (\boldsymbol{z_s}(s_0),\boldsymbol{a_0})$ is the net present value, which has a financial interpretation and can be used to compute the annualized rate of return by
\begin{equation}
    \text{initial amount} \cdot (1+annualized)^T =Q(\boldsymbol{z_s}(\boldsymbol{s_0}),\boldsymbol{a_0}) \cdot (1+\delta_f)^T.
\end{equation}

The results of the numerical experiments on the upper bound are shown in Table \ref{tab: suboptimality}. Since the portfolio diversification task is the same for all algorithms, the result that they possess comparatively close upper bounds (IR Bounds) is quite reasonable. By comparing the bounds and gaps, we find tha our scalable RL trading algorithm is closer to the upper bound compared with raw TD3. 


