\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Backgrounds and Objectives}

Gait analysis (GA) is a widely utilized method for examining and understanding angular kinematics, which involves studying the rotational movements of joints during activities such as walking. This analysis provides valuable insights into joint function and coordination, aiding in the assessment of movement patterns and the identification of potential abnormalities %Its application holds significant potential for distinguishing between normal and abnormal gait behaviors, providing critical insights into biomechanics, movement efficiency, and functional impairments 
(see e.g., \citet{kay2000impact}, \citet{lofterod2007preoperative} and \citet{rao2008spectrum}). The study in this paper is driven by a growing scientific interest in the intersection of chronic risk communication and gait analysis. 

As individuals age, changes in joint flexibility, muscle strength, and neuromuscular control can alter these angular movements, leading to decreased mobility and increased fall risk.
An emerging concept, referred to as “effective age” has garnered increasing attention in recent years (see e.g., \citet{anderson2010osteoarthritis,loeser2010age}, \citet{li2013age}, and \citet{spiegelhalter2016old}). Effective age represents the age of a “healthy” individual who shares the same risk profile as a person being evaluated. In many cases, a person’s health status is more accurately reflected by their effective age rather than their chronological age, as it accounts for the impact of chronic disease risks and overall well-being. Evaluating effective age through gait analysis is a vital component in the proactive management of joint health, facilitating early detection of degeneration, customization of rehabilitation strategies, and the promotion of sustained mobility and independence.

Here we analyze a dataset generated from the experiment conducted by \citet{fukuchi2018public}. The study involves 42 healthy volunteers aged between 21 and 84, all of whom had no lower-extremity injuries in the six months prior to participation and no orthopedic or neurological conditions affecting their gait. Studying the association between chronological age and gait angular kinematics in healthy individuals is essential, as it serves as a baseline for assessing new subjects. %Studying the association between chronological age and angular kinematics of gait in healthy individuals is crucial, as chronological age closely aligns with effective age in healthy populations, providing a baseline for evaluating the joint health of new individuals. 
Data were collected using standard gait-analysis procedures in a $10\mbox{m}\times12\mbox{m}$ room equipped with 12 cameras, five force platforms embedded in the floor, and a dual-belt instrumented treadmill. This study focuses on treadmill walking data, as they offer greater consistency and are not influenced by terrain variations. The angular kinematics were recorded at various gait speeds, categorized into eight groups (S1 --- S8) ranging from the slowest to the fastest speeds. The ensemble averages of angular kinematics across different speed levels are shown in Figure \ref{ensemble}. We develop an innovative tool to study the association between joint angular kinematics and chronological age, as a building block for effective age evaluation. {\it To achieve this, we employ multiple functional regression, where age serves as the response variable and angular kinematics curves are the predictors.} %This approach enables the evaluation of effective age based on individual angular kinematics, offering valuable insights into joint health status and potential risks.

