%Therefore, incorporating joint coordination into the analysis of angular kinematics is crucial for accurately understanding movement dynamics and evaluating joint effective age.

\subsection{Functional Linear Regression and Covariate Grouping}
Multivariate functional data analysis (FDA) offers a powerful toolbox for handling multiple complex functional objects. Its primary strength lies in addressing the dual challenges of high dimensionality inherent in individual functional objects and the intricate interdependence among them. Recent advancements in this field have enabled researchers to tackle diverse challenges across various domains, including neuroimaging analysis (e.g., \citet{wang2019semiparametric} and \citet{jiao2024filtrated}), public health (e.g., \citet{zhou2013functional}), and gait analysis (e.g., \citet{cheng2020nonlinear}, \citet{dannenmaier2020application}, and \citet{cao2024functional}). These developments underscore the versatility and potential of multivariate FDA in addressing complex, high-dimensional problems across diverse domains. Among its various tools, functional linear regression (see \citet{ramsay2004functional} and \citet{morris2015functional} for overviews) stands out as a well-established class of linear models. In this article, we focus on the following model with a scalar response $y_n\in\mathbb{R}$ and multiple functional covariates $\{X_{jn}(t)\in L^2[0,1]\colon j=1,\ldots,p\}$, %which can be expressed as follows:
\begin{equation}
\label{mlr}
y_n=\beta_0+\sum_{j=1}^{p}\langle X_{jn},\beta_j\rangle+\epsilon_n,\\\ \mbox{E}\epsilon_n=0,\ \mbox{Var}(\epsilon_n)=\sigma^2,
\end{equation} 
where the coefficient functions $\{\beta_j(t)$, $j=1,\ldots,p\}\in (L^2[0,1])^p$, and $\{(X_{jn}(t),\epsilon_n)\colon j\ge1\}$ are independent across $n$. More research works on functional linear models include, but are not limited to, %\citet{faraway1997regression}, 
\citet{chiou2003functional,chiou2004functional,chiou2016multivariate}, \citet{muller2005generalized, muller2008functional}, \citet{yao2005functional},  \citet{ferraty2012regression},  \citet{jiao2021shape, jiao2023functional}.

In gait analysis, coordination among different joints is pivotal for understanding human movement patterns and their underlying biomechanics. Existing research underscores the interdependence of angular kinematics, where the motion of one joint often influences or compensates for the movement of others to maintain balance, stability, and forward progression (see e.g., \citet{ren2022lower} and \citet{dussault2023effect}). Thus, an important task is to account for the interdependence among different covariates (i.e., the angular kinematics of various joints). Such interdependence often leads to covariates being associated with the response variable (e.g., chronological age) in a homogeneous manner. A reasonable strategy, therefore, is to group covariates based on the similarity of their effects on the response. %Identifying such an unknown grouping structure is commonly referred to as ``grouping pursuit".
Notable works in this area include \citet{shen2010grouping}, \citet{ke2015homogeneity}, \citet{ma2017concave}, \citet{she2022supervised}, and \citet{jiao2024coefficient}. {\it However, ordinary grouping pursuit methods face a fundamental limitation: they rely on a uniform grouping structure.} For functional data, this restriction is inappropriate due to its infinite-dimensional nature. Specifically, different components of functional covariates may exhibit distinct grouping structures. 
Consequently, assuming a common structure can lead to model misspecification, reduced estimation efficiency, and hindered interpretability. Therefore, adopting flexible grouping strategies that accommodate variations in grouping structures across different dimensions is essential for accurate modeling and insightful interpretation.
%The approach proposed in this paper allows for varying grouping structures across different dimensions, enhancing both interpretability and modeling flexibility.

\subsection{Contributions and Novelties}
The primary methodological contribution of this paper is the development of a filtration grouping algorithm for model \eqref{mlr}, termed {\it filtrated grouping}, which allows for varying grouping structures across different dimensions. %is designed to analyze multi-resolution homogeneity among functional covariates across different dimensions. 
Here, we define “homogeneous components” as covariate components that share a common coefficient score in some dimension, reflecting their collective contribution to the response variable. 
The {\it key idea} is to iteratively filter out the homogeneous components of functional covariates that exhibit significant associations with the response variable, thereby constructing dimension-specific grouping structures. In the application, we employ the proposed methodology to examine the relationship between angular kinematics and chronological age for the healthy participants, with a particular focus on the coordination among different joints. The multi-resolutional coordination structure is discovered and highlights several important factors in the evaluation of effective age. The findings contribute to a deeper understanding of angular kinematics and offer a valuable tool for evaluating joint effective age, providing insights for enhancing age-related gait assessments and joint health evaluations. 

This idea is inspired by filtration, a widely used technique in graph analysis and topological data analysis (see, e.g., \citet{edelsbrunner2008persistent}, \citet{lee2011computing}, and \citet{hofer2020graph}), which iteratively constructs a sequence of nested structures and serves as a fundamental building block for multi-resolution analysis. However, it has been rarely applied in multivariate functional data analysis. To the best of our knowledge, the only related work is \citet{jiao2024filtrated}, which employs filtration to construct a multi-resolution structure for partial common functional principal components (PCfPC), offering a more flexible and efficient representation of PCfPCs. This paper is the first work to develop filtration techniques for multiple functional regression. %In principle, the first layer of filtration extracts the most homogeneous components that are significantly associated with the response, and as the filtration progresses, the extracted components become increasingly idiosyncratic. 
%The filtration process naturally generates a {\it forest-structured} grouping framework across different dimensions. Unlike ordinary grouping pursuit, filtrated grouping offers greater flexibility and precision in capturing both common and idiosyncratic components across different covariates.
%Methodologically, we develop a filtrated grouping methodology for multiple functional regression model to uncover the relationship between chronological age and angular kinematics while exploring the interdependence of angular kinematics of different joints. The proposed framework is designed to fulfill several key objectives: (a) constructing a parsimonious forest-structured model to reduce model complexity; (b) hierarchically extracting the connected components of functional covariates that are strongly associated with the response variable; and (c) devising an efficient algorithm to identify the forest model structure. This framework not only provides deeper insights into the biomechanical mechanisms underlying human movement and aging, offering potential applications in aging research and gait analysis, but also advances the methodological toolkit for analyzing multivariate functional data from a broader statistical perspective.
