The rest of the article is organized as follows. We present the filtrated grouping structure, and develop the model estimation procedure in Section~\ref{s2}. The identification of the filtrated grouping structure is discussed in Section~\ref{s3}. Simulation results are presented in Section~\ref{s4}. In Section \ref{s5}, we apply our methods to analyze the association between chronological age and angular kinematics data. The paper is concluded in Section~\ref{s6}. 

\section{Filtrated multiple functional regression}
\label{s2}
\subsection{Preliminaries}
Notationally, let $X\in L^q_H=L_H^q(\Omega,\mathcal{A},\mathcal{P})$ indicate that, for some $q>0$, a $H$-valued function $X$ satisfies $E\|X(t)\|^q<\infty$. In what follows, we assume that there are one scalar response $y_n$ and $p$ functional covariates $\{X_{jn}(t)\in L^2_H\colon j=1,\ldots,p\}$, where $H=L^2[0,1]$ is equipped with the inner product $\langle x,y \rangle=\int_0^1x(t)y(t)dt$ and the norm $\|x\|^2=\int_0^1x^2(t)dt$. It is assumed that $\{y_n,X_{jn}(t)\colon j=1,\ldots,p\}$ are independent across $n=1,\ldots,N$. By Mercer's theorem, we have the following functional principal component decompositions 
$\mathcal{C}_j(\cdot)=\sum_{d\ge1}\theta_{jd}\langle\nu_{jd},\cdot\rangle\nu_{jd}(t),$ where $\mathcal{C}_j(x)(t)=\int C_j(t,s)x(s)ds$ for $x\in L^2[0,1]$, $\mu_j(t)=\mbox{E}X_{jn}(t)$, and $C_j(t,s)=\mbox{E}\{(X_{jn}-\mu_j)(t)(X_{jn}-\mu_j)(s)\}$. We assume that all the coefficient functions in \eqref{mlr} belong to the space spanned by $\{\nu_{jd}(t)\colon d\ge1,\ j\ge1\}$. 

%$$\Theta_\beta=\left\{\beta(t)\in L^2[0,1]\colon \beta(t)=\sum_{d\ge1}b_{jd}\nu_{jd}(t),\ j\ge1\right\}.$$
%The ordinary multiple functional linear regression model is given by \eqref{mlr}. %below 
%\begin{equation}
%\label{ordinary_model}
%y_n=\beta_0+\sum_{j=1}^p\langle X_{jn},\beta_j\rangle+\epsilon_n,\\\ \mbox{E}\epsilon_n=0,\ \mbox{Var}(\epsilon_n)=\sigma^2. 
%\end{equation}  
%One method to address the homogeneity of covariates is covariate grouping based on coefficient homogeneity. Specifically, 
%\begin{remark}
%From the computational perspective, in the ordinary model \eqref{mlr}, the potential homogeneity of functional coefficients is not accounted for and the estimation burden is high even for moderate $p$ (see e.g., \citet{jiao2024coefficient}). Although the grouped model \eqref{grouped_model} alleviates the estimation burden and improve model interpretability, it fails to capture variations in grouping structures across different dimensions. %These limitations motivate the development of a more flexible modeling framework that can accommodate dimension-specific grouping variability.
%\end{remark}
Suppose that, for some sets of basis functions $\{\nu_{jd}(t)\in H\colon d\ge1\}$ and $\{u_{jd}(t)\in H\colon d\ge1\}$, the functional covariates and functional coefficients admit the following basis representation
$$X_{jn}(t)=\sum_{d\ge1}\xi_{jn,d}\nu_{jd}(t),\ \beta_j(t)=\sum_{d,d'\ge1}b_{jd}M_{j,dd'}u_{jd'}(t),$$ 
in which the basis functions $\{\nu_{jd}(t)\colon d\ge1\}$ and $\{u_{jd}(t)\colon d\ge1\}$ can either be orthonormal or not. The term $M_j=\{M_{j,dd'}\colon d,d'\ge1\}$ represents an infinite matrix satisfying the condition $\sum_{d'\ge1}M_{j,d_1d'}(\int\nu_{jd_2}u_{jd'})=\bm{1}_{\{d_1=d_2\}}$. %Using such general basis representations, 
Then model \eqref{mlr} can be equivalently rewritten in the following form 
\begin{equation}
\label{basis-form}
y_n=\beta_0+\sum_{d=1}^\infty\sum_{j=1}^p \xi_{jn,d}b_{jd}+\epsilon_n,\\\ \mbox{E}\epsilon_n=0,\ \mbox{Var}(\epsilon_n)=\sigma^2. 
\end{equation} 
For illustrating the filtrated grouping structure, we focus on the equivalent form \eqref{basis-form}, as the representation is dimension-specific (layer-specific), enabling a more precise characterization of grouping structures across different dimensions. 

\subsection{Filtrated Grouping Structure}
Given a grouping structure $G=(\mathcal{K}_1,\ldots,\mathcal{K}_m)$ satisfying  $\mathcal{K}_i\cap \mathcal{K}_{i'}=\emptyset$ for $i\ne i'$ and $\cup_{i=1}^m\mathcal{K}_i=\{1,\ldots,p\}$, the ordinary grouped model based on coefficient homogeneity is %given by
\begin{equation}
\label{grouped_model}
y_n=\beta_0+\sum_{i=1}^m\langle Z_{in},\alpha_i\rangle+\epsilon_n,\\\ \mbox{E}\epsilon_n=0,\ \mbox{Var}(\epsilon_n)=\sigma^2, 
\end{equation} 
where $Z_{in}(t)=\sum_{j\in \mathcal{K}_i}X_{jn}(t)$ and $\alpha_i(t)$ is the common coefficient function of group $\mathcal{K}_i$. 

The ordinary model \eqref{mlr} cannot account for the homogeneity of functional coefficients and the estimation burden is high even for moderate $p$ (see e.g., \citet{jiao2024coefficient}). Although the grouped model \eqref{grouped_model} alleviates the estimation burden and improve model interpretability, it fails to capture variations in grouping structures across different dimensions. These limitations motivate the development of a more flexible modeling framework that can accommodate dimension-specific grouping variability.

Building upon the form \eqref{basis-form}, we develop the following filtrated grouping structure%While it resembles \eqref{basis-form} in form, it introduces a fundamentally different modeling perspective rather than serving as a straightforward extension.
\begin{equation}
\label{filt-group}
y_n=\beta_0+\sum_{d=1}^\infty\sum_{i=1}^{m_d}\left\{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{K}_{d,i}}\xi_{jn,d}\right\}a_{d,i}+\epsilon_n,\  \mbox{E}\epsilon_n=0,\ \mbox{Var}(\epsilon_n)=\sigma^2,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{K}_{d,i}$ denotes the $i$-th group in the $d$-th dimension/layer and $m_d$ is the number of groups in the $d$-th dimension. We define the {\it homogeneous components} as follows
$$\zeta_{nd}^{(i)}\overset{\vartriangle}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{K}_{d,i}}\xi_{jn,d},\ i \geq 1,\ d \geq 1,$$
and aim to estimate the associated coefficient scores $\{a_{d,i} \colon i \geq 1,\ d \geq 1\}$. 

The coefficient homogeneity is captured in a multi-resolution manner, resulting in group indices $\{\mathcal{K}_{d,i}\colon i\ge1,d\ge1\}$ that follow a forest structure (see Figure \ref{tree}). Compared to the ordinary grouped model \eqref{grouped_model}, the filtrated grouped model \eqref{filt-group} accommodates varying grouping structures across different dimensions.
%We now elucidate the rationale behind the proposed filtrated grouping structure. 
In the first layer, we identify the most homogeneous covariate components and group them accordingly to obtain $\{\zeta_{n1}^{(i)}\colon i\ge1\}$ and estimate $\{a_{1,i}\colon i\ge1\}$. After filtering out these homogeneous components from each covariate, we divide $\{\mathcal{K}_{1,i}\colon i\ge1\}$ into $\{\mathcal{K}_{2,i}\colon i\ge1\}$, which are either nested within or identical to the groups in the previous layer (i.e., for any $i\ge1$, $\mathcal{K}_{2,i}\subseteq \mathcal{K}_{1,i'}$ for some $i'\ge1$), and then obtain the homogeneous components in the new layer (i.e., $\{\zeta_{n2}^{(i)}\colon i\ge1\}$) and estimate the associated coefficient scores (i.e., $\{a_{2,i}\colon i\ge1\}$). %This iterative procedure continues until the ending criterion (discussed in Section \ref{s3.3}) is met. 
The splitting procedure continues until a predefined stopping criterion is met. In the deeper layers of the filtration, higher-resolution homogeneous components, constructed from fewer covariates, are progressively extracted.


\begin{figure}[ht]
