In this setting, the homogeneity among the 10 coefficient functions cannot be captured by a fixed grouping structure, as the homogeneity across different dimensions is characterized by varying grouping structures. The homogeneity is represented by the forest structure shown in Figure \ref{tree-simulation}. However, this structure may not be optimal. Small differences between $\beta_4(t)$ and $\beta_5(t)$, $\beta_7(t)$ and $\beta_8(t)$, as well as $\beta_9(t)$ and $\beta_{10}(t)$, could cause these pairs to be merged across more layers in the identified forest structure. %In the following, we term this structure the oracle structure.


