% Time-series imputation models have traditionally been developed using complete datasets with artificial masking patterns to simulate missing values. However, in real-world community-scale applications, practitioners often face scenarios where only a small portion of clean data is available and are composed of complex missing patterns. In our work, we explore the challenge of significant missing data across various utilities datasets from different water and electricity providers. Similar to \cite{liu2021adaptive}, we find that there is mismatch between the distributions of the clean data (used for training) and the many sequences of missing data (hold out set). This fundamental mismatch between training assumptions and practical constraints has been shown to limit the effectiveness of existing imputation approaches. However, in our setting where there are many unique sources of data generation, this assumption becomes more complex as clean data may be limited to some of the distributional patterns and datasets are given (collected) all different cities - not one data - several patterns that we don't know aprori know - no single target. Use it to this pattern, but the setting is different specific target / client extract from target to source (heterogeneous data 

Time series imputation models have traditionally been developed using complete datasets with artificial masking patterns to simulate missing values. However, in real-world infrastructure monitoring, practitioners often encounter datasets where large amounts of data are missing and follow complex, heterogeneous patterns. We introduce DIM-SUM, a preprocessing framework for training robust imputation models that bridges the gap between artificially masked training data and real missing patterns. DIM-SUM combines pattern clustering and adaptive masking strategies with theoretical learning guarantees to handle diverse missing patterns actually observed in the data. Through extensive experiments on over 2 billion readings from California water districts, electricity datasets, and benchmarks, we demonstrate that DIM-SUM outperforms traditional methods by reaching similar accuracy with lower processing time and significantly less training data. When compared against a large pre-trained model, DIM-SUM averages 2x higher accuracy with significantly less inference time.

