Initially, Brouwer believed that any orientation preserving homeomorphism of $\mathbb{R}^2$ without fixed points was always conjugated to the translation, \cite{bro9}. However, he quickly corrected this assumption, \cite{bro13}, \cite{bro14}, \cite{bro16}, and provided the example above, demonstrating that this is not the case.

By the construction of the map $f$, for every $x \in S^{2}-\{x_{0}\}$, the sequences $\{f^{n}(x)\}_{n \in \N}$ and $\{f^{-n}(x)\}_{n \in \N}$ converge to $\infty$. On the other hand, consider the square $[0,1]^{2} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{2}$. For every $n \in \Z$, $f^{n}((0,0)) = (-n,0)$, $f^{n}((0,1)) = (n,1)$ and $f^{n}([0,1]^{2}) \subseteq \mathbb{R} \times [0,1]$. Since the set $f^{n}([0,1]^{2})$ is connected, we get that $(\mathbb{R} \times [0,1])- (\{0\}\times [0,1])$ has two connected components and, for $n \neq 0$, the points $f^{n}((0,0))$ and $f^{n}((0,1))$ are in different connected components of $(\mathbb{R} \times [0,1])- (\{0\}\times [0,1])$, then, for $n \neq 0$, $f^{n}([0,1]^{2})\cap(\{0\}\times [0,1])$, which implies that $\{f^{n}([0,1]^{2})\}_{n > 0} = \{\overline{f}^{n}([0,1]^{2})\}_{n > 0}$ do not converge uniformly to $\infty$.  So $\overline{f}$ does not have parabolic dynamics. See \textbf{Figure \ref{fig:intersectionofcompactsonbrouwerexample}} for a picture of $f([0,1]^{2})$ intersecting $[0,1]^{2}$.

