\bb. \textbf{Relative bias, empirical standard deviation, and coverage rate.}  
Table~\ref{tab_no_hiercachic__ext} summarizes the performance of the different approaches.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Under MAR and \( \text{MNAR}_1 \), the relative bias is particularly large, especially for coefficients associated with intermediate categories of \( X_1 \), and the coverage rates fall below the nominal level.
    \item The complete-case (CC) analysis yields reduced bias compared to MAR and \( \text{MNAR}_1 \), but its estimates are more variable and coverage is generally unsatisfactory.
    \item In contrast, \( \text{MNAR}_2 \) and \( \text{MNAR}_3 \) produce estimates that are less biased than MAR and more precise than CC, with better coverage.
\end{itemize}

These findings illustrate that well-calibrated MNAR scenarios—guided by appropriate sensitivity vectors—can outperform both MAR-based imputation and complete-case analysis.



